hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3601","3841 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hunayni narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sad, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, he said: What is ramal now, and uncovering the shoulders? And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has removed shirk and its people; upon that, we do not abandon anything we did with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3602","3842 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata, from Yala ibn Umayya, he said: When Umar performed Hajj, he performed ramal three times. And this was in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with none of them denying it against him."
"3603","3843 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Mansur ibn al-Mutamir, from Shaqiq, from Masruq, he said: I arrived at Mecca performing Umra, and I followed Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him; then he entered the mosque, and performed ramal three times and walked four."
"3604","3844 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, when he arrived at Mecca, circumambulated the House and performed ramal, then ran between al-Safa and al-Marwa; and when he entered ihram at Mecca he did not perform ramal at the House, and he delayed the running between al-Safa and al-Marwa until the Day of Sacrifice, and he did not perform ramal on the Day of Sacrifice. So in this from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, is that he used to perform ramal in Hajj when his ihram for it was from other than Mecca. So this contradicts what Mujahid narrated from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So what Mujahid narrated from him is either abrogated, and what abrogated it is more worthy than it, or it is not authentic from him, so it is more proper not to act upon it, and to act upon its opposite. And when what we mentioned of ramal is established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the absence of the polytheists, and from his Companions after him in the first three circuits, it is established that that is of the Sunnah of tawaf upon arrival, and that no man able to do it should abandon it. And this is the statement of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy upon them."
"3605","Chapter: What of the corners is touched in tawaf. 3845 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: We used to touch all the corners."
"3606","3846 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So some people held that one who circumambulates the House should touch all its corners, and they argued in that with this hadith. Others opposed them in that and said: One should touch in tawaf only the two Yamani corners. And they argued in that with what."
"3607","3847 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dawud, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not pass by these two corners, the Black and the Yamani, except he touched them in tawaf, and he did not touch these other two."
"3608","3848 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3609","3849 - Yazid and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us. (H). 3850 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, he said: I did not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping of the House except the two Yamani corners."
"3610","3851 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would touch of the corners of the House only the Black Corner, and the one next to it toward the house of the Jumahis."
"3611","3852 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from al-Layth, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3612","3853 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Ubayd ibn Jurayj, that he said to Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: I saw you touching of the corners only the Yamanis. He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) touching of the corners only the Yamanis."
"3613","3854 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Abbad narrated to us, he said: Attab ibn Bashir al-Jazari narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan circumambulated the Sacred House, and began touching all the corners. So Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: Why do you touch these two corners, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not touch them? So Muawiya said: Nothing of the House is abandoned. So Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: (There has been for you in the Messenger of Allah a good example). He said: You have spoken the truth. So all these reports inform from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would touch in his tawaf only the two Yamani corners. And with these reports is of successive transmission what is not with the first report. And among the proof in our view, and Allah knows best, for one who holds to these reports also, against one who holds to what contradicts them, is that the two Yamani corners are built upon the extremity of the House on their side, while the other two are not so, for al-Hijr is behind them, and it is of the House. And they have unanimously agreed that what is between the two Yamani corners is not touched, for it is not a corner of the House. So it follows in reasoning that likewise the other two corners are not touched, for they are not two corners of the House. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning al-Hijr, that it is of the House, what."
"3614","3855 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Shayban ibn Abd al-Rahman, Abu Muawiya, narrated to us, from al-Ashath ibn Abi al-Shatha, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about al-Hijr, and he said: It is of the House. I said: What prevented them from including it in it? He said: Funds fell short for them."
"3615","3856 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-Ashath, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, he said: Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about al-Hijr, is it of the House? He said: Yes. I said: Why did they not include it in the House? He said: Your people lacked funds. I said: What is the matter with its door elevated? He said: Your people did it to admit whom they wish and prevent whom they wish; and were it not that your people are recent in Jahiliyya, and I fear their hearts would deny that, I would have considered including al-Hijr in the House, and sticking its door to the ground."
"3616","3857 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulaym ibn Hayyan narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mina narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, he said: Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Were it not that your people are recent in Jahiliyya, I would have demolished the Kaba and stuck it to the ground, and made for it two doors, an eastern door and a western door, and added six cubits of al-Hijr to the House; for Quraysh shortened it when they built the House."
"3617","3858 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Abi Saghira narrated to us, from Abu Qazaa, that Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, while he was circumambulating the House, a speaker said: Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr lies about the Mother of the Believers, saying: I heard her saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Aisha, were it not the recency of your people in disbelief, I would have dismantled the House until I add to it of al-Hijr. So al-Harith ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Rabia said: Do not say that, O Commander of the Believers, for I heard the Mother of the Believers saying it. He said: I wish I had heard this from you before I demolished it, so I would have left it. Since it is established that al-Hijr is of the House, and that the two corners next to it are not two corners of the House, it is established that they are like what is between the two Yamani corners. So as what is between the two Yamani corners is not touched, so likewise these two also, in reasoning, are not touched. And Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, argued with what we argued with of this for the abandonment by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of touching those two corners."
"3618","3859 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abdullah, that Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you not seen that your people, when they built the Kaba, fell short of the foundations of Ibrahim, upon him be peace. She said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, will you not return it upon the foundations of Ibrahim? He said: Were it not the recency of your people in disbelief. He said: So Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: If Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, heard that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I do not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) abandoning touching the two corners next to al-Hijr except that the House was not completed upon the foundations of Ibrahim, upon him be peace. So by these reports what we mentioned is established, and that one should touch of the corners of the House only the two Yamani corners. And this is the statement of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy upon them."
"3619","Chapter: Prayer for tawaf after dawn and after Asr. 3860 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed me, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Ibn Babah, from Jubayr ibn Mutim, raised to the Prophet, that he said: O sons of Abd al-Muttalib, do not prevent anyone who circumambulates this House and prays at whatever hour he wishes of night or day."
"3620","3861 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Yazid ibn Mardhanuba, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O sons of Abd Manaf, if you are given charge of this matter, do not prevent anyone who circumambulates this House and prays at whatever hour he wishes of night or day. Abu Jafar said: So some people held to the permissibility of prayer for tawaf in night and day, with no time of the times forbidden for prayer preventing that in their view, and they argued in that with these reports. Others opposed them in that and said: There is no proof for you in these reports; for what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted therein, and commanded the sons of Abd al-Muttalib, or the sons of Abd Manaf, not to prevent anyone from of tawaf and prayer, is tawaf in the manner it should be performed, and prayer in the manner it should be prayed; as for upon other than that, then no. Do you not see that had a man circumambulated the House naked, or without ablution, or in major impurity, they must prevent him from that, for he circumambulated upon other than what tawaf should be upon. And that is not included in what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them not to prevent of tawaf. So likewise his statement: Do not prevent anyone who prays, is upon what he has been commanded to pray upon of purity, covering the private parts, and facing the qibla, in the times in which prayer has been permitted; as for other than that, then no. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade with a general prohibition prayer at sunrise, at sunset, at midday, after dawn until the sun rises, and after Asr until the sun sets, and the reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) have come successively with that, and I have mentioned that with its chains in other than this place of this book. So among what the people of the first view argued for their statement in that is what."
"3621","3862 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abdullah ibn Babah, he said: Abu al-Darda circumambulated after Asr and prayed before the sun reddened. I said: You, the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), say: There is no prayer after Asr until the sun sets. So he said: This land is not like the rest of lands. They said: The statement of Abu al-Darda proves that the prayer for tawaf was not included in a prohibition from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of prayer in the times you mentioned. It was said to them: You do not hold by this hadith; for we have seen you dislike prayer in Mecca in the times forbidden for prayer therein for other than tawaf, because of the prohibition of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of prayer in those times, and you do not exclude the ruling of Mecca in that from the ruling of the rest of lands. And Abu al-Darda in the hadith you argued with excluded the ruling of Mecca from the ruling of other lands besides it in preventing prayers in that, and informed that the prohibition did not include its ruling, and that by it was meant only other than it, although Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, opposed Abu al-Darda in that."
"3622","3863 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, circumambulated the House after dawn and did not bow, then when he reached Dhu Tuwa and the sun rose, he prayed two rakas."
"3623","3864 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, the like of it. So this Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, did not bow then, for it was not in his view a time of prayer, and he delayed that until the time of prayer entered upon him, then he prayed. And this was in the presence of the rest of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with none of them denying it against him. And had that time been in his view a time of prayer for tawaf, he would have prayed and would not have delayed that, for no one who circumambulates the House should fail to pray then except from an excuse. And there has been narrated from Muadh ibn Afra the like of that, and I have mentioned that in what preceded in this book. And there has also been narrated the like of that from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"3624","3865 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, arrived at Mecca at the dawn prayer, and circumambulated and did not pray except after the sun rose. And reasoning also proves that, for we have seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting the day of Fitr and the day of Sacrifice, and all have unanimously agreed that that is the same in all lands. So reasoning upon that is that what he forbade of prayers in the times in which he forbade prayers therein is in all lands alike. So the statement of one who holds to the permissibility of prayer for tawaf in the times forbidden for prayer therein is thereby nullified. Then those who opposed the people of the first view in that split into two groups. One group among them said: One does not pray in any of these five times for tawaf, as one does not pray therein for voluntary prayer. And among those who said that are Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy upon them. And what we narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, Muadh ibn Afra, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, agrees with them. And one group said: One prays for tawaf after Asr before the yellowing of the sun, and after dawn before the rising of the sun, but one does not pray for that in the remaining three times forbidden for prayer therein. And among those who said that are Mujahid, Ibrahim al-Nakhai, and Ata."
"3625","3866 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: Circumambulate and pray so long as you are in a time; then when the time departs, refrain."
"3626","3867 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Ghaniyya narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Ata, the like of it."
"3627","3868 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja and Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Aswad, from Mujahid, he said: (Perform tawaf). Ubayd Allah said: (After Fajr and after Asr, and pray as long as you are within a time). And Ibn Raja said: During a prayer time. And the like of that has also been narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"3628","3869 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Ghaniyya narrated to us, from Umar ibn Dharr, from Mujahid, he said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to perform tawaf after Asr and pray as long as the sun was white and alive; then when it yellowed and changed he would perform a single tawaf until he prayed Maghrib, then he would pray and perform tawaf after Fajr, and pray as long as it was dark; then when it became bright he would perform a single tawaf, then sit until the sun rose and bowing (in voluntary prayer) became possible."
"3629","3870 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Uqba informed me, from Salim and Ata, that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to perform one circuit (usbu') of tawaf after Fajr and after Asr and pray two rak'as, as long as it was within a prayer time. So here is Ata who stated as his own opinion what we have mentioned. And it has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Do not prevent anyone from circumambulating this House and praying at any hour he wishes, by night or day. That has been taken contrary to what the people of the first view held. And reflection on that - when they differed with this disagreement - is that we saw that sunrise, sunset and midday prevent making up missed prayers, and the Sunna came to that effect from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his delaying making up the Fajr prayer he slept through until the sun rose high and white. So if what we mentioned forbids making up missed obligatory prayers, it more strongly forbids the prayers for tawaf. And Uqba ibn Amir said: Three hours in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid us to pray and to bury our dead: when the sun rises shining until it ascends, when the noon stander stands until it declines, and when the sun inclines toward setting until it sets. We have mentioned that with its chain earlier in this book of ours. So if these times forbid funeral prayer, the prayer for tawaf is likewise. Likewise the prayer after Asr before the sun changes, and after Fajr before sunrise, was permissible for funerals, permissible for making up a missed prayer, and disliked for voluntary prayer; and tawaf necessitated prayer such that its obligation became like the obligation of prayer over funerals. So reflection upon what we mentioned is that its ruling after it becomes obligatory is like the ruling of obligatory prayers that have become due and like the ruling of funeral prayer that has become due. So the prayer for tawaf is prayed in every time in which funeral prayer is prayed and missed prayer is made up, and is not prayed in any time in which funeral prayer is not prayed nor missed prayer made up. This is the reflection in our view in this chapter, in accordance with what Ata, Ibrahim and Mujahid said, and with what has been narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both; to it we go, and it is the view of Sufyan. And it is contrary to the view of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"3630","Chapter: One who enters ihram for Hajj then performs tawaf for it before standing at Arafa. 3871 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ata informed me that Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to say: (No one circumambulates the House, whether a pilgrim or otherwise, except that he exits ihram by it). I said to him: From where did Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, take that? He said: From the statement of Allah Almighty: {Then their place of sacrifice is at the Ancient House}. I said to him: (But that is only after standing at Arafa). He said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, considered it before the standing at Arafa and after it. He said: And Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, took it from the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command to his Companions to exit ihram during the Farewell Hajj; he said it more than once."
"3631","3872 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, that Urwa said to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: You have led the people astray, O Ibn Abbas. He said: And what is that, O Urayya? He said: You give fatwa to the people that when they circumambulate the House they have exited ihram, while Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, would come reciting talbiya for Hajj and remain in ihram until the Day of Sacrifice. Ibn Abbas said: Is it by this that you went astray? I narrate to you from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and you narrate to me from Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both? Urwa said: (Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, knew the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) better than you)."
"3632","3873 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, he said: Qatada informed me, he said: I heard Abu Hassan al-Raqashi, that a man said to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: O Ibn Abbas, what is this fatwa that has spread from you? That whoever circumambulates the House has exited ihram? He said: It is the Sunna of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), even if you dislike it."
"3633","3874 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shababa ibn Sawwar narrated to us. (H). 3875 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us. (H). 3876 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, they said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, he said: I heard Tariq ibn Shihab narrating from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was making his camel kneel at al-Batha, and he said to me: With what did you enter ihram? He said: I said: I entered ihram like the ihram of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have done well; circumambulate the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwa, then exit ihram. So I did. Then I came to a woman of Qays who deloused my head. I used to give people fatwa to that effect, until the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when a man said: O Abd Allah ibn Qays, go slow with some of your fatwa, for you do not know what the Commander of the Believers has introduced concerning the rites after you. So I said: O people, whoever we gave a fatwa, let him wait, for the Commander of the Believers is coming, so follow him. When Umar came I went to him and mentioned that to him. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me: If we take by the Book of Allah, the Book of Allah commands us to complete; and if we take by the Sunna of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not exit ihram until the sacrificial animal reached its place."
"3634","3877 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il al-Madini narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, he said: We entered upon Jabir ibn Abd Allah and asked him about the Hajj of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained nine years without performing Hajj, then it was announced among the people in the tenth year: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) will perform Hajj. Many people came to Medina seeking to follow the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). We went out until when we came to Dhu al-Hulayfa, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the mosque, then rode al-Qaswa until when she stood straight with him upon al-Bayda, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was among us and the Qur'an was being revealed to him and he knew its interpretation; whatever he did of anything, we did it. He entered ihram with tawhid, and the people entered ihram with what they enter ihram with, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not reject anything of it from them, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept to his talbiya. Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: We intended nothing but Hajj; we did not know Umra, until when we were at the last circuit upon al-Marwa he said: Had I known beforehand what I know now, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal, and I would have made it an Umra; so whoever does not have a sacrificial animal with him, let him exit ihram and make it an Umra. So the people exited ihram and shortened, except the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and whoever had a sacrificial animal with him. Then Suraqa ibn Malik ibn Ju'shum stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, is this Umra of ours for this year of ours or forever? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) interlaced his fingers into one another and said: Umra has entered - like this - into Hajj. Twice. So all the people exited ihram and shortened, except the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and whoever had a sacrificial animal with him. Abu Ja'far said: And this statement of Suraqa to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answer to him, may mean: did he mean by it our Umra in the months of Hajj forever or for this year; for they had not known Umra in the months of Hajj before, and used to count that among the gravest wickedness. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered him and said: It is forever."
"3635","3878 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ibn al-Had, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Except that he did not mention the question of Suraqa nor the answer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him."
"3636","3879 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qays ibn Sa'd, from Ata, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived at Mecca when four days of Dhu al-Hijja had passed. When they circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwa, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Make it an Umra. When it was the Day of Tarwiya they recited talbiya; then when it was the Day of Sacrifice they came and circumambulated the House, and did not circumambulate between al-Safa and al-Marwa."
"3637","3880 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We arrived with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Mecca on the morning of the fourth. He commanded us to exit ihram. We said: What kind of exiting, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Complete exiting. Had I known beforehand what I know now, I would have done the like of what you do."
"3638","3881 - Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn A'yan narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When we arrived with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Mecca in the Farewell Hajj, he asked the people: With what did you enter ihram? Some said: We entered ihram for Hajj. Others said: We arrived as mutamatti'un. Others said: We entered ihram with your ihram, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Whoever arrived and did not drive a sacrificial animal, let him exit ihram; for had I known beforehand what I know now, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal, so as to be in a state of lawfulness. Suraqa ibn Malik ibn Ju'shum said: O Messenger of Allah, is this Umra of ours for our year or forever? He said: Rather, forever and ever."
"3639","3882 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to me, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram, and we entered ihram with him for Hajj purely, until when we arrived at Mecca on the fourth of Dhu al-Hijja, we circumambulated the House and al-Safa and al-Marwa; then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded whoever had not driven a sacrificial animal to exit ihram. He said: And he did not insist regarding the matter of women. Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: So we said: He left us until when only five nights remained between us and Arafa, he commands us to exit ihram, so that we come to Arafat while madhiy drips from our private parts, while he himself did not exit ihram, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had driven the sacrificial animal. Our saying reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he stood and addressed the people, praised Allah and extolled Him, then mentioned what had reached him of their saying, and said: You know that I am the most truthful of you, the most God-fearing toward Allah and the most dutiful; had I not driven the sacrificial animal I would have exited ihram, and had I known beforehand what I know now, I would not have brought a sacrificial animal. Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: So we heard and obeyed and exited ihram."
"3640","3883 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Makki narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir narrating about the Hajj of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: (He commanded us after we circumambulated to exit ihram, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you intend to go to Mina, enter ihram; so we entered ihram from al-Batha)."
"3641","3884 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Ata that he heard him narrating from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: We entered ihram with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Dhu al-Hulayfa for Hajj purely, not mixing it with Umra. We arrived at Mecca when four nights of Dhu al-Hijja had passed; when we circumambulated the House and ran between al-Safa and al-Marwa, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to make it an Umra and to go to women. We said: Only five nights remain between us and Arafa, so we will go out to it while the private part of one of us drips semen. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am indeed the most dutiful of you and the most truthful; were it not for the sacrificial animal I would have exited ihram. Then Suraqa ibn Malik ibn Ju'shum stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, is this tamattu' of ours for this year of ours or forever? The Messenger of Allah said: Rather, forever and ever. So the question of Suraqa to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in this hadith was only about tamattu', meaning: our Hajj into which we first entered has become an Umra, then after our exiting from it we entered ihram for Hajj, so we have become mutamatti'un; so is this tamattu' of ours specifically for this year of ours, so we do not do that afterwards, or forever, so we perform tamattu' with Umra to Hajj as we did in this year of ours? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather, forever. And that is not to mean that they thereafter may exit from a Hajj before Arafa, due to their circumambulation of the House and their running between al-Safa and al-Marwa. We shall mention from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after this in this book what indicates that that exiting ihram of theirs before Arafa was specific to them, not for those after them, and we shall place it in its place, Allah willing."
"3642","3885 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Bakr ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions arrived at Mecca reciting talbiya for Hajj. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes may make it an Umra, except whoever has a sacrificial animal with him."
"3643","3886 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We went out seeing nothing but Hajj. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived at Mecca he circumambulated and did not exit ihram, and he had a sacrificial animal with him. Those with him of his women and his Companions circumambulated; those of them who did not have a sacrificial animal exited ihram."
"3644","3887 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Dawud narrated to us, from Abu Nadra, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: We went out from Medina crying out Hajj aloud. When we arrived we circumambulated. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Make it an Umra, except whoever has a sacrificial animal with him. When it was the eve of Arafa, we entered ihram for Hajj."
"3645","3888 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions arrived reciting talbiya for Hajj, and al-Zubayr had a sacrificial animal with him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Whoever does not have a sacrificial animal with him, let him exit ihram. She said: I did not have a sacrificial animal with me that year, so I exited ihram."
"3646","3889 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Qilaba, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr at Medina as four, and prayed Asr at Dhu al-Hulayfa as two, and spent the night there until morning. When he prayed Fajr he rode his mount; when it rose with him he glorified Allah and magnified Him, until when it stood straight with him upon al-Bayda he combined both. When we arrived at Mecca the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them to exit ihram; then when it was the Day of Tarwiya they entered ihram for Hajj."
"3647","3890 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Abu Malih, from Ma'qil ibn Yasar, he said: We performed Hajj with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we found Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, removing her garments. He said to her: What is with you? She said: I was told that you have exited ihram and have exited your family from ihram. He said: Whoever does not have a sacrificial animal has exited ihram; as for us, we have not exited ihram, for we have a sacrificial animal with us until we reach Arafat. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to these reports and followed them, and said: Whoever circumambulates the House before his standing at Arafa, and had not driven a sacrificial animal, has exited ihram. Others disagreed with them and said: No one who entered Hajj may leave it except by completing it, and nothing before the Day of Sacrifice exits him from it, neither tawaf nor anything else. They said: As for what you mentioned of the statement of Allah Almighty: {Then their place of sacrifice is at the Ancient House}, this is concerning sacrificial camels, not concerning the pilgrim; and the meaning of the Ancient House here is the entire Haram, as He said in the other verse: {Until the sacrificial animal reaches its place}; so the Haram is the place of the sacrificial animal, for it is slaughtered in it. As for the children of Adam, their exiting in their Hajj is only on the Day of Sacrifice. As for what they adduced of the reports we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his commanding his Companions to exit from their Hajj by their tawaf which they performed before Arafa, that - in our view - was specific to them in that Hajj of theirs, apart from the rest of people after them. The proof of that is what follows."
"3648","3891 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur and Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad, from Rabi'a ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Bilal ibn al-Harith, from his father, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, tell me about this annulment (faskh) of our Hajj: is it for us specifically or for people generally? He said: Rather, for you specifically."
"3649","3892 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: I heard Rabi'a ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman narrating, from al-Harith ibn Bilal ibn al-Harith al-Muzani, from his father, the like of it."
"3650","3893 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus informed me, from Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari, from al-Muraqqa' ibn Sayfi, from Abu Dharr, he said: The annulment of Hajj was only for the company who were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3651","3894 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Muraqqa' al-Usaydi, from Abu Dharr al-Ghifari, that he said: What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us when we entered Mecca - that we make it an Umra and exit from everything - was a license from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) specifically for us, apart from people."
"3652","3895 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hafs - that is Ibn Ghiyath - narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: al-Muraqqa' al-Usaydi narrated to me, he said: Abu Dharr said: (By Him besides whom there is no god, no one was entitled to enter ihram for Hajj then annul it as Umra except the company who were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him))."
"3653","3896 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: al-Muraqqa' informed me, from Abu Dharr, he said: (No one after us was entitled to enter ihram for Hajj then annul it as Umra)."
"3654","3897 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Abd al-Akram, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, that he said concerning mut'at al-Hajj: (It is not for you, and you have nothing to do with it)."
"3655","3898 - Fahd - that is Ibn Sulayman - narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim al-Taymi narrated to me, from his father, he said: Abu Dharr said: (Mut'a was only for us specifically, the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the mut'a of Hajj)."
"3656","3899 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Mihran - that is al-A'mash, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. And he added: (Meaning: al-faskh)."
"3657","3900 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Mu'awiya ibn Ishaq, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, he said: (Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked about mut'at al-Hajj, and he said: It was for us, not for you)."
"3658","3901 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana and Salih ibn Musa al-Talhi narrated to us, from Mu'awiya ibn Ishaq, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Except that he said: (Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked, or: I asked him)."
"3659","3902 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Nadra narrated to us, that he heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri saying: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, stood as preacher when he was made caliph, and said: (Allah Almighty had given license to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what He wished. Lo, the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has been taken away; so guard the chastity of these women, and complete Hajj and Umra for Allah, as He commanded you)."
"3660","3903 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Nadra, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: We arrived with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) crying out Hajj aloud. When we arrived at Mecca we circumambulated the House and al-Safa and al-Marwa; then when it was the Day of Tarwiya, we entered ihram for Hajj. When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, (became caliph) he said: (Allah Almighty had given license to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what He wished, so complete Hajj and Umra). Abu Ja'far said: And included in this is also the hadith of Abu Musa which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter."
"3661","3904 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Nadra, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Two mut'as we practiced in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade them both, so we shall never return to them."
"3662","3905 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: Kathir ibn Abd Allah - a man of Muzayna - informed me, from one of his grandfathers - or uncles - that he said: (No one after us was entitled to enter ihram for Hajj then annul it as Umra)."
"3663","3906 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Kathir ibn Abd Allah, from Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abd Allah ibn Hilal, a Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has clarified in what we mentioned from him in these reports that that annulment which he commanded his Companions with was specific to them, not for anyone after them. And we joined to what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that what we narrated from those we mentioned in this section of his Companions; for that - in our view - is of what they could not have said from their own opinions, but said it from what they learned, so in what they said on that they are like one who attributed it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). It is established by authenticating these reports that leaving Hajj is only by circumambulation of the House. A group denied the annulment of Hajj, and mentioned on that what follows."
"3664","3907 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: We went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as pilgrims, and we did not exit from anything we had entered ihram for, until it was the Day of Sacrifice. 3908 - Of the argument against whoever adduced this is that Bakr ibn Abd Allah narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions arrived at Mecca reciting talbiya for Hajj, and he said: Whoever wishes to make it an Umra, let him do so, except whoever has a sacrificial animal with him; and that has been mentioned with its chain in this chapter. So in this is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave them leave to exit ihram if they wished, except that he did not insist on that to them. So it is possible they did not exit ihram, though they were entitled to exit ihram; so that returns to the annulment of Hajj for whoever wished to annul it into Umra. And there has been narrated from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, also on that what follows."
"3665","3909 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, from Aisha, she said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of the Farewell Hajj; some of us entered ihram for Umra, some entered ihram for Hajj and Umra, and some entered ihram for Hajj; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram for Hajj. As for whoever entered ihram for Umra, he exited ihram; and as for whoever entered ihram for Hajj or combined Hajj and Umra, they did not exit ihram until it was the Day of Sacrifice. It is possible that was with her as it was with Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, upon what we mentioned. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of authenticating the meanings of the reports. As for its aspect by way of reflection: we found the principle that whoever enters ihram for Umra and circumambulates for it and runs has finished it, and may shave and exit ihram, when he has not driven a sacrificial animal. And we saw him, when he had driven a sacrificial animal for tamattu' and circumambulated for his Umra and ran, not exit from his Umra until the Day of Sacrifice, when he exits from it and from his Hajj with a single exiting. The Sunna came to that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as an answer to Hafsa, may Allah be pleased with her, when she said to him: What is with people that they exited ihram while you did not exit from your Umra? He said: I have matted my head and garlanded my sacrificial animal, so I shall not exit ihram until I sacrifice. So the sacrificial animal he drove for his tamattu', for which no sacrificial animal is due upon him unless he performs Hajj after it, prevented him from exiting ihram by tawaf until the Day of Sacrifice; for the contract of his ihram was thus: to enter Umra and complete it, yet not exit from it until he enters ihram for Hajj, then exit from it and from the Umra he put before it together. Had Umra been ordered for them separately, he would have exited from it upon finishing it when he shaved, and would not have waited for the Day of Sacrifice. And when he drove a sacrificial animal for Hajj, entering ihram for it after finishing that Umra, he remained in his ihram until the Day of Sacrifice. Since the sacrificial animal, which is of the cause of Hajj, prevented exiting ihram by circumambulation of the House before the Day of Sacrifice, his entering Hajj was more deserving to prevent him from that until the Day of Sacrifice. This is the reflection also in our view, and it is the view of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"3666","Chapter: The qarin - how many tawafs are upon him for his Umra and his Hajj? 3910 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari and Muhammad ibn Idris al-Makki narrated to us, they said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever combines Hajj and Umra, one tawaf and one sa'y suffice him for both, then he shall not exit ihram until he exits from both together. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to this hadith and said: Upon the qarin between Hajj and Umra is one tawaf; no other tawaf is due upon him. Others disagreed and said: Rather, he performs one tawaf for each of them and one sa'y for both. Of the argument for them on that is that this hadith is a mistake which al-Darawardi erred in, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); its origin is only from Ibn Umar, from himself - thus the precise narrators narrated it. Moreover, they do not adduce al-Darawardi from Ubayd Allah at all, so how do they adduce him in this? As for what the precise narrators narrated of that from Ubayd Allah, it is what follows."
"3667","3911 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to say: (When he combines, he performs one tawaf for both; when he separates, he performs a tawaf and a sa'y for each). If one says: Ayyub ibn Musa and Musa ibn Uqba narrated from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what returns in meaning to the meaning of what al-Darawardi narrated. And there has been mentioned on that what follows."
"3668","3912 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, went out from Medina to Mecca entering ihram for Umra fearing prevention, then said: The matter of the two is but one; I call you to witness that I have joined a Hajj to my Umra. Then he arrived and performed one tawaf for both, and said: Thus did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3669","3913 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it. They said: This has agreed with what al-Darawardi narrated, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). It is said to them: How may you accept this from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, when the following has come?"
"3670","3914 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim informed me that Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tamattu' in the Farewell Hajj with Umra to Hajj, and sacrificed, driving the sacrificial animal from Dhu al-Hulayfa. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began and entered ihram for Umra, then entered ihram for Hajj; and the people performed tamattu' with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Umra to Hajj. So here Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, informs from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he was in the Farewell Hajj a mutamatti', and that he began and entered ihram for Umra. And the following has come."
"3671","3915 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Humayd informed me, from Bakr ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions arrived at Mecca reciting talbiya for Hajj. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes, let him make it an Umra, except whoever has a sacrificial animal with him. So Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed in this hadith of Bakr that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived at Mecca reciting talbiya for Hajj. And he informed in the hadith of Salim that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began and entered ihram for Umra. So its meaning - in our view, and Allah knows best - is that he first entered ihram for Hajj, as Hajj, then annulled it and made it Umra, then recited talbiya for Umra, then performed tamattu' with it to Hajj, so that these two hadiths of Salim and Bakr both hold and do not contradict. And the annulment by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the Hajj which he had done and commanded his Companions with was after their circumambulation of the House. We mentioned that in the chapter of faskh al-Hajj, which spares us repeating it here. It thereby became impossible that the tawaf which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed for the Umra into which his Hajj turned suffices him for the tawaf of his Hajj for which he entered ihram after that. Rather, its aspect in our view - and Allah knows best - is that he did not circumambulate for his Hajj before the Day of Sacrifice; for the tawaf performed before the Day of Sacrifice in Hajj is performed for arrival, not as part of the essence of Hajj. So Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, sufficed with the tawaf he had performed after arrival in his Umra instead of repeating it in his Hajj. And this is like what has been narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, also of his practice."
"3672","3916 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, when he arrived at Mecca would run (ramal) at the House, then run between al-Safa and al-Marwa; and when he recited talbiya from Mecca for it, he would not run at the House, and would delay the running between al-Safa and al-Marwa until the Day of Sacrifice, and would not run on the Day of Sacrifice. So what we mentioned indicates that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, when he entered ihram for Hajj from Mecca, would not circumambulate for it until the Day of Sacrifice. Likewise what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his entering ihram for the Hajj for which he entered ihram after annulling his first Hajj: he had not circumambulated for it until the Day of Sacrifice. So there is nothing in the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the ruling of the qarin's tawaf for his Umra and his Hajj. And there is established by what we mentioned also the error of al-Darawardi in the hadith of Ubayd Allah which we described. The people of the first view also adduced for their view what follows."
"3673","3917 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us. (H). 3918 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwa, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Hajj, and we entered ihram for Umra. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has a sacrificial animal with him, let him enter ihram for Hajj with Umra, then not exit ihram until he exits from both together. I arrived at Mecca while menstruating; I had not circumambulated the House nor between al-Safa and al-Marwa. I complained of that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Undo your head, comb, enter ihram for Hajj, and leave the Umra. When I completed Hajj, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me with Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, to al-Tan'im, and I performed Umra. He said: This is in place of your Umra. She said: (Those who entered ihram for Umra circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwa, then exited ihram, then performed another tawaf after they returned from Mina for their Hajj. As for those who combined Hajj and Umra, they performed only one tawaf for both). They said: Here Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: (As for those who combined Hajj and Umra, they performed only one tawaf); and they were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and by his command they acted. So in that is what indicates that upon the qarin for his Hajj and his Umra is one tawaf, nothing else upon him. Of our argument against their opponents was that we narrated from Uqayl, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, earlier in this chapter, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Hajj performed tamattu', and the people performed tamattu' with him. And the mutamatti' we know is one who enters ihram for Hajj after his tawaf for Umra. Then Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said in the hadith of Malik from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Hajj, and we entered ihram for Umra; I was told that they entered their ihram as mutamatti'un enter. She said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has a sacrificial animal, let him enter ihram for Hajj with Umra, then not exit ihram until he exits from both. He did not clarify in this hadith the place in which he said this to them. It is possible he said it to them before entering Mecca, or after entering Mecca before tawaf, so they would be qarinun by that Hajj with the Umra for which they had entered ihram before it. And it is possible he said that to them after their tawaf for Umra, so they would be mutamatti'un by that Hajj which he commanded them to enter ihram for. We looked into that and found Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, and Abu Sa'id al-Khudri informing in their two hadiths, which we narrated from them in the chapter of faskh al-Hajj, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that statement at the last circuit upon al-Marwa. So we knew that the statement of Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, in the hadith of Malik - as for those who combined Umra and Hajj - means she intends combining of tamattu', not combining of qiran - she said: (They performed only one tawaf), meaning: they performed only one tawaf after their combining Hajj and Umra, for which Umra they had already performed one tawaf; for that Hajj of theirs joined with Umra was Makkan, and the Makkan Hajj is not circumambulated for before Arafa, but only after Arafa, upon what Ibn Umar used to do in what we narrated from him. So the meaning of what we narrated from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, in this chapter, and what we authenticated of that to remove contradiction from it, returns to the meaning of what we narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and what we authenticated of that. Nothing of this indicates the ruling of the qarin of a Kufan Hajj with a Kufan Umra, how his tawaf for them is: is it one tawaf or two? Those who held that one tawaf suffices the qarin for his Umra and his Hajj also adduced what follows."
"3674","3919 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. (H). 3920 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, they said: Ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: When you return to Mecca, your tawaf suffices you for your Hajj and your Umra. They said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that what is upon her for her Hajj and her Umra is one tawaf. It is said to them: This is not the wording of this hadith which you narrated; its wording is that he said: Your tawaf for your Hajj suffices you for your Hajj and your Umra. He informed that the tawaf performed for Hajj suffices for Hajj and Umra; and you do not say this - you say only that the qarin's tawaf is a tawaf for his qiran, neither for his Hajj apart from his Umra, nor for his Umra apart from his Hajj - besides that others than Ibn Abi Najih, of Ata's companions, narrated this very hadith from Ata upon a meaning other than this meaning."
"3675","3921 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj informed me, and Abd al-Malik informed me, from Ata, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, will all your family return with Hajj and Umra except me? He said: March on; it suffices you. Hajjaj said in his hadith from Ata, he said: She insisted upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he commanded her to go out to al-Tan'im and enter ihram from it for Umra, sending with her her brother Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr; she entered ihram from it for Umra, then arrived and circumambulated, ran and shortened, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificed on her behalf. Abd al-Malik said from Ata: He sacrificed a cow on her behalf. So Abd al-Malik informed, from Ata, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, of her story at length, and that she entered ihram for Umra only at the time when she was to march after her completion of Hajj and Umra, and that what was mentioned as sufficing her is Hajj, of Hajj and Umra, not tawaf. So it is nullified that in this hadith of Ata is any proof concerning the ruling of the qarin's tawaf, how it is. Those who held also concerning the qarin that he performs one tawaf for his Umra and his Hajj adduced what follows."
"3676","3922 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, that Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, says: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Aisha while she was weeping, and said: What makes you weep? She said: I weep because people exited ihram while I did not exit, and circumambulated the House while I did not circumambulate, and this Hajj has come as you see. He said: This is a matter Allah has decreed upon the daughters of Adam; so bathe, enter ihram for Hajj, then perform Hajj, and perform what the pilgrim performs, except that you do not circumambulate the House nor pray. She said: I did that; when I became pure he said: Circumambulate the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwa, then you have exited from your Hajj and your Umra. I said: O Messenger, I find in myself about my Umra that I did not circumambulate until I performed Hajj. So he commanded Abd al-Rahman, who made her perform Umra from al-Tan'im."
"3677","3923 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: al-Layth informed me, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. They said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her, while she was in ihram for Umra and Hajj, to circumambulate the House and run between al-Safa and al-Marwa, then exit ihram. So that indicates that the ruling of the qarin in his tawaf for his Hajj and his Umra is likewise, one tawaf, with nothing else of tawaf upon him. Of the argument against the people of this other view was that this hadith of Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, has been narrated otherwise than what we mentioned."
"3678","3924 - Abu Bakra and Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, they said: Uthman ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed me, he said: Hisham ibn Urwa informed me, from Urwa, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us and said: Whoever wishes may enter ihram for Hajj, and whoever wishes may enter ihram for Umra. She said: I was of those who entered ihram for Umra; I menstruated, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me and commanded me to undo my head, comb, and leave my Umra."
"3679","3925 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Za'ida narrated to us, from Isra'il, from Zayd ibn al-Hasan, from Ikrima, from Aisha, the like of it."
"3680","3926 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Za'ida narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Abi Mulayka, from Aisha, the like of it. So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her - when she menstruated - to leave her Umra, before her tawaf for it. So how could her tawaf in her Hajj for which she entered ihram after that suffice her for that Hajj of hers and for her Umra which she had abandoned? This is impossible. And al-Aswad narrated from her on that also what follows."
"3681","3927 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We went out seeing nothing but Hajj. When he arrived at Mecca he circumambulated and did not exit ihram, and he had a sacrificial animal with him. Those with him of his women and his Companions circumambulated; those of them who did not have a sacrificial animal exited ihram. He said: And she menstruated. She said: So we completed our rites of our Hajj; when it was the night of al-Hasba, the night of departure, I said: O Messenger of Allah, will your Companions return with Hajj and Umra while I return with Hajj? He said: Had you not circumambulated the House on the nights we arrived? She said: I said: No. He said: Go with your brother to al-Tan'im, enter ihram for Umra, then your appointment is such-and-such place. So in this hadith is what indicates that she had exited from her Umra which had taken the place of her Hajj by annulling Hajj, by her proceeding to Arafa before her tawaf for it. For the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Had you not circumambulated on the nights we arrived? Meaning: had you circumambulated, your Umra would have been completed for you along with your Hajj from which you have finished. When she told him she had not circumambulated on the nights they arrived, he made her - by what she did after that for her Hajj, of her standing at Arafa or heading to it - out of her Umra, commanding her to perform another Umra in its place from al-Tan'im. So how may one say that her tawaf of the House for a Hajj she is in counts for that Hajj and for another Umra from which she had exited before that? This is impossible in our view. And al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrated, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, on that what follows."
"3682","3928 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Salama narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning nothing but Hajj. When we came to Sarif I menstruated. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me while I was weeping. He said: What makes you weep? I said: I wish I had not performed Hajj this year - or: had not gone out this year. He said: Perhaps you are menstruating? I said: Yes. He said: This is a matter Allah Almighty has decreed upon the daughters of Adam; so do what the pilgrims do, except that you do not circumambulate the House. She said: When we came to Mecca, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Make it an Umra. So the people exited ihram except whoever had a sacrificial animal with him; the sacrificial animal was with him, and with Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman and the well-to-do. Then they entered ihram for Hajj. When it was the Day of Sacrifice I became pure, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me and I performed ifadah. Beef was brought, and I said: What is this? They said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificed cattle on behalf of his women. When it was the night of al-Hasba I said: O Messenger of Allah, people return with Hajj and Umra while I return with Hajj. So he commanded Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, who mounted me behind him; I remember I would doze and my face would strike the back of the saddle, until we came to al-Tan'im and I entered ihram for Umra, as recompense for the Umra of people which they performed. This is like the hadith before it, and Urwa narrated it from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, more clearly than that."
"3683","3929 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, she said: We went out keeping pace with the crescent. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes to enter ihram for Hajj, let him do so; and whoever wishes to enter ihram for Umra, let him do so. As for me, I enter ihram for Hajj, for I have a sacrificial animal with me. Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Some of us entered ihram for Hajj, some entered ihram for Umra; as for me, I entered ihram for Umra. The day of Arafa came upon me while I was menstruating. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave your Umra aside, undo your hair, comb, then recite talbiya for Hajj. So I recited talbiya for Hajj. When it was the night of al-Hasba and I became pure, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, who took me to al-Tan'im, and I recited talbiya for Umra, as make-up for her Umra. So Aisha clarified that her Hajj was separate from her Umra; between them she had undone her hair and combed. So how may her tawaf for her Hajj - between which and her Umra was what we mentioned of release - suffice her for her Umra and her Hajj? This is impossible, and it is more deserving than the hadith of Abu al-Zubayr from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him; for in that Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, only reported the story of Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, and that she had not become lawful between her Umra and her Hajj, while Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, reported in this the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command to her before her entering her Hajj to leave her Umra and to do what the lawful do, with what she mentioned in her hadith. That also indicates that the hadith of Ata from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, as al-Hajjaj and Abd al-Malik narrated it from him, is not as Ibn Abi Najih narrated it from him. Those who said the qarin performs one tawaf for his Hajj and his Umra also adduced what follows."
"3684","3930 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Artah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined Hajj and Umra, performing one tawaf for both. It is said to them: How strange is this! You adduce the like of this, while you narrated from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Hajj singly. And from Ibn Jurayj, al-Awza'i, Amr ibn Dinar and Qays ibn Sa'd, from Ata, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that they arrived on the morning of the fourth reciting talbiya for Hajj, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them to make it an Umra, while he was upon al-Safa at the last circuit. So how do you accept the like of that and claim the like of this? If they adduce on that what follows."
"3685","3931 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Rabah ibn Abi Ma'ruf narrated to us, from Ata, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not add to one tawaf. It is said to them: Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, means by this the running between al-Safa and al-Marwa; Abu al-Zubayr clarified that from him."
"3686","3932 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, he heard Jabir saying: Neither the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nor his Companions ran between al-Safa and al-Marwa except once. Jabir intended by this only to inform them that sa'y between al-Safa and al-Marwa is not done in the tawaf of the Day of Sacrifice nor in the farewell tawaf, as it is done in the arrival tawaf. There is nothing in any of this proving whether what is upon the qarin of tawaf for his Umra and his Hajj is one tawaf or two. If one says: It is authentic from Ibn Umar in his statement concerning the qarin that he performs one tawaf for his Umra and his Hajj - then to whose statement do you oppose his statement? It is said to him: To the statement of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and Abd Allah."
"3687","3933 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, or Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abu Nasr, he said: I entered ihram for Hajj, then met Ali. I said to him: I entered ihram for Hajj; can I add an Umra to it? He said: (No; had you entered ihram for Umra then wished to join Hajj to it, you would join it). He said: I said: What do I do if I wish that? He said: Pour over yourself a skin of water, then enter ihram for both together, and perform a tawaf for each of them."
"3688","3934 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, he said: Mansur informed me, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abu Nasr al-Sulami, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it. 3935 - Abu Dawud said: Mansur said: I mentioned that to Mujahid, and he said: We used to give people fatwa only with one tawaf, but now, no."
"3689","3936 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ata narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim and Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Udhayna, he said: I asked Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the like of it."
"3690","3937 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Sulayman, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"3691","3938 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Malik, from Abu Nasr, the like of it. 3939 - Mansur said: I mentioned that to Mujahid, and he said: I used to give people fatwa only with one tawaf, but now, no."
"3692","3940 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn Makhlad narrated to us. (H). 3941 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, they said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Zadham, from al-Hakam, from Ziyad ibn Malik, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and Abd Allah, they said: (The qarin performs two tawafs and two sa'ys). So here Ali and Abd Allah held concerning the qarin's tawaf contrary to what Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, held. As for its aspect by way of reflection: we saw that when a man enters ihram for Hajj, there becomes due upon him with it the tawaf of the House and the sa'y between al-Safa and al-Marwa, and there becomes due upon him for violating what was made forbidden to him by his ihram for it, of expiations, what is due upon him in that. Likewise when he enters ihram for Umra, there becomes due upon him also with it the tawaf of the House and the sa'y between al-Safa and al-Marwa, and there becomes due upon him for violating what was made forbidden to him by his ihram for it, of expiations, what is due upon him in that. When he combines them, all agree he is in two sacred states: the sanctity of Hajj and the sanctity of Umra. So it would follow in reflection that there is due upon him for each of them of tawaf and sa'y, and other than that of expiations for violating the sanctities made forbidden upon him in them, what would have been due upon him for each had he performed it singly. It was objected to this view: we saw the lawful person who takes game in the Haram, upon whom recompense is due for the sanctity of the Haram; and we saw the muhrim who takes game in the Hill, upon whom recompense is due for the sanctity of ihram; and we saw the muhrim when he takes game in the Haram, upon whom one recompense is due, for the sanctity of ihram, with the sanctity of recompense for the Haram entering into it - while at the time he took that game he was in two sanctities: sanctity of ihram and sanctity of Haram - so why is there not due upon him for each of the two sanctities what would have been due upon him for each had he been singly in it? They said: likewise the qarin, of what would have been due upon him for each of his Umra and his Hajj had he performed it singly, there is due upon him when he combines them only the like of what is due upon him for one of them, with what would have been due upon him for the other, had it been single, entering into that. It is said to him: you have not decisively held that what is due upon the muhrim for killing game in the Haram is one recompense. Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them, said: analogy in their view in that was that two recompenses are due upon him: a recompense for the sanctity of ihram and a recompense for the sanctity of the Haram, and they departed from that only by istihsan. But we do not say in that as they said; rather, analogy in our view in that is what they mentioned they deemed preferable. That is because we saw the agreed principle that a man may combine Hajj and Umra, but may not combine two Hajjs nor two Umras. So he may by one ihram combine two different forms and enter into both, but may not combine two things of one kind. Since that was so, he may also by rendering one recompense combine what is due upon him by two different sanctities - the sanctity of the Haram, in which fasting does not suffice, and the sanctity of ihram, in which fasting suffices - rendering by that one recompense what is due upon him in both. But he may not by rendering one recompense combine what is due upon him for violating two concordant sanctities of one form - the sanctity of Umra and the sanctity of Hajj - just as he may not by one ihram enter the sanctity of two concordant things. Since that was also so, and the tawaf for Hajj and the tawaf for Umra are of one form, he cannot by one tawaf enter into both, nor does that tawaf suffice for both; he needs to enter into each of them separately, by analogy and reflection upon what we mentioned of what he combines by one ihram, of the different Hajj and Umra, and of what we mentioned of what he does not combine, of two concordant Hajjs and two concordant Umras. If one says: we saw him exit from his Hajj and his Umra by one shaving, with nothing else upon him; so likewise he performs one tawaf for both and one sa'y for both, with nothing else upon him. It is said to him: we saw him exit by one shaving from two different ihrams, in which nothing but two different tawafs suffice him. That is because had a man entered ihram for Umra, circumambulated for it and run, driving a sacrificial animal, then performed Hajj in his year, thereby becoming a mutamatti', his ruling on the Day of Sacrifice would be to shave one shaving, exiting thereby from both together. So he would exit by one shaving from two different ihrams into which he had entered separately. What was due of the ruling of shaving did not necessitate that the ruling of tawaf for them be likewise, one tawaf; rather, it is two tawafs. Likewise of what we mentioned of the qarin's shaving for his Umra and his Hajj with one shaving does not necessitate that likewise the ruling of his tawaf for them be one tawaf. Since he may exit in the two ihrams into which he entered separately by one shaving, in the two ihrams into which he entered with one entry he is more deserving to exit from them likewise. This is the reflection in this chapter upon what was narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and Abd Allah, of the obligation of tawaf for each of Umra and Hajj, and upon what we mentioned of reflection on that, of the obligation of recompense for each of them for violating their sanctity. And it is the view of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"3693","Chapter: The ruling of standing at Muzdalifa. 3942 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid informed me, from al-Sha'bi, from Urwa ibn Mudarris, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Jam', and said: O Messenger of Allah, is there Hajj for me, though I have worn out my mount? He said: Whoever prayed with us this prayer, having stood with us before that and poured forth from Arafa by night or day, his Hajj is complete and he has completed his rite."
"3694","3943 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb informed me, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Ibn Abi al-Safar, and Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi; and Zakariyya from al-Sha'bi; and Dawud ibn Abi Hind from al-Sha'bi, from Urwa ibn Mudarris, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3695","3944 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi; and Ibn Abi Za'ida from al-Sha'bi; and Zakariyya from al-Sha'bi; and Dawud ibn Abi Hind said: I heard Urwa ibn Mudarris ibn Aws ibn Haritha ibn La'im al-Ta'i saying: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Muzdalifa and said: O Messenger of Allah, I came from the two mountains of Tayyi; by Allah, I did not come until I tired myself and wore out my mount, and I left no mountain of these mountains but I stood upon it. Is there Hajj for me? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever witnessed with us this prayer, the Fajr prayer at Muzdalifa, having stood at Arafa before that by night or day, his Hajj is complete and he has completed his rite. Sufyan said: And Zakariyya added in it - and he was the most retentive of the three for this hadith - he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I came this very hour from the two mountains of Tayyi, having exhausted my mount and tired myself; is there Hajj for me? He said: Whoever witnessed with us this prayer and stood with us until we pour forth, having stood before that at Arafa by night or day, his Hajj is complete and he has completed his rite. Sufyan said: And Dawud ibn Abi Hind added, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when dawn flashed, then he mentioned the hadith. Abu Ja'far said: A group held that standing at Muzdalifa is obligatory, Hajj not valid without attaining it. They adduced the statement of Allah Almighty: {Then when you pour forth from Arafat, remember Allah at al-Mash'ar al-Haram}, and this hadith we narrated. They said: Allah Almighty mentioned al-Mash'ar al-Haram in His Book as He mentioned Arafat, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned it in his Sunna; so their ruling is one: Hajj does not suffice without attaining it. Others disagreed and said: As for standing at Arafa, it is of the essence of Hajj, which Hajj does not suffice without attaining; as for standing at Muzdalifa, it is not so. Of the argument for them on that is that the statement of Allah Almighty: {Then when you pour forth from Arafat, remember Allah at al-Mash'ar al-Haram} contains no proof it is obligatory; for Allah Almighty mentioned only remembrance, not standing, and all agree that had he stood at Muzdalifa without remembering Allah Almighty, his Hajj is complete. Since the remembrance mentioned in the Book is not of the essence of Hajj, the place in which that remembrance occurs - not mentioned in the Book - is more deserving not to be obligatory. Allah Almighty mentioned things of Hajj in His Book without intending their obligation, such that Hajj does not suffice without attaining them in anyone's view: of that His Almighty statement: {Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwa are of the rites of Allah; whoever performs Hajj or Umra, no blame upon him to circumambulate them}; and all agree that had he performed Hajj without running between al-Safa and al-Marwa, his Hajj is complete, with a blood (sacrifice) in place of what he omitted. Likewise Allah Almighty's mention of al-Mash'ar al-Haram in His Book contains no proof of its obligation such that Hajj does not suffice without attaining it. As for what is in the hadith of Urwa ibn Mudarris, there is likewise no proof of what they mentioned; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in it only: whoever prayed with us this prayer of ours, having come to Arafa before that by night or day, his Hajj is complete and he has completed his rite. He mentioned prayer, and all agree that had he spent the night there and stood but slept through prayer, not praying it with the imam until he missed it, his Hajj is complete. Since attending prayer with the imam mentioned in this hadith is not of the essence of Hajj without which Hajj does not suffice, the place in which that prayer occurs - not mentioned in the hadith - is more deserving not to be so. So by this hadith obligation is established only for Arafa specifically. And Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'mar al-Dayli narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that."
"3696","3945 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Ata, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'mar al-Dayli, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing at Arafat. People of Najd came and asked him about Hajj. He said: Hajj is the day of Arafa; whoever reaches Jam' before the Fajr prayer has reached Hajj. The days of Mina are three days: the days of tashriq: {Whoever hastens in two days, no sin upon him; and whoever delays, no sin upon him}. Then he mounted a man behind him to proclaim that."
"3697","3946 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shababa ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Ata, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'mar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it, without mentioning the question of the people of Najd nor his mounting the man. So in this hadith is that the people of Najd asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about Hajj, and his answer to them was: Hajj is the day of Arafa. We know the answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the complete answer, with no deficiency nor excess; for Allah Almighty gave him the concise and sealed speech. Had they, when they asked him about Hajj, meant by that what is indispensable in Hajj, he would have mentioned Arafa, tawaf, Muzdalifa and what is done of Hajj. Since he omitted that in his answer to them, we knew that what they meant by asking him about Hajj is what, if missed, Hajj is missed; so he answered them saying: Hajj is the day of Arafa. Had Muzdalifa been like Arafa, he would have mentioned Muzdalifa along with Arafa; but he mentioned Arafa specifically, for it is the essence of Hajj, which if missed, Hajj is missed. Then he said fresh speech, that people may know that whoever reaches Jam' before dawn has reached Hajj - not meaning he has reached all of Hajj; for it is established in the first of his speech: Hajj is Arafa, necessitating thereby that missing Arafa is missing Hajj. Then he said: Whoever reaches Jam' before the Fajr prayer has reached Hajj - not meaning nothing of Hajj remains upon him; for after that is the visiting tawaf, obligatory and indispensable - but: he has reached Hajj by his prior standing at Arafa. This is the best construction of the meanings of these reports, authenticated upon it without contradiction. As for its aspect by way of reflection: we saw the agreed principle that the weak may hasten from Jam' by night. Likewise the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the young boys of Banu Abd al-Muttalib, which we shall mention in its place in this book, Allah willing. And he gave license to Sawda to leave standing at it."
"3698","3947 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim informed me, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Sawda was a slow, heavy woman. She sought permission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to pour forth from Jam' before standing, and he permitted her. I wish I had sought his permission and he had permitted me. Abu Ja'far said: So standing at Muzdalifa lapsed from them due to excuse; while Arafa must be stood at, not lapsing for any excuse. What lapses by excuse is what is not of the essence of Hajj; what is indispensable, lapsing neither by excuse nor otherwise, is of the essence of Hajj. Do you not see that the visiting tawaf is of the essence of Hajj, not lapsing from the menstruating woman by excuse, while the farewell tawaf is not of the essence of Hajj, lapsing from the menstruating woman by the excuse of menstruation? Since standing at Muzdalifa is of what lapses by excuse, it is of the kind that is not obligatory. Thereby what we described is established. And it is the view of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"3699","Chapter: Combining the two prayers at Jam' - how is it? 3948 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Isra'il informed me, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: (I went out with Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, to Mecca. When he came to Jam', he prayed the two prayers, each of them with adhan and iqama, and did not pray between them)."
"3700","3949 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that he prayed with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, two prayers twice at Jam', each prayer with adhan and iqama, with dinner between them. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to these two hadiths and claimed that Maghrib and Isha are combined at Muzdalifa with two adhans and two iqamas. Others disagreed and said: The first of them is prayed with adhan and iqama, and the second is prayed with neither adhan nor iqama. They said: As for what was of Umar's, may Allah be pleased with him, act, and his calling adhan for the second, he did that only because people had dispersed for their dinner, so he called adhan to gather them. Likewise we say: when people disperse from the imam for dinner or otherwise, he commands the muezzin, who calls adhan so they gather for his adhan. This is the meaning of what was narrated on this from Umar; and what was narrated from Abd Allah is likewise."
"3701","3950 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, used to place dinner at Muzdalifa between the two prayers. So the meaning of what was narrated from Abd Allah on this returns to the meaning of what was narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, also. Then we considered what was narrated on that when they are prayed together, how we act in them."
"3702","3951 - For Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from al-Hakam, that he prayed with Sa'id ibn Jubayr at Jam' Maghrib as three and Isha as two, with one iqama. Then he narrated that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, did the like of that, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did the like of that in that place."
"3703","3952 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from al-Hakam, that he prayed with Sa'id ibn Jubayr at Jam' Maghrib as three and Isha as two, with one iqama. Then he narrated that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, did the like of that, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did the like of that in that place."
"3704","3953 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam ibn Utayba and Salama ibn Kuhayl informed me, they said: Sa'id ibn Jubayr led us in prayer with one iqama, Maghrib as three; when he gave salam he stood and prayed the two rak'as of Isha. Then he narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he did with them in that place the like of that, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did with them in that place the like of that."
"3705","3954 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from al-Hakam, he said: I witnessed Sa'id ibn Jubayr call the iqama at Jam' for prayer - and I think he said: (he called adhan) - and he prayed Maghrib as three, then stood and prayed Isha as two with the first iqama. And he narrated that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, did in this place this, and narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did the like of that."
"3706","3955 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Salama, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Maghrib and Isha at Jam' with one iqama."
"3707","3956 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'ba, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Malik, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3708","3957 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us. (H). 3958 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Sufyan ibn Sa'id al-Thawri informed me, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Malik, he said: I prayed with Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, Maghrib as three and Isha as two with one iqama. It was said to him: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, what is this? He said: I prayed them with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this place with one iqama."
"3709","3959 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiya narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Malik ibn al-Harith, he said: Abd Allah ibn Umar led us in prayer at Muzdalifa in the Maghrib prayer with an iqama with no adhan, three rak'as, then gave salam, then said: The prayer; then stood and prayed Isha as two, then gave salam. Khalid ibn Malik al-Harithi said to him: What is this prayer, O Abu Abd al-Rahman? He said: I prayed these two prayers with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this place, with no adhan with them."
"3710","3960 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: Four narrated to me, all trustworthy, among them Sa'id ibn Jubayr and Ali al-Azdi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he prayed Maghrib and Isha at Muzdalifa with one iqama. So here Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, informs from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he prayed them without calling adhan between them nor iqama. And there has been narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, on this something with wording other than this wording."
"3711","3961 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Maghrib and Isha at Muzdalifa together; he did not call out in either of them except with iqama, and did not glorify (pray voluntary) between them nor after either of them."
"3712","3962 - Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Nafi', from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Except that he said: He did not call out between them nor after either of them except with iqama. And thus is my memorization from Yunus, from Ibn Wahb, except that I found it in my book as I stated it in the hadith before this."
"3713","3963 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined the two prayers at Jam'; he did not call out in each of them except with iqama, and did not glorify between them. His statement in this hadith: he did not call out in each of them except with iqama - that may mean he intended the iqama he called for each of them, or the iqama he called for both together; but the most deserving for us is to take it upon the iqama he called for both, that its meaning agree with the meaning of what we narrated before that, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And there has been narrated from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari and from al-Bara ibn Azib what agrees with that also."
"3714","3964 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Umar ibn al-Rumi narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' informed me, he said: Ghaylan informed me, from Adi ibn Thabit al-Ansari, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Ansari, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, he said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Maghrib and Isha with one iqama."
"3715","3965 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Abu Yusuf informed me, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Adi ibn Thabit, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid, from al-Bara ibn Azib, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Others disagreed with them and said: Rather, he prays the first of them with adhan and iqama, and the second with iqama without adhan. They adduced on that what follows."
"3716","3966 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, and he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he came to Muzdalifa, prayed therein Maghrib and Isha with one adhan and two iqamas. So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Maghrib and Isha with one adhan and two iqamas; this is contrary to what Malik ibn al-Harith narrated from Ibn Umar. And they agreed that the first of the two prayers combined at Arafa is called adhan for and iqama; so reflection upon that is that likewise is the ruling of the first of the two prayers combined at Jam'."
"3717","3967 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Malik informed me, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Kurayb, freedman of Abd Allah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Usama ibn Zayd, that he heard him saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) moved from Arafa until when he was at the ravine he dismounted and urinated, then performed wudu without completing it. I said to him: The prayer. He said: The prayer is ahead of you. He rode until he came to Muzdalifa, dismounted, performed wudu completing it; then iqama for prayer was called and he prayed Maghrib. Then every man made his camel kneel at his place; then iqama for Isha was called and he prayed it, praying nothing between them. Reports differed from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the two prayers at Muzdalifa: did he pray them together, or do an act between them? There has been narrated on that what we mentioned in the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Usama. And it differed from him how he prayed them: some said with adhan and iqama; some said with one adhan and two iqamas; some said with one iqama with no adhan with them. Since they differed on that as we mentioned, and the two prayers combined at Muzdalifa - Maghrib and Isha - are as the two prayers combined at Arafa - Zuhr and Asr; this combining in both places is only for a muhrim in the sanctity of Hajj, not for a lawful person nor a mere mu'tamir; and the two prayers at Arafa are prayed one immediately after the other, with no act between them, called for with one adhan and two iqamas, as is done at Arafa likewise. This is the reflection in this chapter, contrary to the view of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah be pleased with them. That is because they held concerning combining the two prayers at Arafa to what we mentioned, and held concerning combining the two prayers at Muzdalifa that it be with one adhan and one iqama, adducing on that what was narrated from Ibn Umar. Sufyan al-Thawri held in that that he prays them with one iqama with no adhan with them, upon what we narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). What we narrated from Jabir on this is dearer to us, for what reflection testifies to it; then afterwards we found the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, returning to the meaning of the hadith of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him."
"3718","3968 - And that is because Harun ibn Kamil and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, that Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined Maghrib and Isha at Jam' - that is Muzdalifa: he prayed Maghrib as three, then gave salam, then called iqama for Isha and prayed it as two, then gave salam, with no prostration (voluntary) between them. So this informs that he prayed them with two iqamas. And we found from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, himself, not raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he called adhan for them."
"3719","3969 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Bishr informed me, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he combined Maghrib and Isha at Jam' with adhan and iqama, placing nothing between them. So it was impossible he introduced an adhan therein unless he learned it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and what we narrated from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, on this is dearer to us, for what reflection testifies to it."
"3720","Chapter: The time of stoning Jamrat al-Aqaba for the weak who are licensed to leave standing at Muzdalifa. 3970 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us. (H). 3971 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Wahb narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Shu'ba, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I was of those the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent on the Day of Sacrifice; we stoned the Jamra at dawn."
"3721","3972 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Khallad ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Sufayr narrated to us, from Ata, he said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to al-Abbas on the night of Muzdalifa: Take our weak and our women, let them pray Fajr at Mina, and stone Jamrat al-Aqaba before the crush of people befalls them. He said: Ata used to do it after he grew old and weak. Abu Ja'far said: A group held that the weak may stone Jamrat al-Aqaba after dawn, adducing this hadith. Others disagreed and said: They should not stone it until the sun rises; if they stone it before that, it suffices them, but they have done wrong. They said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, did not mention in the hadith of Shu'ba his freedman that they stoned the Jamra at dawn by the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command to them to do so. It is possible they did that supposing it was its time, while its time in reality is otherwise. As for what Ata narrated from him, he did not mention therein the time of stoning Jamrat al-Aqaba: is it after sunrise or before that? The people of the first view also adduced for their view what follows."
"3722","3973 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, that Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to send ahead the weak of his family; they would stand at al-Mash'ar al-Haram and Muzdalifa by night, remembering Allah Almighty as seemed to them, then move before the imam stands and before he moves. Some of them would arrive at Mina for the Fajr prayer, some after that; when they arrived they stoned the Jamra. Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave license to those. Of the argument against them for the people of the other view was that he did not mention in this hadith from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave them license to stone Jamrat al-Aqaba then. The license he gave them may have been only moving from Muzdalifa by night. They also adduced on that what follows."
"3723","3974 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Salim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd Allah, freedman of Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with her, informed me that she said: O my son, has the moon set on the night of Jam'? - while she was praying, having alighted at Muzdalifa. He said: I said: No. She prayed a while, then said: O my son, has the moon set or gone? I said: Yes. She said: Then depart. We departed with her until she stoned the Jamra, then returned and prayed Fajr at her dwelling. I said to her: O dear mother, we have prayed in darkness! She said: No, O my son, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave permission to the women travelers. It may be he meant praying in darkness in moving from Muzdalifa, or he meant praying in darkness in stoning; she told him that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted them praying in darkness when he asked her about praying in darkness in that. Of the argument for those who held that their stoning time is after sunrise is what follows."
"3724","3975 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Uqba narrated to me, he said: Kurayb informed me, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command his women and his baggage on the morning of Jam' to pour forth at first dawn in darkness, and not to stone the Jamra except in daylight. So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them to pour forth at first dawn and not to stone until daylight. That indicates that the time in which he commanded them to stone does not begin at dawn, but begins at the daylight after that."
"3725","3976 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj informed me, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him with the baggage and said: Do not stone the Jimar until daylight. It may be that daylight is sunrise, or before that; so we looked into that."
"3726","3977 - For Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Banu Hashim: O sons of my brother, hasten before the crush of people, and do not stone the Jamra until the sun rises."
"3727","3978 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent ahead the weak of his family on the night of Jam'. He said: A man of them came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who moved his thigh and said: Do not stone Jamrat al-Aqaba until the sun rises."
"3728","3979 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us. (H). 3980 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us. (H). 3981 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us; they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from al-Hasan al-Urani, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent ahead the young boys of Banu Abd al-Muttalib from Jam' by night, tapping our thighs and saying: O my sons, do not stone Jamrat al-Aqaba until the sun rises."
"3729","3982 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Imran narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to me, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to me, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it, except that he said: He would take the upper arm of each of us."
"3730","3983 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from al-Hasan al-Urani, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: We poured forth from Jam'; when we reached Mina the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not stone Jamrat al-Aqaba until the sun rises. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified to them in this hadith the daylight time in which he commanded them to stone in the hadith in the preceding section, that it is after sunrise. This hadith is more deserving than the hadith of Shu'ba, freedman of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both; for this has come successively from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, with the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command to them upon what we mentioned. For pouring forth from Muzdalifa was licensed to the weak by night only lest the crush of people befall them at the time of their pouring forth; when they reach Mina they can stone Jamrat al-Aqaba after sunrise before people come, what the non-weak cannot when they come; for the non-weak come to them at the time they pour forth, before sunrise - thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them."
"3731","3984 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq. (H). 3985 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: We were standing with Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, at Jam'. He said: The people of ignorance would not pour forth until the sun rose, saying: Shine, Thabir. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposed them, pouring forth before sunrise."
"3732","3986 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. (H). 3987 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: We were standing with Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, at Jam'. He said: The people of ignorance would not pour forth until the sun rose, saying: Shine, Thabir, that we may hasten. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposed them, pouring forth before sunrise by about the time of a traveler's Fajr prayer. Since the non-weak pour forth from Muzdalifa before sunrise by this short span, the weak who preceded them to Mina can stone the Jamra after sunrise before the others come to them; so there was no meaning to licensing the weak to stone before sunrise, for license in the like of this is only for necessity, and here there is no necessity. Thereby what we mentioned of the hadith of Ibn Abbas which we narrated on delaying stoning Jamrat al-Aqaba until sunrise is established; and it is the view of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"3733","Chapter: Stoning Jamrat al-Aqaba on the night of sacrifice before dawn. 3988 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama informed me, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from Urwa, that the day of Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, came round to the Day of Sacrifice. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her on the night of Jam' to pour forth; she stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba and prayed Fajr at Mecca. Abu Ja'far said: A group held that stoning Jamrat al-Aqaba on the night of sacrifice before dawn is permissible, adducing this hadith. They said: She could not have prayed Fajr at Mecca unless her stoning of Jamrat al-Aqaba was before dawn, given the distance between the two places. Others disagreed and said: No one may stone it before dawn; whoever stones it before dawn is as one who did not stone, upon him to repeat stoning in stoning time, and if he does not, a blood (sacrifice) is upon him for that. Of the argument for them on that is that this hadith has been disputed from Hisham ibn Urwa; it was narrated from him upon what we mentioned, and narrated from him contrary to that."
"3734","3989 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Zaynab bint Abi Salama, from Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her on the Day of Sacrifice to meet him with the Fajr prayer at Mecca. So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her with what he commanded her of this on the Day of Sacrifice - that is for the Fajr prayer on the day after the Day of Sacrifice; this is contrary to the first hadith. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also hastened of his wives Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her; their going was to Mina, where they prayed Fajr, not heading then to Mecca. Of what was narrated on that is what follows."
"3735","3990 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aisha, that Sawda bint Zam'a sought permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to pray Fajr on the Day of Sacrifice at Mina, and he permitted her; she was a slow woman. I wish I had sought his permission as she did."
"3736","3991 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Salim ibn Shawwal, that he heard Umm Habiba saying: We used to depart in darkness from Muzdalifa to Mina in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So in this is that they would pour forth after dawn; this is further from them than in the first hadith. We mentioned in the preceding chapter in the hadith of Asma that she stoned then returned to her dwelling and prayed Fajr; Abd Allah said to her: We have prayed in darkness! She said: The Messenger of Allah gave license to the women travelers. She informed that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had licensed therein to the women travelers is pouring forth from Muzdalifa at the time they reach Mina in the state they may pray Fajr. Since the hadith of Hisham ibn Urwa is confused as we mentioned, acting upon what Hammad ibn Salama narrated is no more deserving than what Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated. Hammad ibn Salama mentioned in his hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by hastening Umm Salama to where he hastened her only because it was her day - that he might attain from her on that day of hers what a man attains from his wife; yet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of Sacrifice did not leave Mina, nor perform the visiting tawaf until night."
"3737","3992 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Tariq narrated to me, from Tawus; and Abu al-Zubayr, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed the visiting tawaf until night."
"3738","3993 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aisha, that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) poured forth at the end of his day. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not perform the visiting tawaf on the Day of Sacrifice until night, it was impossible he had need - for Umm Salama's, may Allah be pleased with her, presence at Mecca before that - of anything; for he wants her only because it is her day, to attain from her what a man attains from his wife, and that is not lawful to him from her except after tawaf. So the most resembling in our view - and Allah knows best - is that he commanded her to meet the Fajr prayer at Mecca on the morrow of the Day of Sacrifice, at a time when he would be lawful at Mecca. The Muslims knew the time of stoning Jamrat al-Aqaba on the Day of Sacrifice by the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3739","3994 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba on the Day of Sacrifice in the forenoon, and the rest after midday."
"3740","3995 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"3741","3996 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it. The Muslims thereby knew that the time in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned the Jimar is their time. We wanted to see whether he licensed the weak to stone before that or not. We found him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instructing the weak of Banu Hashim beforehand, when he sent them ahead to Mina: Do not stone the Jamra except after sunrise. We thereby knew that the weak were not licensed to precede the non-weak, and that the stoning time of all is one time, after sunrise. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for by way of reflection: we saw them agree that stoning Jamrat al-Aqaba for the second day after the Day of Sacrifice, at night before dawn, does not suffice him until his stoning of it is in its day. Reflection upon that is that likewise it is on the Day of Sacrifice: it may not be stoned except in its day, even though part of its day in that is better than part; on the second day, stoning in part of it is better than stoning in part of it. And this is the view of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"3742","3997 - I found in the book of Abd Allah ibn Suwayd, in his handwriting, from al-Athram, of what Abd Allah ibn Suwayd mentioned to us that al-Athram permitted it to whoever copied it from his handwriting, and Abd Allah ibn Suwayd permitted it to us from al-Athram - meaning Abu Bakr - he said: Abu Abd Allah - meaning Ahmad ibn Hanbal - may Allah have mercy on him, said to me: Abu Mu'awiya narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Zaynab, from Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her to meet him on the Day of Sacrifice at Mecca. None transmitted it with a chain but Abu Mu'awiya, and it is an error."
"3743","3998 - Ahmad said: And Waki' said, from Hisham, from his father, disconnected, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her to meet him at the Fajr prayer on the Day of Sacrifice at Mecca, or the like of this. He said: And this too is strange. Abu Ubayd Allah said: And what would the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) be doing at Mecca on the Day of Sacrifice? As though denying that. He said: I came to Yahya ibn Sa'id and asked him. He said: From Hisham, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her to meet - that is not his affair. He said: And between that is a difference: on the Day of Sacrifice the Fajr prayer is at al-Abtah. He said: And Yahya said to me: Ask Abd al-Rahman - that is Ibn Mahdi. I asked him and he said: Thus, from Sufyan, from Hisham, from his father: (meet). Then Abu Abd Allah said to me: May Allah have mercy on Yahya, how precise and strict he was! (He was a muhaddith), and he praised him with fine praise."
"3744","Chapter: A man leaves stoning Jamrat al-Aqaba on the Day of Sacrifice then stones it afterwards. 3999 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Qays narrated to me, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The shepherd pastures by day and stones by night. Abu Ja'far said: Abu Hanifa, may Allah have mercy on him, held that in this hadith is proof that night and day are one time for stoning. He said: (If a man leaves stoning Jamrat al-Aqaba on the Day of Sacrifice then stones it afterwards in the following night, nothing is upon him; if he does not stone it until morning of the morrow, he stones it with a blood (sacrifice) upon him for delaying it beyond its time, which is dawn of that day). Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them, disagreed, saying: When he remembers it in any of the stoning days, he stones it with nothing upon him besides that, neither blood nor otherwise; if he does not remember it until the stoning days pass, then remembers it without having stoned it, a blood is upon him for leaving it. Muhammad ibn al-Hasan adduced against Abu Hanifa - may Allah have mercy on him - on that what follows."
"3745","4000 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr informed me, from his father, from Abu al-Baddah, from Asim ibn Adi, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave license to the shepherds to take turns: they would stone in the early morning of the Day of Sacrifice, leave a night and a day, then stone the next day. So in this hadith is that they would stone in the early morning of the Day of Sacrifice, then leave a day and a night, then stone the next day. They would stone the stoning of the second day on the third day, without that necessitating a blood upon them, nor necessitating that the ruling of the third day for stoning for the second day differs from the ruling of the fourth day. In that is proof that whoever leaves stoning Jamrat al-Aqaba on the Day of Sacrifice and remembers it in any of the tashriq days stones it with nothing upon him. Then reflection on that also testifies to this view: we saw things done in Hajj for which all time is a time - of them running between al-Safa and al-Marwa, and the farewell tawaf; and of them things done in a specific time which is specifically their time - of them stoning the Jimar. As for what of these things all time is a time for: whenever done, nothing is upon its doer along with doing it, neither blood nor otherwise. What of them has a specific time of time: when not done in its time, a blood is due upon its leaver. What of them is done while its time remains: nothing is upon its doer but doing it; what of them is not done for lack of its time: a blood is due in its place. Since Jamrat al-Aqaba, when stoned the morrow of the Day of Sacrifice as make-up for the stoning of the Day of Sacrifice, has been stoned on a day of its time - were it not so, he would not be commanded to stone it, just as its leaver until after the passing of the tashriq days is not commanded to stone it afterwards. Since the second day of the days of sacrifice is a time for it, and we mentioned of what they agreed that what is done in its time of Hajj matters entails nothing upon its doer, so likewise this stoner of it, stoning it in its time, nothing is upon him in that but stoning it. If one says: we imposed the blood upon him only for leaving its stoning on the Day of Sacrifice and in the following night, for the wrongdoing from him in that. It is said to him: we saw the leaver of the farewell tawaf until he returns to his family, and the leaver of running between al-Safa and al-Marwa until he returns to his family, as wrongdoers, while you say: when they return and do what they left of that, their wrongdoing does not necessitate a blood upon them, for they did what they did of that in its time. Likewise the stoner on the second day of the days of Mina of Jamrat al-Aqaba, which was due upon him on the Day of Sacrifice, stoning it in its time - nothing is upon him in that but stoning it. This is the reflection in this chapter, and it is the view of Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"3746","Chapter: Talbiya - when does the pilgrim stop it? 4001 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Salama - that is al-Majishun - informed me, from Umar ibn Husayn, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Salama, from Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the morning of Arafa; some of us recited tahlil and some recited takbir. As for us, we recited takbir, while with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I said to him: How strange of you! Why did you not ask him what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do in that?"
"3747","4002 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiya al-Darir informed me, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Usama ibn Zayd, that he said: I was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the eve of Arafa; he would add nothing to takbir and tahlil, and when he found a gap he hastened."
"3748","4003 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik narrated to him, from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Thaqafi, that he asked Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, while both were going to Arafa - how did you do on this day with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: The one of us reciting tahlil would recite tahlil, none objected to him; and the one reciting takbir would recite takbir, none objected to him."
"3749","4004 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to me, he said: I met Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, while we were going from Mina to Arafat. I said to him: How did you do on this morning? He said: I shall tell you; I was in a company among whom was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The one reciting tahlil would recite tahlil, none objected to him; and the one reciting takbir would recite takbir, none objected to him; and I do not affirm what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did of that."
"3750","4005 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'a narrated to me, from Abu al-Zubayr, he said: I asked Jabir ibn Abd Allah about tahlil on the day of Arafa. He said: We would recite tahlil short of Arafa and recite takbir on the day of Arafa. Abu Ja'far said: A group held that the pilgrim does not recite talbiya at Arafa, differing over his stopping talbiya when it should be: some said when he heads to Arafat; some said when he stands at Arafat; adducing these reports. Others disagreed and said: Rather, the pilgrim recites talbiya until he stones Jamrat al-Aqaba. They said: You have no proof in these reports you adduced against us; for mentioned in them is only that some recited takbir and some recited tahlil - that does not preclude their doing that while entitled to recite talbiya; for the pilgrim - before the day of Arafa - may recite takbir, may recite tahlil, and may recite talbiya; his takbir and tahlil did not preclude talbiya. Likewise what you mentioned of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tahlil and takbir on the day of Arafa does not preclude talbiya. Successive reports came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his talbiya after Arafa until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba. Of that is what follows."
"3751","4006 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Aban ibn Salih, from Ikrima, he said: I stood with al-Husayn ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both; he would recite talbiya until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba. I said: O Abu Abd Allah, what is this? He said: My father used to do that, and told me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do that. He said: I returned to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and told him. Abd Allah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: He spoke the truth. My brother al-Fadl told me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited talbiya until he finished the first of them, and he was his riding companion."
"3752","4007 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from al-Fadl, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited talbiya until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba."
"3753","4008 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim ibn Malik, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl, he said: I was riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and he mentioned the like of it."
"3754","4009 - Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us. (H). 4010 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us; they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited talbiya until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba."
"3755","4011 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qays, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from al-Fadl, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"3756","4012 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed me, from Thuwayr, from his father, he said: I performed Hajj with Abd Allah; he kept reciting talbiya until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba. He said: He did not hear people reciting talbiya on the eve of Arafa, and said: O people, have you forgotten?! By Him in whose Hand is my soul, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting talbiya until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba."
"3757","4013 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam informed me, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: I performed Hajj with Abd Allah; when he poured forth to Jam' he began reciting talbiya. An Arab Bedouin spoke, and Abd Allah said: Have people forgotten or gone astray?! Then he recited talbiya until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba."
"3758","4014 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Humayd al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Abi Dhuhab, from Mujahid, from Abd Allah ibn Sakhabra, he said: Abd Allah recited talbiya while heading to Arafat. People said: Who is this Bedouin? Abd Allah turned to me and said: Have people gone astray or forgotten?! By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept reciting talbiya until he stoned the Jamra, except that he would mix that with tahlil or takbir."
"3759","4015 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Abi Dhuhab, from Mujahid al-Makki, from Ibn Sakhabra, he said: I went in the morning with Ibn Mas'ud on the morning of Jam' while he recited talbiya. Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Have people gone astray or forgotten?! I testify: we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he recited talbiya until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba."
"3760","4016 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Husayn, from Kathir ibn Mudrik, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud said, while we were at Jam': I heard the one upon whom Surat al-Baqara was revealed reciting talbiya in this place: Here I am, O Allah, here I am."
"3761","4017 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Abd al-Awwal al-Ahwal narrated to us; they said: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Husayn - then he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"3762","4018 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: I heard Yunus, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Usama ibn Zayd was riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Arafa to Muzdalifa, then he mounted al-Fadl ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, behind him from Muzdalifa to Mina; both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept reciting talbiya until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba. These reports came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he recited talbiya until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba, sound in transmission; what we presented at the beginning of this chapter does not contradict them in our view, for what we explained. Al-Fadl ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he moved from Arafa, and saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Arafa reciting talbiya then and afterwards. We mentioned from Usama that he said: I was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Arafa, and he would add nothing to tahlil and takbir. So his talbiya at Arafa indicates that he was entitled to recite talbiya also at Arafa, and that his takbir and tahlil at Arafa were as entitled before it, not making tahlil and takbir in place of talbiya. Do you not see the statement of Abd Allah in the hadith of Mujahid: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited talbiya until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba, except that he would sometimes mix that with takbir and tahlil. So Abd Allah informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would mix takbir with tahlil; tahlil and takbir do not indicate no talbiya in its time, while talbiya in that time indicates that time was its talbiya time. It is established by authenticating these reports that talbiya time extends until he stones Jamrat al-Aqaba on the Day of Sacrifice. If one says: there has been narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contrary to what you authenticated these reports upon, and he mentioned what follows."
"3763","4019 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ya'qub informed me, from Mus'ab ibn Thabit, from his uncle Amir ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, would recite tahlil on the day of Arafa until he set out."
"3764","4020 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that she would leave talbiya when she set out to the standing place. Of the argument against them for the people of the other view is that al-Qasim did not inform in his hadith which we narrated from him, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said talbiya stops before standing at Arafa. He only informed of her act, saying: she would leave talbiya when she set out to the standing place. It is possible she did that, not because talbiya time had ended, but because she takes up other remembrance of takbir and tahlil, as she may do that before the day of Arafa also, without that proving the stoppage of talbiya and the exit of its time. Likewise what Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, on that also, and it is like this. 4020M - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, he said: I performed Hajj with al-Aswad. When it was the day of Arafa and Ibn al-Zubayr addressed at Arafa, and he did not hear him recite talbiya, al-Aswad went up to him and said: What prevents you from reciting talbiya? He said: Does a man recite talbiya when in the like of this station of yours? Al-Aswad said: Yes; I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, reciting talbiya in the like of this station of yours, then he kept reciting talbiya until his camel left the standing place. He said: So Ibn al-Zubayr recited talbiya."
"3765","4021 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, from Sakhr ibn Juwayriya, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, he said: I heard Ibn al-Zubayr addressing on the day of Arafa, saying: (This is a day of glorification, takbir and tahlil; so glorify and recite takbir. Al-Aswad hastened to me - inciting people - until he went up to him while on the pulpit, and said: I testify against Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he recited talbiya upon the pulpit on this day). Ibn al-Zubayr said: (Here I am, O Allah, here I am). Do you not see that when al-Aswad told Ibn al-Zubayr of Umar's, may Allah be pleased with him, talbiya on the like of that day of his, he accepted it and acted upon it, reciting talbiya, without Ibn al-Zubayr saying to him: (I saw Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, not reciting talbiya on this day), upon what Amir ibn Abd Allah narrated from his father from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. Rather, Ibn al-Zubayr only witnessed Umar's leaving talbiya that day, while Umar did not tell him that leaving was for the exit of talbiya time. So that was with Ibn al-Zubayr as for the exit of talbiya time. When al-Aswad told him of Umar's, may Allah be pleased with him, talbiya that day, Ibn al-Zubayr knew that the time in which Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, had not recited talbiya is a time for talbiya, and that Umar's leaving was for other than the exit of talbiya time; Ibn al-Zubayr himself had supposed it was for the exit of talbiya time, and it is not so. So he recited talbiya, deeming what al-Aswad told him of Umar's talbiya more deserving than what he himself saw of his leaving talbiya."
"3766","4022 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid informed me, from Wabara, he said: al-Aswad ibn Yazid went up to Ibn al-Zubayr while on the pulpit on the day of Arafa, whispered something to him; then al-Aswad descended and Ibn al-Zubayr recited talbiya. People supposed al-Aswad ordered him that."
"3767","4023 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qays ibn Sa'd, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, reciting talbiya in the morning at Muzdalifa."
"3768","4024 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: I was with Abd Allah at Arafa, and Abd Allah recited talbiya; Abd Allah kept reciting talbiya until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqaba. A man said: Who is this reciting talbiya in this place? He said: And Abd Allah said in his talbiya something I heard from no one: (Here I am, to the number of dust). So in these reports is that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, would recite talbiya at Arafa while upon the pulpit, and that Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr did that after him when al-Aswad told him of it from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, with no one of the people of the horizons denying it - that is consensus and proof. And here Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, did that. It is established by the act of those we mentioned, agreeing with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his doing that, that talbiya in Hajj does not stop until Jamrat al-Aqaba is stoned; and it is the view of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"3769","Chapter: Clothing and perfume - when do they become lawful for the muhrim? 4025 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Lahi'a informed me, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, from Urwa, from Judama bint Wahb, sister of Ukasha ibn Wahb, that Ukasha ibn Wahb, a Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and I think another with him - came to her when the sun set on the Day of Sacrifice, casting off their shirts. She said: What is with you both? They said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has not poured forth from here, let him cast off his garments; and they had perfumed themselves and worn garments."
"3770","4026 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'a narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwa, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan, she said: Ukasha ibn Mihsan and another entered upon me in my house on the evening of the Day of Sacrifice, removing their garments and leaving perfume. I said: What is with you both? They said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: Whoever has not poured forth to the House by this evening, let him leave garments and perfume. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to this, saying: Clothing and perfume become lawful to no one until women become lawful to him, when he performs the visiting tawaf; adducing this hadith. Others disagreed, saying: When he stones and shaves, clothing becomes lawful to him. They differed over perfume: some said its ruling is the ruling of clothing, becoming lawful as clothing becomes lawful; others said its ruling is the ruling of intercourse, not becoming lawful until intercourse becomes lawful. They adduced on that what follows."
"3771","4027 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Al-Hajjaj ibn Artah informed us, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you have stoned and shaved, then perfume, clothes, and everything except women has become lawful for you."
"3772","4028 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Al-Hajjaj ibn Artah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Amrah, from Aishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"3773","4029 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, that al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrated to him, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for his release from ihram when he released himself before circumambulating the House."
"3774","4030 - Usamah said: And Abu Bakr ibn Hazm narrated to me, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"3775","4031 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"3776","4032 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Aflah ibn Humayd narrated to us, from al-Qasim, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"3777","4033 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us. (Hah) 4034 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"3778","4035 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Al-Qasim narrated to me, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"3779","4036 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"3780","4037 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it. So this is Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about perfuming after the stoning and shaving before the visit circumambulation, with what we have mentioned. And the hadith of Ibn Lahi'ah with which we began mentioning in this chapter contradicts that, so these are more worthy, because with them is the corroboration and soundness of transmission which others do not have like it. Then it has also been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of that, except that he added to it another point."
"3781","4038 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us. (Hah) 4039 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from al-Hasan al-Urani, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: When you have stoned the Jamrah, then everything except women has become lawful for you. A man said to him: And perfume? He said: As for me, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anointing his head with musk; so is that perfume? So in this hadith from the statement of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, is what we mentioned of the permissibility of everything except women when the Jamrah has been stoned, and he does not mention shaving in that. And in it is that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anointing his head with musk, and he did not report the time in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did that. And it is possible that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before shaving, and possible after it, except that the worthiest thing for us is to carry that upon what agrees with what we mentioned from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, not upon what contradicts it. So what he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing of that was after his stoning the Jamrah and his shaving, according to what is in the hadith of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her. Then Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said, exercising his opinion: when he has stoned, then by his stoning shaving has become lawful for him, and wearing clothes and perfuming has become lawful for him. And this is a point open to examination, and that is that ihram prohibits shaving the head, clothes, and perfume, so it is possible that when shaving the head becomes lawful these things become lawful, and possible they do not become lawful until the shaving takes place. So we considered that, and we saw the umrah performer, whom his ihram in his umrah prohibits what his ihram in his hajj prohibits. Then when we see him, when he has circumambulated the House and run between al-Safa and al-Marwah, it has become lawful for him to shave, but women, perfume, and clothes do not become lawful for him until he shaves. So since the sanctity of the umrah stands while it has become lawful for him to shave, and his becoming lawful to shave is not in the ruling of one for whom what is besides that of clothes and perfume has become lawful, so likewise in hajj, what has become lawful for him of shaving in it does not make lawful for him anything besides it of what was prohibited to him by it until he shaves, by analogy and examination upon what they agreed upon in umrah. Then we returned to the examination between these two groups altogether and the people of the first view who went to the hadith of Ukashah. And we saw the man before he enters ihram, for whom women, perfume, clothes, hunting, shaving, and the rest of the things prohibited to him by ihram are lawful, then when he enters ihram, all of that becomes prohibited to him by one cause, which is ihram. So it was possible that just as they became prohibited to him by one cause, he also becomes released from them by one cause, and possible he becomes released from them by different things, one release after another. So we considered that, and we saw them agree that when he has stoned, shaving has become lawful for him; this is of what there is no disagreement among the Muslims, and they agreed that intercourse is prohibited to him in its first state, so it is established that he becomes released from what was prohibited to him by one cause by different causes. So by this reason which we mentioned the objection falls. So when it is established that shaving becomes lawful for him when he has stoned, and that it is permissible for him after shaving his head to shave what he wishes of the hair of his body and to clip his nails, we wanted to examine: is the ruling of clothes the ruling of that, or is its ruling the ruling of intercourse, so it does not become lawful until intercourse becomes lawful? So we considered that, and we saw the one in ihram for hajj, if he has intercourse before standing at Arafah, his hajj is spoiled, and we saw him, if he shaves his hair or clips his nails, a ransom becomes due upon him for that, but his hajj is not spoiled by that. And we saw that if he wears clothes before standing at Arafah, his ihram is not spoiled upon him by that, and a ransom becomes due upon him for that. So the ruling of clothes before Arafah was like the ruling of cutting hair and nails, not like the ruling of intercourse. So the examination upon that is that its ruling also after the stoning and shaving be like their ruling, not like the ruling of intercourse. So this is the examination in that. Then if a speaker says: but we saw kissing prohibited to the one in ihram after he shaves, while before standing at Arafah it is in the ruling of clothes, not in the ruling of intercourse, so why would clothes after shaving not also be like it? It is said to him: clothes resemble shaving more than kissing; because kissing is one of the causes of intercourse, and its ruling is its ruling, it becomes lawful where it becomes lawful and prohibited where it becomes prohibited, in examining all things. And shaving and clothes are not of the causes of intercourse, rather they are of the causes of grooming the body, so the ruling of each of them by the ruling of its companion is more resembling than its ruling by kissing. So it is established by what we mentioned that there is no harm in clothes after the stoning and shaving. And the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that after him."
"3782","4040 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah Musa ibn Mas'ud narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Ibn Umar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When you have shaved and stoned, then everything except women and perfume has become lawful for you."
"3783","4041 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, the same as it."
"3784","4042 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed the people at Arafah, and he mentioned the same as it."
"3785","4043 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj and Musa, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he used to cut his nails, mustache, and beard, meaning before visiting. So this is Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who permitted them when they stoned and shaved everything except women and perfume, and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Ibn al-Zubayr opposed him in perfume specifically. As for Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and Ibn Abbas, we have narrated that from them in what preceded of this chapter. And as for Ibn al-Zubayr."
"3786","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr saying: When he has stoned the Greater Jamrah, then what was prohibited to him has become lawful for him except women, until he circumambulates the House. And there has also been narrated from Ibn Umar what indicates this as well."
"3787","4044 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Tawus, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said, and he mentioned the same as what we narrated from him in the section before this. He said: Then Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I used to perfume the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he stoned the Jamrat al-Aqabah before he went to pour forth. So the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more worthy to be taken than the sunnah of Umar. And examination after that in this also indicates that, because the ruling of perfume resembles the ruling of clothes more than its ruling resembles the ruling of intercourse, for what we have explained of what preceded in this chapter. And this is the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah be pleased with them, and that has also been narrated from a group of the Followers."
"3788","4045 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Aflah ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Hazm, he said: Sulayman ibn Abd al-Malik invited us on the Day of Sacrifice; he sent to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, Salim ibn Abdullah, Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn Umar, Kharijah ibn Zayd, and Ibn Shihab, and asked them about perfume on this day before pouring forth. They said: Will you perfume yourself, O Commander of the Believers? Except that Abdullah ibn Abdullah said: Abdullah ibn Umar was a man who had seen Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when he stoned the Jamrat al-Aqabah he made his camel kneel, sacrificed, shaved, then went on his way and poured forth to the House."
"3789","4046 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, and Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, that al-Walid ibn Abd al-Malik asked Salim ibn Abdullah and Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, after he had stoned the Jamrat al-Aqabah and shaved, about perfume; so Salim forbade him, and Kharijah permitted him."
"3790","Chapter 26: A woman menstruates after she has circumambulated for the visit before she circumambulates for departure. 4047 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Abu Awanah, from Ya'la ibn Ata, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Zajjaj, from al-Harith ibn Aws al-Thaqafi, he said: I asked Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, about a woman who menstruates before she circumambulates. He said: She makes her last contact the circumambulation. He said: Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to me when I asked him. Then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me: I see your repeating the hadith; you asked me about something I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about, as if to oppose him."
"3791","4048 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: from al-Harith ibn Abdullah ibn Aws."
"3792","4049 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of the hadith of Ibn Marzuq in its chain and text, except that he said: I asked Umar about a woman who circumambulates the House then menstruates. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to this hadith and said: It is not lawful for anyone to depart until he circumambulates the departure circumambulation, and they did not excuse in that a menstruating woman for her menstruation. And others opposed them in that and said: She may depart even if she has not circumambulated the House, and they excused her for menstruation; this when she has already circumambulated the visit circumambulation before that. And they argued in that with what"
"3793","4050 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Sulayman, who is Ibn Abi Muslim al-Ahwal, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The people used to depart from every direction. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let no one depart until his last contact is the circumambulation of the House."
"3794","4051 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him: The people were commanded that their last contact be with the House, except that it has been lightened for the menstruating woman."
"3795","4052 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from al-Hasan ibn Muslim, from Tawus, he said: Zayd ibn Thabit said to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him: Are you the one who gives fatwa to the menstruating woman to leave before her last contact is the circumambulation of the House? He said: Yes. He said: Do not do so. He said: Ask so-and-so the Ansari woman: did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command her to leave? So he asked the woman, then returned to him and said: I see you have spoken the truth."
"3796","4053 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Razin narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Ikrimah, that Zayd ibn Thabit and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, differed about a woman who menstruates after she has circumambulated the House on the Day of Sacrifice. Zayd said: Her last contact is the circumambulation of the House, and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: She departs whenever she wishes. The Ansar said: We will not follow you, O Ibn Abbas, while you oppose Zayd. He said: Ask your companion Umm Sulaym. So they asked her and she said: I menstruated after I poured forth on the Day of Sacrifice, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to depart; and Safiyyah menstruated, so Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said to her: Loss be to you, you have detained our people. That was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he commanded her to depart."
"3797","4054 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Anas, from Umm Sulaym, that she menstruated after she poured forth on the Day of Sacrifice, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her to depart."
"3798","4055 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to depart, he saw Safiyyah at the door of her tent gloomy and grieved, and she had menstruated. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will indeed detain us; had you poured forth on the Day of Sacrifice? She said: Yes. He said: Then depart."
"3799","4056 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"3800","4057 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus al-Taghlibi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the like of its meaning."
"3801","4058 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman and Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3802","4059 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab and Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to me, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3803","4060 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"3804","4061 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3805","4062 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that Safiyyah bint Huyay, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), menstruated, and she mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Is she detaining us? I said: She has already poured forth. He said: Then no."
"3806","4063 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Aflah narrated to us, from al-Qasim, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3807","4064 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3808","4065 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah and Sulayman, uncle of Ibn Abi Najih, from Tawus, he said: Ibn Umar for nearly two years forbade the menstruating woman to depart until her last contact be with the House. Then he said: I was told that he has permitted women."
"3809","4066 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Tawus al-Yamani informed me that he heard Abdullah ibn Umar being asked about detaining women from circumambulating the House when they menstruate before departure, while they had poured forth on the Day of Sacrifice. He said: Aishah used to mention from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a concession for women, and that was a year before the death of Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"3810","4067 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to permit the menstruating woman, when she had poured forth, to depart. Tawus said: And I heard Ibn Umar saying: She does not depart, then I heard him afterwards saying: She departs; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted them."
"3811","4068 - Abu Ayyub Abdullah ibn Ayyub, known as Ibn Khalaf al-Tabarani, narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Muhammad al-Naqid narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Whoever performs hajj of this House, let his last contact be the circumambulation of the House, except the menstruating women; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted them. So these reports are established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the menstruating woman may depart before she circumambulates the departure circumambulation when she has already circumambulated the visit circumambulation before that in purity. And a group of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who had said otherwise returned to that: Zayd ibn Thabit and Ibn Umar, and they made what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the concession in that for the menstruating woman a concession and an exclusion by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of her ruling from the ruling of the rest of the people in what was obligated upon them of that. So by that the abrogation of these reports is established, of the hadith of al-Harith ibn Aws and what Umar went to of that. And what we have explained is the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"3812","Chapter 27: One who advances one rite of his hajj before another rite. 4069 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Masruq al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Abi Rabi'ah, from Zayd ibn Ali, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I poured forth before I shaved. He said: Shave, and no harm. And another came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I sacrificed before I stoned. He said: Stone, and no harm. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about circumambulation before shaving, and he said: Shave, and no harm. So it bears that that be a permission from him for circumambulation before shaving, and latitude from him in that, so he made for the pilgrim to advance whichever of these two he wishes before its companion. And in it also is that another came to him and said: I sacrificed before I stoned, and he said: Stone, and no harm. So that also bears what we mentioned in his answer in the first question. And there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something of that."
"3813","4070 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about one who shaved before he sacrificed, or sacrificed before he shaved, and he said: No harm, no harm."
"3814","4071 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Al-Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that it was said to him on the Day of Sacrifice while he was at Mina about sacrifice, shaving, stoning, advancing, and delaying, and he said: No harm."
"3815","4072 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Hibban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: He was not asked on that day about one who advanced something before something except that he said: No harm, no harm. So that bears what the first hadith bears. And there has been narrated from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, something of that."
"3816","4073 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qays, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, I sacrificed before I stoned. He said: Stone, and no harm. Another said: O Messenger of Allah, I shaved before I sacrificed. He said: Sacrifice, and no harm. Another said: O Messenger of Allah, I circumambulated the House before I sacrificed. He said: Sacrifice, and no harm. So this too is like what is before it, and the discussion in it is like the discussion in what is before it. And there has been narrated from Usamah ibn Sharik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something of that."
"3817","4074 - Ahmad ibn al-Hasan, who is Ibn al-Qasim al-Kufi, narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, he said: We performed hajj with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he was asked about one who shaved before he sacrificed, or sacrificed before he shaved, and he said: No harm. Then when they pressed him much, he said: O people, hardship has been lifted, except one who seizes something from his brother unjustly; that is hardship. So this too is like what is before it. And it also bears that his statement No harm is upon sin, i.e., there is no harm upon you in what you did of this, because you did it out of your ignorance of it, not deliberately in opposition to the sunnah, so there is no blame upon you in that. And there has been narrated from that, explained and expounded, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3818","4075 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Thabit Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, I think from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Zayd ibn Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Ali, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man asked him in his hajj and said: I stoned and poured forth, and forgot and did not shave. He said: Then shave, and no harm. Then another man came to him and said: I stoned and shaved, and forgot to sacrifice. He said: Then sacrifice, and no harm."
"3819","4076 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik and Yunus narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Isa ibn Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood in the Farewell Hajj for the people asking him. A man came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I did not realize and shaved before I sacrificed, and he said: Sacrifice, and no harm. Another came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I did not realize and sacrificed before I stoned. He said: Stone, and no harm. He said: He was not asked on that day about anything advanced or delayed except that he said: Do, and no harm."
"3820","4077 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Isa ibn Talhah, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: A man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I shaved before I sacrificed. He said: Sacrifice, and no harm. Another said: I sacrificed before I stoned. He said: Stone, and no harm."
"3821","4078 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd informed me that Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrated to him that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same as it, meaning that he stood for the people in the year of the Farewell Hajj while they asked him; a man came and said: I did not realize, so I sacrificed before I stoned. He said: Stone, and no harm. Another said: O Messenger of Allah, I did not realize and shaved before I sacrificed. He said: Sacrifice, and no harm. He said: He was not asked about anything advanced or delayed except that he said: Do, and no harm. So what we mentioned indicates that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only lifted hardship from them in that for forgetfulness, not that he permitted that to them so that it be permissible for them to do that deliberately. And Abu Sa'id al-Khudri narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that as well."
"3822","4079 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Ali narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Ubadah ibn Nusayy, he said: Abu Zubayd narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked, while he was between the two Jamrahs, about a man who shaved before he stoned. He said: No harm; and about a man who sacrificed before he stoned. He said: No harm. Then he said: O servants of Allah, Allah the Mighty and Majestic has laid down hardship and straitness, and learn your rites, for they are of your religion. Do you not see that he commanded them to learn their rites, because they did not know them well, so that indicates that the hardship and straitness which Allah lifted from them is for their ignorance of the matter of their rites, not for other than that. And there has been narrated in the hadith of Usamah ibn Sharik which we mentioned in what preceded of this chapter what indicates this meaning as well."
"3823","4080 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, that the Bedouins asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about things, then said: Is there harm upon us in such? And is there harm upon us in such? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic has lifted hardship from His servants, except one who seizes something from his brother wronged; that is the one who is in hardship and perished. Do you not see that those asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were only Bedouins with no knowledge of the rites of hajj? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered them with his statement No harm, upon permission from him to them of advancing in that and delaying in what they advanced of that and delayed. Then he said to them what Abu Sa'id mentioned in his hadith: And learn your rites. Then there has come from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, what indicates this meaning as well."
"3824","4081 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Whoever advances something of his hajj or delays it, let him shed blood for that."
"3825","4082 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, the same as it. So this is Ibn Abbas obligating upon one who advances something of his rites or delays it blood, while he is one of those who narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he was not asked on that day about anything advanced or delayed of the matter of hajj except that he said: No harm. So the meaning of that with him was not the meaning of permission in advancing what they advanced, nor in delaying what they delayed of what we mentioned, since he obligated in that blood. But the meaning of that with him was upon that what they did in the hajj of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon their ignorance of the ruling in it, how it is. So he excused them for their ignorance, and commanded them in the future to learn their rites. And the people spoke after this about the qarin when he shaves before he sacrifices. Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Upon him is blood. And Zufar said: Upon him are two bloods. And Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both, said: Nothing is upon him, and they argued in that with the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to those who asked him about that upon what we narrated in the preceding reports, and with his answer to them that there is no harm upon them in that. And of the proof upon them both in that for Abu Hanifah and Zufar, may Allah have mercy on them both, is what we mentioned of explaining the meanings of these reports. And another proof, which is that the asker of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not known, whether he was a qarin, a mufrid, or a mutamatti. If he was a mufrid, then Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, and Zufar do not deny that nothing is due upon him in that, because that sacrifice before which he advanced shaving is a sacrifice not obligatory, but it was better for him to advance sacrifice before shaving, yet when he advances shaving it suffices him, and nothing is upon him. And if he was a qarin or mutamatti, then the answer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that was upon what we mentioned. We have mentioned from Ibn Abbas about advancing in hajj and delaying that in it is blood, and that the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): No harm, does not repel that. So since the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that: No harm, does not negate with Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, the obligation of blood, so likewise it does not negate it with Abu Hanifah and Zufar, may Allah have mercy on them both. And the qarin, his sacrifice is an obligatory sacrifice upon him by which he releases himself. So we wanted to examine the things by which the pilgrim releases himself when he delays them until he releases himself, how is their ruling? We found Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: And do not shave your heads until the sacrificial animal reaches its place. So the muhsar shaves after the sacrificial animal reaches its place, and releases himself by that, and if he shaves before its reaching its place, blood becomes due upon him, and this is consensus. So the examination upon that is that likewise the qarin, when he advances shaving before the sacrifice by which he releases himself, blood is upon him, by analogy and examination upon what we mentioned of that. So by this what Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both, went to is nullified, and what Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, said, or what Zufar, may Allah have mercy on him, said, is established. So we examined in that, and this qarin has shaved his head at a time when shaving is prohibited to him, while he is in the sanctity of a hajj and in the sanctity of an umrah. And the qarin, whatever he commits in his qiran of what, had he committed it while in a single hajj or single umrah, blood would be due upon him, then when he commits it while a qarin, two bloods become due upon him, so it bore that his shaving also before its time would obligate upon him two bloods as well, as Zufar said. So we examined in that, and we found the things that obligate upon the qarin two bloods in what he commits in his qiran are the things which, had he committed them while in the sanctity of a hajj or in the sanctity of an umrah, blood would be due upon him. So when he commits them in both their sanctities, two bloods become due upon him, like intercourse and what resembles it. And his shaving before he sacrifices was not prohibited to him by cause of umrah specifically, nor by cause of hajj specifically, rather it became due upon him by cause of both, and by the sanctity of combining them, not by the sanctity of hajj specifically, nor by the sanctity of umrah specifically. So we wanted to examine the ruling of what becomes due by combining, is it two things or one thing? We examined in that, and we found the man, when he enters ihram for a single hajj or single umrah, nothing becomes due upon him, and when he combines them both, there becomes due upon him for his combining them something that would not be due upon him in singling each of them, so that thing was one blood. So the examination upon that is that likewise shaving before sacrifice, which combining umrah and hajj prohibited, while neither of them had it been single would prohibit it, that what becomes due by it in it is one blood. So the basis of what becomes due upon the qarin in his violating the sanctity in his qiran is that we examine what of those sanctities is prohibited by hajj specifically and by umrah specifically. Then when both are combined, that sanctity is prohibited for two different things, so upon one who violates it are two expiations. And every sanctity which hajj alone does not prohibit, nor umrah alone, rather combining them prohibits it, then when it is violated, upon the one who violated it is one blood, because he violated a sanctity prohibited to him by one cause. So this is the examination in this chapter, and it is the statement of Abu Hanifah, and by it we take."
"3826","Chapter 28: The Makkan wanting umrah, from where should he enter ihram for it. 4083 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, Amr ibn Aws informed him, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr informed me, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to mount Aishah behind me to al-Tan'im and make her perform umrah."
"3827","4084 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman informed us, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from Hafsah bint Abd al-Rahman, from her father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr: Mount your sister behind you and make her perform umrah from al-Tan'im; when you descend with her from the hill, command her to enter ihram, for it is an accepted umrah. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that the umrah of one who is in Makkah has no appointed place for it other than al-Tan'im, and they made al-Tan'im specifically a mawaqit for the umrah of the people of Makkah, and said: They should not pass beyond it, just as others should not pass a mawaqit of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed for him while wanting ihram except in ihram. And others opposed them in that and said: The appointed place of the people of Makkah from which they enter ihram for umrah is the Hill, so from whichever part of the Hill they enter ihram for it, that suffices them, and al-Tan'im and other than it of the Hill, with them, in that are alike. And of the proof for them in that was that it is possible the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) aimed at al-Tan'im in that because it was the nearest Hill from her, not because other than it of the Hill is not in that like it. And it also bears that he intended by it appointing for the people of Makkah in umrah, and that they not pass beyond it for it to other than it. So we examined in that, and lo:"
"3828","4085 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir Salih ibn Rustum narrated to us, from Abu Mulaykah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me at Sarif while I was weeping. He said: What is that? I said: I have menstruated. He said: Do not weep; do what the pilgrim does. So we came to Makkah, then we came to Mina, then we went in the morning to Arafah, then we stoned the Jamrah those days, then when it was the day of departure he departed and alighted at al-Hasbah. She said: By Allah, he alighted at it only for my sake, then he commanded Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr and said: Carry your sister and take her out of the Haram. She said: By Allah, he did not mention al-Ji'irranah nor al-Tan'im; so she should enter ihram for umrah, for the nearest of it to us from the Haram was al-Tan'im, so I entered ihram for umrah, we circumambulated the House and ran between al-Safa and al-Marwah, then we came to him and he departed. So Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he wanted to make her perform umrah, aimed only at the Hill, not at a specific place of it in particular, and that Abd al-Rahman only aimed with her at al-Tan'im because it was the nearest Hill to them, not for a meaning in it distinguishing it from the rest of the Hill. So by that it is established that the appointed place of the people of Makkah for their umrah is the Hill, and that al-Tan'im in that and other than it are alike, and all this is the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"3829","Chapter 29: The sacrificial animal barred from the Haram, should it be slaughtered outside the Haram or not? 4086 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, from his father, from Siba ibn Thabit, from Umm Kurz, she said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Hudaybiyah asking him about the meat of the sacrificial animals. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that when the sacrificial animal is barred from the Haram, it is slaughtered outside the Haram, and they argued in that with this hadith, and said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slaughtered the sacrificial animal at al-Hudaybiyah when barred from the Haram, that indicates that one prevented from bringing his sacrificial animal into the Haram may slaughter it outside the Haram. And others opposed them in that and said: Slaughtering the sacrificial animal is not permissible except in the Haram. And of their proof in that was the statement of Allah the Mighty and Majestic: As a sacrificial animal reaching the Ka'bah. So the sacrificial animal was made by Allah the Mighty and Majestic what reaches the Ka'bah, so it is like fasting which Allah the Mighty and Majestic made consecutive in the expiation of zihar and the expiation of killing, so other than consecutive is not permissible, even if the one upon whom it is due cannot bring it consecutively, yet necessity does not permit him to fast it separately. So likewise the sacrificial animal described with reaching the Ka'bah, it does not suffice the one upon whom it is as such, even if barred from reaching the Ka'bah out of necessity, to slaughter it in other than that. And of the proof for them upon the people of the first view in the slaughter by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that sacrificial animal which he slaughtered at al-Hudaybiyah when barred from the Haram, and his giving its meat in charity at Qudayd, is that a group claimed that his slaughtering it was in the Haram."
"3830","4087 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Mikhwal ibn Ibrahim ibn Mikhwal ibn Rashid narrated to us, from Israel, from Majza'ah ibn Zahir, from Najiyah ibn Jundub al-Aslami, from his father, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the sacrificial animal was barred, and said: O Messenger of Allah, send the sacrificial animal with me so I slaughter it in the Haram. He said: And how will you take it? I said: I will take it through valleys where they cannot overpower me in them, so he sent it with me until I slaughtered it in the Haram. So this hadith indicates that the sacrificial animal of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was slaughtered in the Haram. And others said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at al-Hudaybiyah, while he could enter the Haram. They said: And he was barred only from the House, and they argued in that with what"
"3831","4088 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Bishr al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Za'idah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from al-Miswar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at al-Hudaybiyah, his tent in the Hill and his prayer place in the Haram. So by what we mentioned it is established that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not barred from the Haram, and that he could reach part of it. And it is not permissible in the statement of any of the scholars for one able to enter part of the Haram to slaughter his sacrificial animal short of the Haram. So when it is established by the hadith we mentioned that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) could reach part of the Haram, it becomes impossible that the slaughter of the sacrificial animal be outside the Haram; because the one who permits slaughter of the sacrificial animal outside the Haram only permits it in the state of being barred from the Haram, not in the state of ability to enter it. So by what we mentioned it is negated that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slaughtered the sacrificial animal outside the Haram, and this is the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. And a group argued for permitting slaughter of the sacrificial animal outside the Haram with what"
"3832","4089 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ya'qub ibn Khalid, from Abu Asma, freedman of Abdullah ibn Ja'far, he said: I went out with Uthman and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, and al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, complained at al-Suqya while in ihram, and he was struck by birsam, so he motioned to his head and Ali shaved his head, and slaughtered a camel for him and fed the people of the water."
"3833","4090 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it, except that he did not mention Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, and that al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, was in ihram. So they argued with this hadith, because in it is that Ali slaughtered the camel short of the Haram. So of the proof upon them in that was that they do not permit one not barred from the Haram to slaughter outside the Haram, but they differ only when he is barred from it. So what we mentioned indicates that when Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, slaughtered in this hadith outside the Haram while reaching the Haram, he did not intend by it the sacrificial animal, but he intended by it another meaning of charity upon the people of that water and seeking nearness to Allah the Exalted by that, besides that there is not in the hadith that he intended by it the sacrificial animal. So just as it is permissible for one who carries it upon that it is a sacrificial animal what he carried it upon of that, so likewise it is permissible for one who carries it upon that it is not a sacrificial animal what he carried it upon of that. And we began with examination in that, and mentioned in the beginning of this chapter, so that sufficed us from repeating it here."
"3834","Chapter 30: The mutamatti who finds no sacrificial animal and does not fast in the ten. 4091 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sallam narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about the mutamatti when he does not find the sacrificial animal and has not fasted in the ten, that he fasts the days of tashriq."
"3835","4092 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Kamil Fudayl ibn al-Husayn al-Jahdari narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Isa, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and from Salim, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not permit fasting the days of tashriq except for a muhsar or mutamatti."
"3836","4093 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and from Salim, from his father, that they both permitted the mutamatti, when he does not find a sacrificial animal and has not fasted before Arafah, to fast the days of tashriq. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to this and permitted fasting the days of tashriq for the mutamatti, the qarin, and the muhsar when they do not find a sacrificial animal and had not fasted before that; they fast these days, and they barred from them others besides them, and they argued in that with these reports. And others opposed them in that and said: Neither these nor others of the people may fast these days for anything of that, nor for anything of the expiations, nor in voluntary fasting, due to the prohibition of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from that. But upon the mutamatti and qarin is the sacrificial animal for their tamattu and qiran, and another sacrificial animal, because they released themselves without sacrificial animal or fasting. And they argued in that from the reports narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what"
"3837","4094 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, from Bishr ibn Suhaym al-Aslami, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The crier of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out in the days of tashriq and said: These days are days of eating and drinking."
"3838","4095 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd al-Madani narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to call out in the days of Mina that they are days of eating, drinking, and intercourse, so there is no fasting in them, meaning the days of tashriq."
"3839","4096 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla informed us, from Ata, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The days of tashriq are days of eating, drinking, and remembrance of Allah the Exalted, Mighty and Majestic."
"3840","4097 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn al-Had, from Abu Murrah, freedman of Aqil ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, that he entered, he and Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, upon Amr ibn al-As, and that was the morrow or after the morrow of the Day of Sacrifice, so Amr brought them food. Abdullah said: I am fasting. Amr said to him: Break fast, for these are the days which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us to break fast in, or he forbade us from fasting them, so Abdullah broke fast and ate, and I ate."
"3841","4098 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to me, he said: Sa'id ibn Kathir informed me that Ja'far ibn al-Muttalib informed him that Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As entered upon Amr ibn al-As, so he invited him to lunch and he said: I am fasting, then the second time likewise, then the third. He said: No, unless you heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), meaning the prohibition, from fasting the days of tashriq."
"3842","4099 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr and Salim, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abdullah ibn Hudhafah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to call out in the days of tashriq that they are days of eating and drinking."
"3843","4100 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Abdullah ibn Hudhafah to go about in the days of Mina: Do not fast these days, for they are days of eating, drinking, and remembrance of Allah."
"3844","4101 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Abi Salamah informed us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The days of tashriq are days of eating, drinking, and remembrance of Allah the Mighty and Majestic."
"3845","4102 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id, who is Ibn Mansur, narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha informed us, from Abu al-Malih al-Hudhali, from Nubayshah al-Hudhali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"3846","4103 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Nafi ibn Jubayr informed him, from a man of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Amr said: Nafi named him and I forgot him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man of Banu Ghifar called Bishr ibn Suhaym: Stand and call out among the people: They are days of eating and drinking, in the days of Mina. 4104 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed us, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, from Bishr ibn Suhaym, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"3847","4105 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us. (Hah) 4106 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, from Bishr ibn Suhaym, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"3848","4107 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Al-Rabi ibn Subayh and Marzuq Abu Abdullah al-Shami narrated to us, they said: Yazid al-Raqashi narrated to us, that Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting the three days of tashriq after the Day of Sacrifice."
"3849","4108 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, from al-Rabi ibn Subayh, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"3850","4109 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, from Ma'mar ibn Abdullah al-Adawi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to call out in the days of tashriq at Mina: Let no one fast, for they are days of eating and drinking."
"3851","4110 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad and Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, they said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Nadr, that he heard Sulayman ibn Yasar and Qabisah ibn Dhu'ayb narrating from Umm al-Fadl, wife of Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, she said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Mina in the days of tashriq, and I heard a crier saying: These days are days of food, drink, and remembrance of Allah. She said: So I sent a messenger: Who is the man, and who commanded him? The messenger came to me and told me that he is a man called Ibn Hudhafah, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me with it."
"3852","4111 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, he said: Al-Mundhir informed me, from Amr ibn Khaldah al-Zuraqi, from his mother, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, in the middle of the days of tashriq, calling out among the people: Do not fast in these days, for they are days of eating, drinking, and intercourse."
"3853","4112 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Hakim ibn Hakim, from Mas'ud ibn al-Hakam al-Zuraqi, he said: My mother narrated to me, she said: As if I am looking at Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, upon the white mule of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until he stood at the ravine of the Ansar while saying: O assembly of Muslims, they are not days of fasting; they are days of eating, drinking, and remembrance of Allah the Mighty and Majestic."
"3854","4113 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Tammam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Maymun ibn Yahya narrated to me, he said: Makhramah ibn Bukayr narrated to me, from his father, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Yasar claiming that he heard Ibn al-Hakam al-Zuraqi saying: My father narrated to us that they were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Mina, and they heard a rider shouting: Let no one fast, for they are days of eating and drinking."
"3855","4114 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, he narrated to him that Mas'ud narrated to him, from his mother, the like of it."
"3856","4115 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Fahmi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal informed us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, that he heard Yusuf ibn Mas'ud ibn al-Hakam al-Zuraqi saying: My grandmother narrated to me, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3857","4116 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Mas'ud ibn al-Hakam al-Ansari, from a man of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Abdullah ibn Hudhafah to mount his riding animal in the days of Mina and shout among the people: Let no one fast, for they are days of eating and drinking. He said: I saw him upon his riding animal calling out with that. They said: So when it is established by these reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the prohibition of fasting the days of tashriq, and his prohibition from that was at Mina while the pilgrims were staying there, and among them the mutamattis and qarins, and he did not except from them a mutamatti or qarin, the mutamattis and qarins enter that prohibition as well. Then if a speaker says: Why has this become worthier than what you narrated in the beginning of this chapter? It is said to him: Because of the soundness of what came in this, and the succession of reports with it, and the unsoundness of what came in the first section. Of that, the hadith of Yahya ibn Sallam from Shu'bah, for it is a rejected hadith, which the people of knowledge of narration do not establish, due to the weakness of Yahya ibn Sallam with them, and Ibn Abi Layla, and the corruption of their memories, though I do not like to disparage any of the scholars with anything, but I mentioned what the people of narration say in that. And of that the hadith of Yazid ibn Sinan which we mentioned after it, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that they both said: No one was permitted to fast the days of tashriq except a muhsar or mutamatti. So their statement of that, it is possible they meant by this permission what Allah the Mighty and Majestic said in His Book: Then fasting three days in hajj, so they counted the days of tashriq of the days of hajj and said: The mutamatti pilgrim and muhsar were permitted to fast the days of tashriq for this verse, and because these days with them are of the days of hajj, and hidden from them was what was of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) subsequently stopping the people upon that these days are not included in what Allah the Mighty and Majestic permitted fasting of that. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of verifying the meanings of the reports. And as for by way of examination, we saw them agree that the Day of Sacrifice is not fasted in for anything of that, while it is nearer to the days of hajj than the days of tashriq, due to what came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of prohibition of fasting it, of what we shall mention in this chapter, Allah the Exalted willing. So just as the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that includes the mutamattis, qarins, and muhsars, so likewise his prohibition of fasting the days of tashriq includes them as well. So of what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in prohibition of fasting the Day of Sacrifice is what"
"3858","4117 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed us, from Sa'id ibn Khalid, from Abu Ubayd, freedman of Ibn Azhar, he said: I witnessed the Eid with Ali and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them both, and they would pray then turn away reminding the people, and I heard them both saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting these two days: the Day of Sacrifice and the Day of Fitr."
"3859","4118 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Ubayd, he said: I witnessed the Eid with Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: These are two days the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting them: the Day of Fitr and the Day of Sacrifice. As for the Day of Fitr, it is the day of your breaking fast from your fasting, and as for the Day of Sacrifice, it is a day you eat in it of your sacrifices."
"3860","4119 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Ismail ibn Mujammi' and Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Ubayd, freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, he said: I prayed the Eid with Umar, and he mentioned the same as it."
"3861","4120 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Kathir al-Ansari narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Sa'id, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he forbade fasting two days: the Day of Fitr and the Day of Sacrifice."
"3862","4121 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"3863","4122 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that al-Mundhir ibn Ubayd al-Madani narrated to him that Abu Salih al-Samman narrated to him that he heard Abu Hurayrah informing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same as it."
"3864","4123 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, from al-Rabi ibn Subayh, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"3865","4124 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hibban, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"3866","4125 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Qaza'ah, from Abu Sa'id, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it. So when the Day of Sacrifice is outside the days of hajj which Allah the Mighty and Majestic made for the mutamatti fasting in them as a substitute for the sacrificial animal, due to what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) excluded of the days fasted in by his prohibition of fasting it, so likewise the days of tashriq are outside the days of hajj which Allah the Mighty and Majestic made for the mutamatti fasting in them as a substitute for the sacrificial animal, due to what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) excluded them of the days fasted by his prohibition of fasting them. So by what we mentioned it is established that the days of tashriq are not for anyone to fast them in tamattu, qiran, ihsar, or other than that of the expiations, nor of voluntary fasting. And this is the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And there has been narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, what indicates that as well."
"3867","4126 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj informed us, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that a man came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, on the Day of Sacrifice and said: O Commander of the Believers, I performed tamattu and did not sacrifice, and did not fast in the ten. He said: Ask among your people. Then he said: O Mu'ayqib, give him a sheep. Do you not see that Umar did not say to him: These are the days of tashriq, so fast them. So his leaving that and his commanding him with the sacrificial animal indicates that the days of hajj with him, in which Allah the Mighty and Majestic commanded the mutamatti to fast, are before the Day of Sacrifice, and that the Day of Sacrifice and what is after it of the days of tashriq are not of them."
"3868","Chapter 31: The ruling of the muhsar in hajj. 4127 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Al-Hajjaj al-Sawwaf narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, from Ikrimah, from al-Hajjaj ibn Amr al-Ansari, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever limps or breaks a bone has released himself, and upon him is another hajj. He said: So I narrated that to Ibn Abbas and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them, and they said: He spoke the truth."
"3869","4128 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj al-Sawwaf, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it, except that he did not mention: Ikrimah mentioning that to Ibn Abbas and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them."
"3870","4129 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, he said: Abdullah ibn Rafi, freedman of Umm Salamah, said that he said: I asked al-Hajjaj ibn Amr about one detained while in ihram, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned the same as it. So I narrated that to Ibn Abbas and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them, and they said: He spoke the truth. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that the one in ihram for hajj or umrah, when he breaks a bone or limps, has released himself then, so upon him is making up what he released himself from; if it was a hajj, then a hajj, and if it was an umrah, then an umrah, and they argued in that with this hadith. And others opposed them in that and said: He does not release himself until the sacrificial animal is slaughtered for him; then when the sacrificial animal is slaughtered for him he releases himself. And they argued in that with what"
"3871","4130 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Rumi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Thawr narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificed on the Day of al-Hudaybiyah before he shaved, and commanded his companions with that."
"3872","4131 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Tammam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Maymun ibn Yahya narrated to me, from Makhramah ibn Bukayr, from his father, he said: I heard Nafi, freedman of Ibn Umar, saying: Ibn Umar said: When an enemy confronts the one in ihram, then he releases himself then; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did that when the disbelievers of Quraysh detained him in his umrah from the House, so he sacrificed his sacrificial animal, shaved, and he and his companions released themselves, then they returned until they performed umrah the next year. So since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not release himself merely in his umrah by the enemy detaining him until he sacrificed the sacrificial animal, that indicates that likewise is the ruling of the muhsar: he does not release himself by ihsar until he slaughters the sacrificial animal. And there is not in what we narrated first any opposition to this with us, because the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever breaks a bone or limps has released himself, it bears that it be: it has become lawful for him to release himself, not upon that he has already released himself from his ihram. And this is like as is said: So-and-so woman has become lawful to men, when she has come out of a waiting period upon her from a husband she had before that; not upon the meaning that she has already become lawful to them so that intercourse with her be theirs, but upon the meaning that it has become lawful for them to marry her with a marriage making intercourse with her lawful. This is permissible, acceptable speech. So since this hadith bore what we mentioned, and there came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Urwah from al-Miswar what we described, by that this interpretation is established. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic explained that in His Book with His statement the Mighty and Majestic: Then if you are detained, then what is easy of the sacrificial animal, and do not shave your heads until the sacrificial animal reaches its place. So when Allah the Exalted commanded the muhsar not to shave his head until the sacrificial animal reaches its place, it is known by that that the muhsar does not release himself from his ihram except at the time what makes shaving his head lawful for him. So this is indicated by the statement of Allah the Exalted, then the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the time of al-Hudaybiyah. And of the evidence upon the soundness of that interpretation as well is that the hadith of al-Hajjaj ibn Amr, Ikrimah mentioned that he narrated it to Ibn Abbas and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them, and they said: He spoke the truth. So that hadith became from Ibn Abbas and from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them, as well. And Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said about the muhsar what agreed with the interpretation to which we directed the hadith of al-Hajjaj, and indicated by what"
"3873","4132 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah: And complete hajj and umrah for Allah, then if you are detained, then what is easy of the sacrificial animal. He said: When the man is detained, he sends the sacrificial animal. And do not shave your heads until the sacrificial animal reaches its place, then whoever of you is sick or has hurt in his head, then a ransom of fasting, charity, or sacrifice, so fasting three days. Then if he hastens and shaves before the sacrificial animal reaches its place, upon him is a ransom of fasting, charity, or sacrifice: fasting three days, or he gives charity upon six poor, each poor half a sa, or the sacrifice: a sheep. Then when he is safe from what was with him, then whoever performs tamattu with umrah to hajj, then if he proceeds on that course of his, upon him is a hajj, and if he delays the umrah to the next year, upon him is a hajj and umrah, and what is easy of the sacrificial animal, then whoever does not find, then fasting three days in hajj, the last of them the Day of Arafah, and seven when you return. He said: So I mentioned that to Sa'id ibn Jubayr, and he said: This is the statement of Ibn Abbas, and he contracted thirty."
"3874","4133 - Abu Shurayh Muhammad ibn Zakariyya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, that he said: About the statement of Allah the Mighty and Majestic to us: Then if you are detained, he said: From confinement or sickness. Ibrahim said: So I narrated it to Sa'id ibn Jubayr, and he said: Thus Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said. So this is Ibn Abbas, he did not make him release himself from his ihram by ihsar until the sacrificial animal is slaughtered for him. And there has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whoever breaks a bone or limps has released himself. So that indicates that the meaning of has released himself with him is, i.e., it is lawful for him to release himself, upon what we went to in that. And that has also been narrated from other than Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as well."
"3875","4134 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad ibn Shaddad al-Abdi, companion of Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: A companion of ours was stung at Dhat al-Tananin while in ihram for umrah, and that grieved us, so we met Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, and mentioned his matter to him. He said: He sends a sacrificial animal and appoints his companions an appointment; then when it is slaughtered for him he releases himself."
"3876","4135 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Abdullah said: Then upon him is an umrah after that."
"3877","4136 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-A'mash, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"3878","4137 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, he said: I heard Ibrahim narrating from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: A man of Nakha entered ihram for umrah, called Umayr ibn Sa'id, and was stung; while he lay fallen on the road, a party in whom was Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, came upon them and they asked him. He said: Send the sacrificial animal and make between you and it a day as a sign; then when that is, let him release himself. Al-Hakam said: And Umarah ibn Umayr said, and sufficient is he, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, that Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: And upon him is the umrah the next year. Shu'bah said: And I heard Sulayman narrate it with it, the same as what al-Hakam narrated exactly."
"3879","4138 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that he said: The muhsar does not release himself until he circumambulates the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, and if compelled to something of wearing clothes which he must have and medicine, he does that and ransoms. So by these narrations also from the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is established what agrees with what we interpreted the hadith of al-Hajjaj upon which we mentioned. Then the people differed after this about the ihsar whose ruling is this, by what thing is it? Or by what meaning does it occur? A group said: It occurs by every preventer preventing him, of sickness or other than it, and it is the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And we narrated that as well in what preceded of this chapter from Ibn Mas'ud and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them. And others said: The ihsar whose ruling is what we described occurs only by the enemy specifically, not by sicknesses, and it is the statement of Ibn Umar."
"3880","4139 - Muhammad ibn Zakariyya Abu Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them. He said: Ihsar occurs only from an enemy."
"3881","4140 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, that he said: Whoever is detained short of the House by sickness, he does not release himself until he circumambulates the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah. So when this disagreement occurred in this, and we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of al-Hajjaj ibn Amr, Ibn Abbas, and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them, what we mentioned of his statement, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever breaks a bone or limps has released himself, and upon him is another hajj, by that it is established that ihsar occurs by sickness as it occurs by the enemy. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of verifying the meanings of the reports. And as for its aspect by way of examination, we saw them agree that the ihsar of the enemy obligates for the muhsar release as we mentioned. And they differed about sickness: a group said its ruling is the ruling of the enemy in that, when it has prevented him from proceeding in hajj as the enemy prevented him. And others said its ruling is distinct from the ruling of the enemy. So we wanted to examine what is permitted by necessity of the enemy, is it permitted by necessity of sickness or not? We found the man, when he can stand, it is an obligation to pray standing, and if he fears if he stands that the enemy will see him and kill him, or the enemy stands over his head and prevents him from standing, all agree that it has become lawful for him to pray sitting, and the obligation of standing falls from him. And they agreed that if sickness or chronic ailment befalls a man and prevents him from standing, the obligation of standing falls from him, and it becomes lawful for him to pray sitting, bowing and prostrating if he can that, or gesturing if he cannot that. So we saw what is permitted to him of this by necessity of the enemy has been permitted to him by necessity of sickness. And we saw the man, when the enemy comes between him and water, the obligation of ablution falls from him, and he performs tayammum and prays. So these things in which excuse is made by the enemy, excuse is also made in them by sickness, and the state in that is alike. Then we saw the pilgrim muhsar by the enemy excused, so made for him in that to do what is made for the muhsar to do until he releases himself, and they differed about the muhsar by sickness. So the examination upon what we mentioned of that is that what became due to him of excuse by necessity of the enemy becomes due to him also by necessity of sickness, and his ruling in that is alike, as his ruling in that was also alike in purifications and prayers. Then the people differed after this about the one in ihram for umrah detained by enemy or sickness. A group said: He sends a sacrificial animal and appoints them to slaughter it for him; then when it is slaughtered he releases himself. And others said: Rather he remains upon his ihram forever, for it has no time like the time of hajj. And of the proof for those who went to that he releases himself from it by the sacrificial animal was what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the beginning of this chapter, when he was detained in umrah at the time of al-Hudaybiyah, the disbelievers of Quraysh detained him, so he sacrificed the sacrificial animal and released himself, and did not wait for the detention to leave him, since it has no time like the time of hajj; rather he made the excuse in detention by it like the excuse in detention by hajj. So by that it is established that its ruling in detention in both is alike, and that he sends the sacrificial animal until he releases himself by it from what he was detained of them both. Except that upon him in umrah is making up an umrah in place of his umrah, and upon him in hajj a hajj in place of his hajj and an umrah for his deficiency. And we narrated in umrah that the one in ihram may be muhsar by it, what preceded in this chapter from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. And as for the examination in that, we saw things obligated upon servants of what is made for them a specific time, and things obligated upon them of what the whole of time is made a time for them. Of them prayers, obligated upon them in specific times, performed in those times with preceding causes for them, of purification with water and covering nakedness. And of them fasting in expiations of zihar, expiations of fasting, and expiations of killing; that is made upon the one who declares zihar and the killer, not in particular days, but the whole of time is made a time for them. And likewise the expiation of oath, Allah the Mighty and Majestic made it upon the oath-breaker in his oath, which is feeding ten poor, clothing them, or freeing a neck. Then Allah the Mighty and Majestic made one upon whom prayers are obligated, with the causes preceding and the causes done in them in that, an excuse when prevented from them. Of that is what is made for him in lack of water, of the falling of purification with water and tayammum. And of that is what is made for one prevented from covering nakedness, to pray with nakedness exposed. And of that is what is made for one prevented from the qiblah, to pray to other than the qiblah. And of that is what is made for one prevented from standing, to pray sitting, bowing and prostrating; then if prevented from that also, to gesture; so that is made for him. And even if there remains of the time what may allow that excuse to leave him and he return to his state before the excuse while in the time, he is not commanded to wait for that, but commanded to do what is possible for him of what is due upon him in that state, since Allah the Exalted has permitted him that. Then when he does what he was commanded of that, and then the excuse leaves him while he is in the time, he does not repeat. So this and its likes indicate that every duty which has no end to its time but the whole of time is a time for it, then what is made for one prevented from it of its likes by an excuse is permissible in that state as it is permissible in other than it, and it suffices him. So likewise the muhrim for umrah, since the whole of time is the time of its performance, and whatever prevents him of enemy, sickness, or other than that, if that preventer remains with him until he dies, he would die deprived, prevented from what is made obligatory upon him of umrah, and meeting Allah the Exalted while its obligation remains due from him, so he is commanded to do what he can in his state in it to release himself from it as much as possible, and that is slaughtering the sacrificial animal. Then when he does that, he releases himself; then if the preventer leaves him afterwards, upon him is making up what was due upon him of it, as one who misses prayer in its time then makes it up after its time. Likewise hajj, though it has a specific time and specific causes, and some of it is in that time while some is outside that time, and it may be that what is outside that time of it, tayammum and other than it, suffices in it like the prayer whose time has a beginning and end. Then it is more worthy that what of it may suffice after its passing suffice in it, when done after its time has passed out of necessity. So this is the examination in this chapter. Then the people spoke after this about the muhsar, when he slaughters his sacrificial animal, does he shave his head or not? A group said: It is not upon him to shave, because the whole of the rites has gone from him, and of those who said that are Abu Hanifah and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both. And others said: Rather he shaves; then if he does not shave he releases himself and nothing is upon him, and of those who said that is Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him. And others said: He shaves and that is due upon him, as it is due upon the pilgrim and umrah performer. Of the proof of Abu Hanifah and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both, in that was that there has fallen from him by ihsar all the rites of hajj, of circumambulation, running between al-Safa and al-Marwah, and that is of what the one in ihram releases himself by from his ihram. Do you not see that when he circumambulates the House on the Day of Sacrifice, it becomes lawful for him to shave, so perfume, clothes, and women become lawful for him by that. They said: So since that of what he does until he releases himself, all of that falls from him by ihsar, there also falls from him the rest of what the one in ihram releases himself by, by cause of ihsar. This is a proof for Abu Hanifah and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both. And of the proof of the others upon them both in that was that those things of circumambulation of the House, running between al-Safa and al-Marwah, and stoning the Jamrahs, the one in ihram has been barred from them and come between him and them, so it falls from him to do them. And shaving, none has come between him and it, and he is able to do it. So what he can do, its ruling in it in the state of ihsar is like its ruling in it in the state of non-ihsar. And what he cannot do in the state of ihsar is what falls from him by ihsar. So it is the examination with us. And when his ruling in the time of shaving is upon him while muhsar like its ruling in its obligation upon him while not muhsar, his leaving it while muhsar is also like his leaving it while not muhsar. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that the ruling of shaving remains upon the muhsars as it is upon one who reaches the House."
"3882","4141 - And that is that Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Abi Najih narrated to me, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Men shaved on the Day of al-Hudaybiyah and others shortened. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on those who shave. They said: O Messenger of Allah, and those who shorten? He said: May Allah have mercy on those who shave. They said: O Messenger of Allah, and those who shorten? He said: May Allah have mercy on those who shave. They said: O Messenger of Allah, and those who shorten? He said: And those who shorten. They said: Why do those who shave have your apparent mercy? He said: They did not doubt."
"3883","4142 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"3884","4143 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: Al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Ibrahim al-Ansari, he said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri narrated to us, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking forgiveness on the Day of al-Hudaybiyah for those who shave three times, and for those who shorten once."
"3885","4144 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Ismail al-Khazzaz narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us that Abu Ibrahim al-Ansari narrated to him, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of al-Hudaybiyah sought forgiveness for those who shave once, and for those who shorten once. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions shaved their heads, except two men: a man of the Ansar and a man of Quraysh. Abu Ja'far said: So when they all shaved except those of them who shortened, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preferred those of them who shaved over those who shortened, by that it is established that shaving and shortening was due upon them, as it was due upon them had they reached the House; and had it not been that, they would have been in it but alike, and some of them would have no merit in that over others. So in the preferring by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that of those who shave over those who shorten is evidence that they were in that like others than the muhsars. So by what we mentioned it is established that the ruling of shaving or shortening is not removed by ihsar, and Allah I ask for success."
"3886","Chapter 32: Hajj of the young. 4145 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Uqbah narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a woman asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a boy: Is there hajj for this one? He said: Yes, and for you is reward."
"3887","4146 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"3888","4147 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Majishun narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that when the boy performs hajj before his puberty, that suffices him for the hajj of Islam, and it is not upon him to perform hajj after that after his puberty, and they argued in that with this hadith. And others opposed them in that and said: It does not suffice him for the hajj of Islam, and upon him after his puberty is another hajj. And of the proof for them with us upon the people of the first view was that this hadith is only in it that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that for the boy is a hajj, and this is of what all people agree upon, and they did not differ that for the boy is a hajj, just as for him is prayer, but that prayer is not an obligation upon him. So likewise also it is possible that for him is a hajj, but that hajj is not an obligation upon him; rather this hadith is a proof upon one who claims that there is no hajj for the boy. As for one who says: For him is a hajj, but it is not an obligation, he has not opposed anything of this hadith, but opposed the interpretation of a particular opposition. And this Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, is the one who narrated this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he directed the hajj of the boy to other than obligation, and that it does not suffice him after his puberty for the hajj of Islam."
"3889","4148 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Israel narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Safar, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: O people, let me hear what you say, and do not go out saying: Ibn Abbas said: Whichever boy his family takes for hajj and he dies, he has fulfilled the hajj of Islam; then if he reaches puberty, upon him is hajj. And whichever slave his family takes for hajj and he dies, he has fulfilled the hajj of Islam; then if freed, upon him is hajj."
"3890","4149 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, companion of al-Huli, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about the slave when he performs hajj then is freed after that? He said: Upon him is hajj also. And about the boy performing hajj then reaching puberty? He said: He performs hajj also. And you claimed that one who narrates a hadith is more knowledgeable of its interpretation, so this is Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we mentioned in the beginning of this chapter, then he said what we mentioned. So upon your principle it must be evidence upon the meaning of what is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that. Then if a speaker says: What indicates that that hajj does not suffice him for the hajj of Islam? I said: The statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The pen is lifted from three: from the young until he grows. And I mentioned that with its chains in other than this place of this book. So when it is established that the pen is lifted from the boy, it is established that hajj is not written upon him. And they agreed that if a boy enters the time of a prayer and prays it, then reaches puberty after that in its time, upon him is to repeat it, and he is in ruling one who did not pray it. So when that is established of their agreement, it is established that hajj is likewise, and that when he reaches puberty having performed hajj before that, he is in the ruling of one who did not perform hajj, and upon him is to perform hajj after that. Then if a speaker says: We saw in hajj a ruling opposing the ruling of prayer, and that is that Allah the Mighty and Majestic only obligated hajj upon one who finds a way to it, and did not obligate it upon other than him. So one who does not find a way to hajj, no hajj is upon him, like the boy who has not reached puberty. Then they agreed that one who does not find a way to hajj, then burdens himself and walks until he performs hajj, that that suffices him, and if he finds a way to it after that, it is not upon him to perform hajj a second time, for the hajj he had performed before finding the way. So the examination upon that would be that likewise the boy, when he performs hajj before puberty and does what was not due upon him, that suffices him, and it is not upon him to perform hajj a second time after puberty. It is said to him: The one who does not find the way, the obligation only falls from him for lack of reaching the House; then when he walks and reaches the House, he has reached the House and become of those finding the way, so hajj becomes due upon him for that. Therefore we said that a hajj suffices him, and because after his reaching the House he becomes like one whose dwelling is there, so hajj is upon him. As for the boy, the obligation of hajj is not due upon him, before his reaching the House and after his reaching it, due to the lifting of the pen from him. Then when he reaches puberty after that, only then does the obligation of hajj become due upon him. Therefore we said that what he had performed of hajj before his puberty does not suffice him, and that upon him is to begin hajj anew after his puberty, like one who had not performed hajj before that. So this is the examination also in this chapter, and it is the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"3891","Chapter 33: Entering the Haram, is it valid without ihram? 4150 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us. (Hah) 4151 - And Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hakim al-Awdi narrated to us. (Hah) 4152 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, they said: Sharik narrated to us, from Ammar al-Duhni, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah on the Day of Conquest with a black turban upon his head."
"3892","4153 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us. (Hah) 4154 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"3893","4155 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him. (Hah) 4156 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah with a helmet upon his head; then when he removed the helmet from his head it was said to him: Ibn Khatal is attached to the curtains of the Ka'bah, and he said: Kill him. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that there is no harm in entering the Haram without ihram, and they argued in that with these reports. And others opposed them in that and said: It is not valid for one whose dwelling is beyond the mawaqit to the cities to enter Makkah except in ihram. And these differed: some said: And likewise all people, whoever is after the mawaqit and before the mawaqit, other than the people of Makkah specifically. And others said: Whoever's dwelling is in some of the mawaqit or in what is after them to Makkah, he may enter Makkah without ihram. And whoever's dwelling is before the mawaqit does not enter Makkah except in ihram, and of those who said this statement are Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And others said: The people of the mawaqit, their ruling is the ruling of one before the mawaqit, and Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them, made the ruling of the people of the mawaqit like the ruling of those beyond them to Makkah. And the examination in this with us is not what they said: we saw one wanting ihram, when he passes the mawaqit lawful until he finishes his hajj without returning to the mawaqit, blood is upon him. And one who enters ihram from the mawaqit does well, and likewise one who enters ihram before them is likewise. So since entering ihram from the mawaqit is in the ruling of entering ihram from before them, not in entering ihram from after them, it is established that the ruling of the mawaqit is like the ruling of what is before them, not like the ruling of what is after them. So it is not permissible for their people to enter the Haram except what is permissible for the people of the cities before the mawaqit. So by this what Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah be pleased with them, said about the ruling of the people of the mawaqit is negated. And we needed examination in the reports: is in them what repels entering the Haram without ihram? And is in them what informs of a meaning in these two preceding hadiths by which meaning that entering which was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) without ihram is specific to him?"
"3894","4157 - So we considered in that, and lo, Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Abu Yusuf Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic made Makkah sacred the day He created the heavens and earth, the sun and moon, and placed it between these two mountains; it was not lawful to anyone before me, and was not lawful to me except an hour of daytime; its grass is not cut, its trees are not lopped, and its lost property is not lifted except by one announcing. Al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Except idhkhir, for the people of Makkah cannot do without it for their houses and graves? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Except idhkhir."
"3895","4158 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, he said: Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu Shurayh al-Ka'bi saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic made Makkah sacred, and the people did not make it sacred; so whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not shed blood in it, nor lop a tree in it. Then if one seeking license seeks license and says: It was made lawful to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then Allah the Mighty and Majestic made it lawful to me and did not make it lawful to people, and He only made it lawful to me for an hour."
"3896","4159 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to me, from Abu Shurayh al-Khuza'i, he said: When Amr ibn Sa'id sent the force to Makkah to fight Ibn al-Zubayr, Abu Shurayh came to him and spoke to him with what he heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then went out to the assembly of his people and sat, so I stood to him and sat with him. He said: He narrated from what Amr narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and from what Amr answered him with. He said: I said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he conquered Makkah; then when it was the morrow of the Day of Conquest, he addressed us and said: O people, Allah the Mighty and Majestic made Makkah sacred the day He created the heavens and earth, so it is sacred by the sacredness of Allah to the Day of Resurrection; it is not lawful to a man believing in Allah and the Last Day to shed blood in it, nor to lop a tree in it. It was not lawful to anyone before me, nor is it lawful to anyone after me, and it was not lawful to me except this hour, in anger against its people. Lo, then it has returned as its sanctity yesterday. So whoever says to you: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has made it lawful, say to him: Allah the Mighty and Majestic has made it lawful to His Messenger and did not make it lawful to you. Then he said to me: Turn away, O shaykh, for we know its sanctity better than you; it does not prevent a shedder of blood, nor one harboring ruin, nor one casting off obedience. I said: I was a witness and you were absent, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us that our witness convey to our absent, and I have conveyed to you."
"3897","4160 - Bahr, who is Ibn Nasr, narrated to us, from Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Shurayh al-Khuza'i, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3898","4161 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Darawardi informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood upon al-Hajun, then said: By Allah, you are the best of Allah's land and the most beloved of Allah's land to Allah; it was not lawful to anyone before me, nor is it lawful to anyone after me, and it was made lawful to me only for an hour of daytime, and after this hour of it it is sacred to the Day of Resurrection."
"3899","4162 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal and Abu Salamah Musa ibn Ismail al-Tabudhaki narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"3900","4163 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: Al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to us, he said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, he said: When Allah the Mighty and Majestic opened Makkah to His Messenger, upon him be peace, Hudhayl killed a man of Banu Thaqif for a slain one they had in Jahiliyyah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic withheld from the people of Makkah the elephant, and gave power over them to His Messenger and the believers. It was not lawful to anyone before me, nor is it lawful to anyone after me, and it was only made lawful to me for an hour of daytime; and in this hour of mine it is sacred: its trees are not lopped, its thorns are not cut, and its fallen thing is not picked up except by one announcing."
"3901","4164 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it, except that he said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic withheld from the people of Makkah the elephant. He said: And its stray is not picked up except by one announcing. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in these reports that Makkah was not lawful to anyone before him, nor is it lawful to anyone after him, and that it was only made lawful to him for an hour of daytime, then returned sacred as it was to the Day of Resurrection. So that indicates that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered it the day he entered it while it was lawful to him, so by that entering it without ihram was his, while after it is sacred, so no one enters it except in ihram. Then if a speaker says: The meaning of what was made lawful to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of it is bearing arms in it for fighting and shedding blood, not other than that. It is said to him: This is impossible: if what was permitted to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of it were what you mentioned specifically, since he did not say: Nor is it lawful to anyone after me. And we saw them agree that if the polytheists overpower Makkah and prevent the Muslims from it, it is lawful for the Muslims to fight them, bear arms in it, and shed blood, and that the ruling of those after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that in its permission is in the ruling of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So that indicates that the meaning with which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was specified in it, and it was made lawful to him for it, is not fighting. And when it is negated that it be fighting, it is established that it is ihram. Do you not see the statement of Amr ibn Sa'id to Abu Shurayh: The Haram does not prevent a shedder of blood, nor one harboring ruin, nor one casting off obedience, in answer to what Abu Shurayh narrated to him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), yet Abu Shurayh did not deny that upon him, nor say to him: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only meant by what I narrated to you that the Haram shelters all people, but he knew that and did not deny it. And this Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then said from his opinion: No one enters the Haram except in ihram, and we shall mention that in its place, Allah the Exalted willing. So his statement indicates that what is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what was made lawful to him is not upon displaying arms in it, but upon another meaning. Because when this statement is negated, and there is no other than it and the other statement, the other statement is established. Then we needed after this examination in the ruling of those after the mawaqit to Makkah, may they enter the Haram without ihram or not? We saw the man, when he wants to enter the Haram, he does not enter it except in ihram, alike whether he wants to enter the Haram for ihram or for a need other than ihram. And we saw one wanting to enter those places between the mawaqit and the Haram for a need, that he may enter them without ihram. So by that it is established that the ruling of these places, when entered for needs without ihram, is like the ruling of what is before the mawaqit, and that their people do not enter the Haram except as one enters it whose people are beyond the mawaqit to the horizons. So this is the examination with me in this chapter, and it opposes the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. And that is that they only followed in what they went to of this what:"
"3902","4165 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he went out of Makkah wanting Madinah; then when he reached Qudayd, news reached him of an army that had come to Madinah, so he returned and entered Makkah without ihram."
"3903","4166 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, went out of Makkah while wanting Madinah. Then when he was near, the army of Ibn Duljah met him, so he returned and entered Makkah lawful."
"3904","4167 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, that Abdullah ibn Umar came from Makkah until when he was at Qudayd, news from Madinah reached him, so he returned and entered Makkah lawful. So they followed that and adhered to it, while the examination in that with us was opposite what they went to. And there has been narrated from other than Ibn Umar in that what opposes this."
"3905","4168 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Uthman al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ata said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: There is no umrah upon the Makkan except that he goes out of the Haram, then does not enter it except in ihram. It was said to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: What if a man goes out of Makkah near? He said: Yes, he fulfills his need and makes with its fulfillment an umrah."
"3906","4169 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Hakam, from Ata, he said: No one enters the Haram except in ihram. It was said: Not even the woodcutters? He said: Not even the woodcutters. He said: Then it reached me afterwards that he permitted the woodcutters."
"3907","4170 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik informed us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to say: No trader enters Makkah, nor seeker of need, except in ihram."
"3908","4171 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed us, from al-Hasan, that he used to say that."
"3909","4172 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qays, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: No one enters Makkah except in ihram."
"3910","4173 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Aflah ibn Humayd narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: No one enters Makkah except in ihram. Then if a speaker says: Is it permissible for one after the mawaqit to Makkah to perform tamattu? It is said to him: Yes, and he in that is also opposite the people of Makkah, and this also opposes the statement of our companions; but it is the examination with us upon what we mentioned and explained. And those present at the Sacred Mosque with us are the people of Makkah specifically. And this statement which we went to in this was said by Nafi, freedman of Ibn Umar, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz al-A'raj."
"3911","4174 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Makhramah ibn Bukayr informed me, from his father, he said: I heard Nafi, freedman of Ibn Umar, being asked about the statement of Allah the Mighty and Majestic: That for whoever whose family are not present at the Sacred Mosque, is it inside Makkah or around it? He said: Inside Makkah. And Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj said that."
"3912","Chapter 34: A man sends the sacrificial animal to Makkah and stays among his family, does he undress when he garlands the sacrificial animal? 4175 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ata ibn Abi Labibah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Jabir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he tore his shirt from its opening until he took it out from his feet. The people looked at the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: I commanded for my camels which I sent that they be garlanded today and marked, at such-and-such place; so I wore my shirt and forgot, and I would not take my shirt out from my head. And he had sent his camels and stayed at Madinah. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that when a man sends the sacrificial animal and stays among his family, and the sacrificial animal is garlanded and marked, he undresses and stays thus until the people release themselves from their hajj. And they argued in that with this hadith, and they narrated that as well from Ibn Abbas and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them."
"3913","4176 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman, that she informed him that Ziyad ibn Abi Sufyan wrote to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: Whoever sends a sacrificial animal, prohibited to him is what is prohibited to the pilgrim until he slaughters his sacrificial animal; and I have sent a sacrificial animal, so write to me with your command, or command the owner of the sacrificial animal. Aishah said: It is not as Ibn Abbas said; I twisted the garlands of the sacrificial animal of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with my hands, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) garlanded it with his hand, then sent it with my father, and nothing which Allah the Mighty and Majestic made lawful to him became prohibited to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until the sacrificial animal was slaughtered."
"3914","4177 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah informed us, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar, when he sent his sacrificial animal while staying, refrained from what the one in ihram refrains from until he slaughters his sacrificial animal."
"3915","4178 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that when he sent his sacrificial animal, he refrained from women. And others opposed them in that and said: No undressing is due upon anyone, nor leaving anything of what the one in ihram leaves, except by his entering ihram, either for hajj or umrah. And of what they argued with in that is what we narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, in what she answered Ziyad with, and with what:"
"3916","4179 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid informed us, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, he said: I said to Aishah: There are men here who send sacrificial animals to the House, and instruct the one they send with them to mark them with a marker of theirs on that day, and they remain in ihram until the people exit ihram. She clapped her hands, and I heard that from behind the veil, and she said: Glory be to Allah, I used to twist the garlands of the sacrificial animals of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with my own hands, and he would send them to the Kabah, and he would stay among us, not abandoning anything that a non-muhrim does, until the people returned."
"3917","4180 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yala ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3918","4181 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind informed us, from Amir, from Masruq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I used to twist with my hands (garlands) for the camels of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he would send the sacrificial animal while he was residing in Madinah, and he would do what a non-muhrim does before it reached the House."
"3919","4182 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, she said: I would often twist the garlands for the sacrificial animal of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he would garland it, then send it, then stay back, not avoiding anything that a muhrim avoids."
"3920","4183 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Juhadah narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We used to garland sheep and it would be sent - or she said: we would send it - while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was non-muhrim, nothing becoming forbidden for him."
"3921","4184 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I would often twist the garlands for the sacrificial animal of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he would garland it, then send it, then stay back, not avoiding anything that a muhrim avoids."
"3922","4185 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3923","4186 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Mansur, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3924","4187 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"3925","4188 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from al-Layth, from Ibn Shihab, he narrated to him, from Urwah and Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"3926","4189 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"3927","4190 - Rabi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Hisham, from Urwah, from Aishah, the like of it."
"3928","4191 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"3929","4192 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman and Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Maslamah al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Aflah narrated to us, from al-Qasim, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"3930","4193 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"3931","4194 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3932","4195 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to me, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, and he added: (And we do not know anything that releases a muhrim except tawaf of the House)."
"3933","4196 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Abi Bakr, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it, except that he did not mention the addition found in the previous one. These reports from Aishah with what we mentioned have reached mass-transmission, unlike anything contradicting them from others. If this is judged by soundness of chains, the chain of this hadith of Aishah is a sound chain with no dispute among the people of knowledge about it, unlike the hadith of Jabir ibn Abd Allah. If judged by manifestness and mass-transmission of narration, Aishah's hadith is also worthier, for that exists in it and is absent in Jabir's hadith. If judged by reasoning: those who follow Jabir's hadith say the sanctity binding the sender of the sacrificial animal by garlanding and marking it is lifted when the people exit ihram, without any act he himself performs. So we examined the agreed-upon ihram: is it like that? We found that a man who enters ihram for Hajj or Umrah becomes a muhrim by agreed ihram, and he does not exit it except by acts he performs, by which he becomes lawful, not otherwise. Do you not see that if he is a pilgrim and misses the standing at Arafah until its time passes, his Hajj is lost, and he is not released except by an act he performs: tawaf of the House, sai between Safa and Marwah, and shaving or shortening. If he stood at Arafah and did everything the pilgrim does except the obligatory tawaf, women never become lawful to him until he performs the obligatory tawaf. Likewise Umrah is never exited except by tawaf of the House, sai between Safa and Marwah, and the shaving after that. These are the rulings of the agreed-upon ihram: mere passage of time does not remove it, only acts do. One who entered ihram for Umrah and drove the sacrificial animal intending tamattu, then performed tawaf and sai for his Umrah, is not released until he completes his Hajj and slaughters the animal: an added sanctity caused by the animal, for without it he would have shaved and become lawful after tawaf and sai, and only the driven animal prevented that; and his release from that sanctity is also only by an act he performs, not by passage of time. So the agreed-upon ihram is not exited by passage of times nor by others' acts, but by acts he himself performs. As for one who merely sends an animal, stays with his family, and orders it garlanded and marked, so that undressing (tajarrud) becomes obligatory on him in the view of those who impose it, and he is released from that sanctity without any act of his own but at the time the people are released - that contradicts the agreed-upon ihram, so it is not established likewise; disputed matters are only established when they resemble agreed matters, and when they do not resemble them they are not established except with a decisive text establishing proof, which would remove disagreement. Thus what we mentioned establishes the correctness of the view of those who follow Aishah's hadith, may Allah be pleased with her, and the corruption of the view of those who oppose it in favor of Jabir ibn Abd Allah's hadith. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"3934","4197 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Said, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Rabiah ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Hudayr, that he saw a man undressed (mutajarrid) in Iraq. He said: I asked the people about him, and they said: He ordered his sacrificial animal to be garlanded, so for that he undressed. Rabiah said: I met Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, and he said: An innovation, by the Lord of the Kabah. In our view Ibn al-Zubayr would not have sworn it was an innovation unless he knew the Sunnah was contrary to it."
"3935","4198 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Aliyah, he said: I asked Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, about a man who sends his sacrificial animal: does he abstain from women? Ibn Umar said: We do not know a muhrim to be released until he performs tawaf of the House. The meaning of this is that the muhrim for whom women are forbidden is the one released from that by tawaf of the House; this one has no tawaf upon him, so there is no meaning in his avoiding that. This contradicts what we narrated from Ibn Umar at the beginning of this chapter."
"3936","Chapter 35: Marriage of the muhrim. 4199 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik and Ibn Abi Dhib both narrated to him, from Nafi, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, brother of Banu Abd al-Dar, from Aban ibn Uthman, he said: I heard my father Uthman ibn Affan say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The muhrim does not marry, nor give in marriage, nor propose."
"3937","4200 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he did not say: nor propose."
"3938","4201 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abd al-Jabbar ibn Nubayh ibn Wahb, from his father, from Aban ibn Uthman, from Uthman, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He does not marry, nor give in marriage, nor propose."
"3939","4202 - Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: Yusuf al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from Zayd ibn Ali, from Aban ibn Uthman, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, except that he did not say: nor propose."
"3940","4203 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Musa al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Nubayh narrated to me, from Aban ibn Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The muhrim does not marry nor give in marriage. Abu Jafar said: A group went with this hadith and said: It is not permissible for the muhrim to marry, give in marriage, or propose. Others differed from them and said: We see no harm in all that for the muhrim, but if he marries he should not consummate with her until he exits ihram. They argued with what follows:"
"3941","4204 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us. (H). 4205 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to me, he said: Aban ibn Salih and Abd Allah ibn Abi Najih narrated to us, from Mujahid and Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Maymunah bint al-Harith while he was in ihram, and stayed in Makkah three days; then Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza came to him with a group of Quraysh on the third day and said: Your term has expired, so leave us. He said: What harm to you if you left me to consummate among you, and we made food for you and you attended it? They said: We have no need of your food, leave us. So the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left, taking Maymunah, until he consummated with her at Sarif."
"3942","4206 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Rabah ibn Abi Maruf narrated to us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Maymunah bint al-Harith while he was a muhrim."
"3943","4207 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3944","4208 - Ali ibn Shaybah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Khaytham, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3945","4209 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. (H). 4210 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, both said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Humayd, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3946","4211 - Abu Bakrah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us. (H). 4212 - And Ismail ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, both said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Amr said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Maymunah, she being his maternal aunt, while he was non-muhrim. Amr said: I said to al-Zuhri: What would Yazid ibn al-Asamm know? A bedouin urinater! Do you make him like Ibn Abbas?!"
"3947","4213 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married some of his wives while he was a muhrim."
"3948","4214 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Kamil Abu al-Ala narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married while he was a muhrim. The people of the first view said to them: Who follows you that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Maymunah while muhrim? For Abu Rafi and Maymunah both mention that it was while he was non-muhrim. They cited what follows:"
"3949","4215 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Matar, from Rabiah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Rafi: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, while non-muhrim, and consummated with her while non-muhrim, and I was the messenger between them."
"3950","4216 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin and Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, they said: Asad narrated to us. (H). 4217 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Habib ibn Maymun ibn Mihran, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from Maymunah bint al-Harith, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me at Sarif while we were both non-muhrim, after returning from Makkah. Ibn Khuzaymah did not say: after returning from Makkah."
"3951","4218 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me that he heard Abu Fazarah narrate, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, he said: Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her while non-muhrim. Part of our proof against them: if this matter is judged by soundness and uprightness of chain - and that is their doctrine - then the hadith of Abu Rafi they cited was only narrated by Matar al-Warraq, and Matar in their view is not one whose hadith is proof; and Malik, who is more precise and retentive than him, narrated it disconnected."
"3952","4219 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik narrated to him, from Rabiah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Sulayman ibn Yasar: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Abu Rafi his freedman and a man of the Ansar, and they married him to Maymunah bint al-Harith while he was in Madinah before leaving. As for Yazid ibn al-Asamm's hadith, Amr ibn Dinar disparaged him in his address to al-Zuhri, and al-Zuhri did not object, removing him from the people of knowledge and making him a bedouin urinater; yet they disparage a man with less than this speech and from one lesser than Amr ibn Dinar and al-Zuhri - so how when both agreed on what we mentioned about Yazid ibn al-Asamm? Moreover the proof according to you regarding Maymun ibn Mihran is Jafar ibn Burqan, and he narrated this hadith disconnected."
"3953","4220 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Burqan narrated to us, from Maymun ibn Mihran, he said: I was with Ata when a man came and said: May the muhrim marry? Ata said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic never forbade marriage since He permitted it. Maymun said: I said to him: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to me: Ask Yazid ibn al-Asamm whether the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he married Maymunah, was non-muhrim or in ihram? Yazid said: He married her while non-muhrim. Ata said: We only took this from Maymunah; we used to hear that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her while a muhrim. So Jafar ibn Burqan reported from Maymun ibn Mihran the occasion by which this hadith reached him from Yazid ibn al-Asamm: it was only Yazid's statement, not from Maymunah nor from anyone else; then Maymun argued with Ata citing Yazid without endorsing it by him. Had he possessed it from one further removed he would have argued with it to strengthen his proof. So this is the origin of this hadith too: from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, not from others. Those who narrated that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her while muhrim are people of knowledge, and the most established companions of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him - Said ibn Jubayr, Ata, Tawus, Mujahid, Ikrimah, and Jabir ibn Zayd - all imams and jurists whose narrations and views are taken as proof; likewise among them Amr ibn Dinar, Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, and Abd Allah ibn Abi Najih - also imams followed in narration. It was also narrated from Aishah agreeing with Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated from her by one impugned by none: Abu Awanah, from Mughirah, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq - all imams whose narration is proof. What they narrated is worthier than what one unlike them in precision, firmness, jurisprudence, and trustworthiness narrated. As for Uthman's hadith, may Allah be pleased with him, it was only narrated by Nubayh ibn Wahb, who is not like Amr ibn Dinar, nor Jabir ibn Zayd, nor those who narrated in agreement from Masruq from Aishah; Nubayh has no standing in knowledge like any of those we mentioned. So it is not permissible, being so, to oppose with it all those we mentioned who narrated contrary to what he narrated. This is the ruling of this chapter by way of reports. As for reasoning: intercourse with women is forbidden for the muhrim, so it was possible the marriage contract with them is likewise. We examined and found them agreed there is no harm in the muhrim buying a slave girl, though he may not touch her until he exits ihram; no harm in buying perfume to wear after exiting, nor in buying a shirt to wear after exiting - though intercourse, perfuming, and clothing are all forbidden to him while muhrim, yet that sanctity did not prevent him contracting ownership of them. We saw the muhrim may not buy game, so the ruling of the marriage contract could be like buying game or like buying what we described besides it. We examined: one who enters ihram with game in hand is ordered to release it, while one who enters ihram wearing a shirt or holding perfume is ordered to lay it aside; yet that was not like game which he is ordered to release and not keep. We saw him when he enters ihram with a wife, he is not ordered to release her but to guard and protect her - so the wife in that is like clothing and perfume, not like game. By reasoning, initiating a marriage contract upon her is like initiating ownership of clothes and perfume, which becomes lawful to wear and use after exiting ihram. One objected: we saw one who marries his foster sister - his marriage is void, though buying her is valid; purchase may be contracted over one whose intercourse is unlawful, while marriage is only contracted over one whose intercourse is lawful; and the woman's intercourse is forbidden to the muhrim, so by reasoning her marriage to him is forbidden. The reply of the others: we saw the fasting person and the one in itikaf, intercourse forbidden to each, yet all agree that sanctity of intercourse does not prevent them contracting marriage for themselves, since what forbade intercourse to them is a religious sanctity like the sanctity of a woman's menstruation, which does not prevent her contracting marriage upon herself. The sanctity of ihram by reasoning is likewise. We saw fosterage, which forbids marrying a woman on its account, when it occurs after marriage dissolves the marriage; likewise initiating marriage upon it is impermissible. But ihram when it occurs after marriage does not dissolve it, so by reasoning it should not prevent initiating the marriage bond either; the sanctity of intercourse by ihram is exactly like its sanctity by fasting, and since fasting sanctity does not prevent the marriage contract, ihram sanctity does not prevent the marriage bond either. This is the reasoning in this chapter, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"3954","4221 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Amash, from Ibrahim: Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, saw no harm in the muhrim marrying."
"3955","4222 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Habib al-Muallim, Qays, and Abd al-Karim, from Ata: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, saw no harm in two muhrims marrying."
"3956","4223 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to me, he said: I asked Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, about the marriage of the muhrim, and he said: What harm is in it? Is it but like a sale."
"3957","In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. Book 7: The Book of Marriage. Chapter 1: What is forbidden of outbidding a man over his brother's bargaining and proposing over his brother's proposal. 4224 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, he said: Nafi narrated to me, from Ibn Umar: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) said: A man shall not sell over his brother's sale, nor propose over his brother's proposal."
"3958","4225 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3959","4226 - Yunus and Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shimasah al-Mahri, that he heard Uqbah ibn Amir say upon the pulpit: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believer is the brother of the believer; it is not lawful for him to buy over his brother's purchase until he abandons - i.e. leaves - nor to propose over his brother's proposal until he abandons."
"3960","4227 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3961","4228 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Sakhr ibn Juwayriyah informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you shall sell over another's sale, nor shall one of you propose over his brother's proposal until the proposer abandons or permits him to propose."
"3962","4229 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Salih ibn Dinar, from his father, from Abu Said al-Khudri: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man shall not bargain over his brother's bargaining."
"3963","4230 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus - i.e. Ibn Yazid - informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Said ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, meaning he said: None of you shall propose over his brother's proposal until he marries or abandons."
"3964","4231 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): A man shall not propose over his brother's proposal, nor bargain over his brother's bargaining."
"3965","4232 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3966","4233 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"3967","4234 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: None of you shall propose over his brother's proposal until he marries or abandons."
"3968","4235 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you shall propose over his brother's proposal."
"3969","4236 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik narrated to him, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3970","4237 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu Kathir say: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man shall not outbid over his brother's bargaining until he buys or abandons, nor propose over his brother's proposal until he marries or abandons."
"3971","4238 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Nafi informed me, from Dawud ibn Qays, from Abu Said, freedman of Abd Allah ibn Amir ibn Kurayz, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you shall sell over another's sale, nor shall one of you propose over another's proposal. Abu Jafar said: A group went with this and said: It is not lawful for anyone to bargain over something another is bargaining over until the one bargaining abandons it; likewise he should not propose to a woman another has proposed to until that proposer abandons her, and they argued with these reports. Others differed and said: If the bargainer or proposer has been inclined to, then none may bargain over his bargaining nor propose over his proposal until he abandons; and the bargaining and proposal mentioned in the first reports that are forbidden are only what we described. As for one who bargained with a man over something, or proposed to him for a woman he is guardian of, without being inclined to, then it is permissible for others to bargain over what he bargained and propose over what he proposed. They argued with what follows:"
"3972","4239 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Jahm, he said: I heard Fatimah bint Qays say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: When your waiting period ends, inform me. She said: Suitors proposed to me - plural of suitor - among them Muawiyah and Abu al-Jahm. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Muawiyah is light of condition - i.e. poor - and Abu al-Jahm beats women - or: has harshness toward women - but you should have Usamah ibn Zayd."
"3973","4240 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Jahm, from Fatimah, the like of it."
"3974","4241 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Kathir al-Ansari narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Fatimah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3975","4242 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah, from Abu Salamah, from Fatimah bint Qays: When her waiting period ended, Abu al-Jahm and Muawiyah proposed to her, each time the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) saying: Where are you from Usamah?"
"3976","4243 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik informed him, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid, freedman of al-Aswad ibn Sufyan, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Fatimah bint Qays, she said: When I became lawful I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) and mentioned to him that Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan and Abu Jahm had proposed to me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) said: As for Abu Jahm, he never puts his stick down from his shoulder; as for Muawiyah, he is a pauper with no wealth; but marry Usamah ibn Zayd. She said: I disliked him, then he said: Marry Usamah, so I married him, and Allah placed good in him, and I was envied for him."
"3977","4244 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Salamah and Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Fatimah bint Qays, she said: When I became lawful, Muawiyah and a man of Quraysh proposed to me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Marry Usamah. I disliked him, then he said: Marry him, so I married him."
"3978","4245 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, he said: al-Mujalid ibn Said narrated to us, from Amir, from Fatimah bint Qays: A man of Quraysh proposed to her, and she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he said: Shall I not marry you to a man I love? She said: Yes, so he married her to Usamah. Abu Jafar said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) proposed Fatimah for Usamah after knowing of Muawiyah's and Abu al-Jahm's proposal, that proves such a state is one in which people may propose; so the first reports forbid something else: what we mentioned earlier in this chapter is where there is inclination to the suitor, and what we mentioned after is where there is no inclination to the suitor, so these reports stand sound and their meanings agree without contradiction. Likewise bargaining follows the same meaning, as clarified by what follows:"
"3979","4246 - Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: al-Akhdar ibn Ajlan informed us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi informed me, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him: A man of the Ansar came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complaining of poverty, then returned saying: O Messenger of Allah, I have come from a household I do not think I shall return to before some of them die of hunger. He said: Go, do you find anything? He went and brought a coarse cloth and a cup, saying: O Messenger of Allah, this cloth they would spread part of and wrap in part, and this cup they drank from. He said: Who will take them from me for a dirham? A man said: I will. He said: Who bids more than a dirham? A man said: I take them for two dirhams. He said: They are yours. He called the man and said: Buy with a dirham food for your family, and with a dirham an axe, then come to me. He did so, then came, and he said: Go to this valley and leave in it no thorn nor firewood, and do not come to me except after ten days. He did so, then came to him saying: Blessed is what you commanded me. He said: This is better for you than coming on the Day of Resurrection with spots of begging on your face, or scratches of begging - the doubt is from Muhammad ibn Bahr. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted auctioning in this hadith, which involves bargaining after bargaining except that the earlier bargaining had no inclination with it, that also proves what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade of a man's bargaining over his brother's is the contrary. This hadith clarifies the meaning of what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade of a man's bargaining over his brother's, and Fatimah bint Qays's hadith clarifies what he forbade of a man's proposal over his brother's. This meaning by which we validated these reports, in what we permitted of bargaining and proposal and what we forbade, is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. It was also narrated permitting sale by outbidding from those after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family)."
"3980","4247 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from al-Layth ibn Sad, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: I found the people selling spoils to the highest bidder."
"3981","4248 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf informed us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Nafi, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: There is no harm in bargaining over a man's bargaining when in the open market, this one and that one bargaining; but if a man is alone with him, none shall bargain over him."
"3982","Chapter 2: Marriage without a guardian from the agnates. 4249 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family), he said: Any woman married without her guardian's permission, her marriage is void; if he consummated with her, she has her dower for what he made lawful of her private part; if they dispute, the ruler is the guardian of one who has no guardian."
"3983","4250 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3984","4251 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Muammar ibn Sulayman al-Raqqi narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3985","4252 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Rabiah narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3986","4253 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Jafar, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: A group went with this and said: A woman marrying herself is not permissible except with her guardian's permission; among those who said that: Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them both, and they argued with these reports. Others differed and said: A woman may marry herself to whom she wills, and her guardian may not object to her in that, when she places herself where she ought to place herself. Part of their proof was that Ibn Jurayj mentioned he asked Ibn Shihab about the hadith of Ibn Jurayj we mentioned from Sulayman ibn Musa, and he did not recognize it."
"3987","4254 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us of that, he said: Yahya ibn Main informed us, from Ibn Ulayyah, from Ibn Jurayj of that. Abu Jafar said: They invalidate a hadith for less than this. As for Hajjaj ibn Artah, they do not affirm his hearing from al-Zuhri, and his hadith from him in their view is mursal, and they do not argue by mursal; and Ibn Lahiah - they object to their opponent arguing against them by his hadith, so how do they argue against him with it in such as this? Then even if what they narrated from al-Zuhri were established, there is narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, what contradicts it."
"3988","4255 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik informed him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): She married Hafsah bint Abd al-Rahman to al-Mundhir ibn al-Zubayr while Abd al-Rahman was absent in Sham. When Abd al-Rahman came he said: Is one like me treated thus and bypassed? Aishah spoke on behalf of al-Mundhir, and al-Mundhir said: That is in Abd al-Rahman's hand. Abd al-Rahman said: I would not reject a matter you have decided, so Hafsah remained with him, and that was not a divorce."
"3989","4256 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: al-Layth informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3990","4257 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Hanzalah and Aflah informed me, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, concerning Hafsah, the like of that. Since Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, held that her marrying Abd al-Rahman's daughter to another than him was permissible, and held that contract sound, even permitting therein the conferral of ownership which only follows a sound established marriage, it is impossible in our view that she held that while knowing that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) said: There is no marriage except with a guardian. Thus the corruption of what was narrated from al-Zuhri in that is established. The people of the first view also argued for their saying with what follows:"
"3991","4258 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us. 4259 - And Abu Bakrah and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, they said: Israil informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Burdah, from his father: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no marriage except with a guardian (i.e. except with his permission). Part of the proof against them in that: on their own principles this hadith establishes no proof, for one firmer than Israil and more retentive than him, like Sufyan and Shubah, narrated it from Abu Ishaq disconnected."
"3992","4260 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Burdah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): There is no marriage except with a guardian."
"3993","4261 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Burdah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So the basis of this hadith from Abu Burdah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family), is by the narration of Shubah and Sufyan, each of whom in their view is proof over Israil - how much more when both combine? If they say: Abu Awanah narrated it raised (marfu) as Israil narrated, they cited in that what follows:"
"3994","4262 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Israil and Abu Awanah narrated to us. (H). 4263 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us. (H). 4264 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no marriage except with a guardian. It was said to them: This was narrated from Abu Awanah as you mentioned, but we examined its basis and it is from Abu Awanah, from Israil, from Abu Ishaq, so Abu Awanah's hadith also reverts to Israil's hadith."
"3995","4265 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us of that, he said: al-Mualla ibn Mansur al-Razi narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Israil, from Abu Ishaq, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Thus it is negated that Abu Awanah has anything in this from Abu Ishaq directly. If they say: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated it from Abu Ishaq as Israil narrated, they cited in that what follows:"
"3996","4266 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Salt al-Kufi narrated to us. (H). 4267 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, both said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no marriage except with a guardian. It was said to them: True, Qays narrated it as you mentioned, but Qays in their view is below Israil; and if Israil is negated as a counter to Sufyan and Shubah, Qays is even less a counter to them. If they say: Some of Sufyan's companions narrated it from Sufyan raised, as Israil and Qays narrated, they cited in that what follows:"
"3997","4268 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Kamil narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Mansur narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family): There is no marriage except with a guardian. It was said to them: True, Bishr ibn Mansur narrated it from Sufyan as you mentioned, but you would not accept from your opponent the like of this if they argued against him by what Sufyan's companions or most of them narrated from him upon a meaning, while he argues against you by what Bishr ibn Mansur narrated from Sufyan contradicting that meaning, and you deem one who argues against you with such ignorant of hadith - so how do you permit yourselves against your opponent what they do not permit against you? This is manifest injustice. My speech in this is not intended to disparage anyone I mentioned, nor do I count such as criticism; I only intended to show this arguer's injustice and bind him by his own proof with what I mentioned. But I say: Even if it were established from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: There is no marriage except with a guardian, it would be no proof for what those who argued by it said in this chapter, for it bears possible meanings. It may bear what our opponent said: that guardian is the nearest agnate to the woman. It may bear that the guardian is whomsoever the woman appoints of men, near or distant. This doctrine validates the view of one who says: A woman may not herself conclude her own marriage contract even if her guardian orders her, nor another's marriage contract, and only men may conclude it. The like of that was narrated from Aishah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with her."
"3998","4269 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: She married a man of her brother's sons to a girl of her brother's sons, striking a veil between them, then spoke, and when nothing remained but the marriage she ordered a man to conclude it, then said: Marriage is not for women. It may also bear that his saying: There is no marriage except with a guardian means the guardian is the one to whom belongs authority over the private part: the father of the minor, the master of the slave woman, or the adult free woman for herself. That would mean none may contract marriage over the first's private part except its guardian, and this is permissible in language; Allah the Exalted said: Let his guardian dictate justly. A group said: The guardian of the right is the one to whom the right belongs; so if one to whom a right belongs is called a guardian, one to whom the private part belongs is also called its guardian. Since what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: There is no marriage except with a guardian bears these interpretations, it is negated to divert it to some to the exclusion of others except by an indicating proof: from Book, Sunnah, or consensus. Those who said: There is no marriage except with a guardian also argued for their saying with what follows:"
"3999","4270 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us. (H). 4271 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from the nephew of Maqil, from Maqil ibn Yasar: His sister was under a man who divorced her, then wanted to take her back, but Maqil refused him, so this verse came down: Do not prevent them from marrying their husbands when they agree among themselves in kindness. They said: Since Allah the Exalted commanded her guardian to leave preventing her, that proves contracting her marriage is his. In our view that bears what they said and bears other than it: Maqil's prevention may have been dissuading his sister from remarriage so that she stopped at that, and he was commanded to leave that. Since these reports contain no proof for what the people of the first view held, we examined what is besides them: do we find anything indicating the ruling in this chapter, how it is?"
"4000","4272 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik narrated to him, from Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl, from Nafi ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The previously-married woman (ayyim) has more right over herself than her guardian, and the virgin is consulted about herself, and her permission is her silence."
"4001","4273 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4002","4274 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Mawhab, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) clarified in this hadith by his saying: The ayyim has more right over herself than her guardian that her affair in marrying herself is hers, not her guardian's. There was also narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) in this chapter what indicates this meaning too."
"4003","4275 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us. (H). 4276 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us. (H). 4277 - And Ibn Abi Dawud also narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, both said: Thabit narrated to us, from Umar ibn Abi Salamah, from Umm Salamah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) came to me after Abu Salamah's death and proposed to me for myself. I said: O Messenger of Allah, none of my guardians is present. He said: None of them, present or absent, dislikes that. She said: Stand, O Umar, and marry the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - so he married her to him. This hadith shows the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) proposed to her for herself, proving the affair of marriage is hers, not her guardians'. She only said: None of my guardians is present; he said: None of them, present or absent, dislikes that; so she said: Stand, O Umar, and marry the Prophet, upon him be peace. This Umar is her son, then a small uncoplified child, for she said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) in this hadith: I am a woman with orphans - meaning Umar her son and Zaynab her daughter - and a child has no guardianship, so she appointed him to conclude the marriage upon her, and he did; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) deemed it permissible, and by that agency Umar stood in place of his appointer. So Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, was as if she herself concluded the marriage upon herself for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not wait for her guardians' presence, that proves her private part is hers, not theirs; had they a right or affair in it, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have proceeded upon their right before their permitting it. If one says: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) is worthier of every believer than himself, it is said: True, he is worthier of him than himself, obeyed in more than one obeys oneself; but not that he is worthier of him than himself in concluding upon him a contract without his order, of sale, marriage, or else - his way in that, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family), is like the rulers after him. Had it been so, Umar's agency would have been from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family), not from Umm Salamah, for he would be her guardian; since it was not so, and the agency was from Umm Salamah, by which he concluded the marriage and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) accepted, that proves the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) only owned that private part by Umm Salamah's conferring it, not by a guardianship right in it. Do you not see she said: None of my guardians is present, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) said to her: None of them, present or absent, dislikes that? Had he been worthier of her than them he would not have said that but: I am your guardian besides them; but he did not deny what she said and said to her: They do not dislike that. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of validating the reports' meanings. Once Umm Salamah's, may Allah be pleased with her, concluding marriage upon her private part without her guardians is established permissible, the reports we cited earlier in this chapter must be carried upon this meaning too, so none contradicts another. As for reasoning: before puberty, her father's authority over her private part and wealth is valid, the contracting in all that being his, not hers, with one unvarying ruling; when she reaches puberty all agree his guardianship over her wealth lapses, and what was his of contracting over her wealth in minority reverts to her; by reasoning the contracting over her private part likewise leaves her father's hand at her puberty, what was his before puberty reverting to her, her ruling over wealth and private part equal after puberty, hers not her father's, her ruling uniform after puberty as it was uniform before. This is the reasoning ruling in this chapter, and this is Abu Hanifah's view, may Allah have mercy on him, except that he said: If a woman marries herself to an unsuitable match her guardian may annul it upon her; likewise if she falls short in her dower, marrying below her peers' dower, her guardian may litigate until it reaches her women's peer dower. Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, used to say: A woman's private part is hers, guardianship in contracting marriage upon it for herself being hers not her guardian's; he said her guardian may not object to her shortfall below her peer dower; then he retracted all this to the view of those who said: There is no marriage except with a guardian. His second view is the view of Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on him, and Allah knows best what is correct."
"4004","Chapter 3: A man wanting to marry a woman - is looking at her lawful for him or not? 4278 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab al-Hannat narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Abi Hathmah, from his uncle Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, he said: I saw Muhammad ibn Maslamah pursue Thubaytah bint al-Dahhak atop her roof with his gaze, pursuing intensely. I said: Do you do this being of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When proposing a woman is cast into a man's heart, there is no harm in his looking at her."
"4005","4279 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah, he said: Abd Allah ibn Isa narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abd Allah ibn Yazid, from Abu Humayd - who had seen the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you proposes to a woman, there is no sin on him in looking at her if he only looks at her for the proposal, even if she does not know."
"4006","4280 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Waqid ibn Amr ibn Sad ibn Muadh, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you proposes to a woman, if he can see of her what pleases him, let him do so. Jabir said: I proposed to a woman of Banu Salamah, and would hide - i.e. conceal myself - among palm trunks until I saw of her some of what pleased me, then proposed to her and married her."
"4007","4281 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Kaysan al-Yashkuri narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah: A man wanted to marry a woman of the Ansar women, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Look at her, for in the eyes of the Ansar women is something, meaning smallness."
"4008","4282 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Bakr ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani: al-Mughirah ibn Shubah wanted to marry a woman, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Look at her, for it is more likely to foster affection between you."
"4009","4283 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Asim, from Bakr ibn Abd Allah, from al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, he said: I proposed to a woman, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Did you look at her? I said: No. He said: Look at her, for it is more likely to foster affection between you. Abu Jafar said: These reports permit looking at a woman's face for one wanting to marry her, and a group went with that. Others differed and said: That is not permissible for one wanting to marry a woman, nor for one not wanting her, except being her husband or a mahram relative. They argued with what follows:"
"4010","4284 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Salamah ibn Abi Tufayl, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Ali, you have a treasure in Paradise, and you are possessor of its two horns, so do not follow one glance with another; only the first is yours, the other is not yours."
"4011","4285 - Abu al-Awwam Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid and Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Amr ibn Said, from Abu Zurah ibn Amr ibn Jarir, from Jarir, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) about the sudden glance. He said: Turn your gaze away."
"4012","4286 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Yunus, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4013","4287 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Yunus, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4014","4288 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abu Rabiah al-Iyadi, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, raised the like of it, meaning: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali: O Ali, do not follow one glance with another; only the first is yours, the second is not yours."
"4015","4289 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abu Rabiah, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: The first glance is yours, the latter is against you. They said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) forbade the second glance because it is by the looker's choice, distinguishing its ruling from what precedes it when without the looker's choice, that proves none may look at a woman's face except with marriage or sanctity between them not forbidding that. The reply of the people of the first view: What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) permitted in the first reports is looking for proposal, not otherwise - a look by a lawful cause. Do you not see a man looking at a woman's face with no marriage between them, to testify against or for her, is permissible? Likewise looking at her face to propose is permissible. What is forbidden in Ali's, Jarir's, and Buraydah's hadiths, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them, is for other than proposal and other than what is lawful - disliked and forbidden. We saw them not differ that a man's looking at a slave woman's chest when wanting to buy her is permissible and lawful, for he looks only to buy her, not otherwise; had he looked for other than buying it would be forbidden. Likewise his looking at a woman's face, if for a lawful meaning, is not disliked; if for a forbidden meaning, it is disliked. Once looking at a woman's face to propose is established lawful, its ruling leaves the ruling of awrah, for we saw what is awrah is not permitted to look at for one wanting marriage. Do you not see one wanting to marry a woman is forbidden to look at her hair, chest, or below that of her body, as it is forbidden for one not wanting her? Since looking at her face is lawful for one wanting her, it is also lawful for one not wanting her when his look intends no forbidden meaning. It was said about Allah the Mighty and Majestic's saying: And let them not display their adornment except what appears thereof, that the exception is the face and hands, agreeing with what we mentioned of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) hadith in this interpretation. Among those holding this interpretation is Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on him, as: 4290 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us of that, from his father, from Muhammad. All this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all."
"4016","Chapter 4: Marrying upon a surah of the Quran. 4291 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik narrated to him, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi: A woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O Messenger of Allah, I have offered myself to you - and she stood a long standing. A man stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, marry her to me if you have no need of her. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you have anything to dower her with? He said: I have only this waist-wrap of mine. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you give it to her, you will sit with no wrap; seek something. He said: I find nothing. He said: Seek even an iron ring. He sought and found nothing, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do you have any of the Quran with you? He said: Yes, such-and-such surah and such-and-such surah - he named the surahs - and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: I have married you for what you have of the Quran."
"4017","4292 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, except that he said: I have wedded you with what you have of the Quran."
"4018","4293 - Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to me, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Al-Layth said: This is not permissible after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): marrying for the Quran. Abu Jafar said: A group held marrying upon a named surah of the Quran is permissible, saying its meaning is upon teaching her that surah, and they argued with this hadith. Others differed and said: Whoever marries upon that, the marriage is valid but he is in the ruling of one who named no dower: she has her peer dower if he consummated, or both died, or one died; if he divorces her before consummation she has mutah. Part of their proof against the first view: what is in Sahl's hadith of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I have married you for what you have of the Quran - carrying that upon the apparent, as is the first view's doctrine elsewhere - is upon the surah, not upon teaching it; and if upon the surah, then upon its sanctity, which is no dower at all, just as Abu Talhah married Umm Sulaym upon his Islam."
"4019","4294 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us of that, he said: al-Khattab ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash informed us, from Utbah ibn Humayd, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Anas, from Anas ibn Malik: Abu Talhah married Umm Sulaym upon his Islam, and I mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he deemed it good. That Islam was not a dower in reality; rather the meaning of marrying her upon his Islam is: he married her because of his Islam. Some added in this hadith of Anas: Anas said: By Allah, she had no other dower. Its meaning in our view, and Allah knows best, is: she sought no other dower from him - likewise the meaning of Sahl's hadith about the woman we mentioned. Part of the proof for this view against the first view: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating by the Quran or taking in exchange for it anything of worldly affairs."
"4020","4295 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Mughirah ibn Ziyad informed us, he said: Ubadah ibn Nusayy informed me, from al-Aswad ibn Thalabah, from Ubadah, he said: I used to teach people of the Suffah the Quran, and a man of them gifted me a bow, to accept it in the path of Allah. I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: If you wish Allah to collar you with it a collar of Fire, accept it."
"4021","4296 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Zayd ibn Sallam, from Abu Sallam, from Abu Rashid al-Hubrani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shibl al-Ansari, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Recite the Quran, do not go to excess in it, do not turn away from it, do not eat by it, and do not seek abundance by it."
"4022","4297 - Muhammad Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir. (H). 4298 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Maslamah Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, he said: Ibn Khuzaymah in his hadith, from Zayd, and Ibn Abi Dawud said: Zayd narrated to us. Then both together said: from Abu Sallam, from Abu Rashid al-Hubrani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shibl: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Recite the Quran, do not go to excess in it, and do not eat by it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them taking in exchange for the Quran anything of worldly compensation. That contradicts what the opponent carried the first hadith's meaning upon, even if its meaning were so - and it is not established, since its interpretation bears what we described. It may also bear another meaning: Allah the Mighty and Majestic permitted His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to own the private part without dower, and granted that to none besides him; Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: And a believing woman if she offers herself to the Prophet, if the Prophet wishes to marry her, exclusively for you apart from the believers. So it may be among what Allah the Mighty and Majestic particularized him with that he may confer upon another what he could own without dower, that being particular to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as al-Layth said. Part of what proves that: she said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I have offered myself to you, then that man stood saying: If you have no need of her, marry her to me. That is what was mentioned in that hadith, with no mention that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) consulted her about herself, nor that she said to him: Marry me to him. That proves, if his marrying her to him was not by a later statement after her saying: I have offered myself to you, but only by her first statement - and she had not said to him: I have granted you to give me to whom you will - by the offering which entails no dower, the marriage was valid. All agree the offering is exclusive to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by the particularization we mentioned apart from the believers. Yet a group said: Exclusively for you, i.e. without dower, making the offering a marriage for others entailing dower; others said: Exclusively for you, i.e. the offering is a marriage for you and is not a marriage for others. Since the woman in Sahl's hadith was married by her offering herself to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) as we described, that marriage is established particular, as those holding that said. If one says: It may be that with what we mentioned in the hadith there was a question from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) to her to marry her to him, though not transmitted to us in that hadith, it is said: Likewise it may be the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) assigned her a dower besides the surah, though not transmitted in the hadith. If you carry the hadith upon its apparent as you hold, you are bound by what we mentioned: that marriage was by the offering we described. If you carry it upon interpretation as you described, another may carry it upon interpretation as we mentioned, and you are no worthier by your interpretation than he by his. This is the aspect of this chapter by validating the reports' meanings. As for its aspect by reasoning: we saw marriage upon an unknown dower does not establish the dower, and the woman's ruling reverts to one with no dower named, so the dower must be known, as prices in sales are known and wages in hires are known. The agreed basis: if a man hires a man to teach him a named surah of the Quran for a dirham, it is impermissible; likewise hiring him to teach specified poetry for a dirham is impermissible; for hires are only valid upon one of two meanings: upon a specified work, like washing a specified garment or sewing it, or upon a known time - either time or work must be known. Hiring to teach a surah is a hire upon neither known time nor known work, for he is hired to teach it, and one may learn with little or much teaching, in little or much time. Likewise selling him his house for teaching a surah of the Quran is impermissible, for the meanings we mentioned in hires. Since that is so in hires and sales, and we described that dower is only valid upon wealth or usufructs upon which sale, hire, and else are valid, and teaching confers neither usufructs nor corporeal wealth, it is established by reasoning that private parts are not owned by it either. This is the reasoning, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all."
"4023","Chapter 5: A man freeing his slave woman upon her freedom being her dower. 4299 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Aban and Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, they said: Shuayb ibn al-Habhab narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) freed Safiyyah and made her freedom her dower. Abu Jafar said: A group held that if a man frees his slave woman upon her freedom being her dower, it is valid, and if he marries her she has no dower besides manumission; among those holding this: Sufyan al-Thawri and Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on them both. Others differed and said: None besides the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) may do this so his marriage completes without dower besides manumission; that was particular to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family), for Allah the Mighty and Majestic granted him to marry without dower and granted that to none of the believers besides him; the Mighty and Majestic said: And a believing woman if she offers herself to the Prophet, if the Prophet wishes to marry her, exclusively for you apart from the believers. Since Allah the Mighty and Majestic permitted His Prophet to marry without dower, he could marry upon manumission which is no dower; one Allah did not permit to marry without dower may not marry upon manumission which is no dower. Among those holding this: Abu Hanifah, Zufar, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. Part of their proof: Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) that he did in Juwayriyah the like of what Anas narrated he did in Safiyyah."
"4024","4300 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, he said: Nafi wrote to me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took Juwayriyah in the raid of Banu al-Mustaliq, freed her, married her, and made her freedom her dower; Abd Allah ibn Umar informed me of that, and he was in that army. Ibn Umar, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them both, narrated this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) as we mentioned, then said himself after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) in the like of this that he renews a dower for her."
"4025","4301 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us of that, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the like of that. This Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, held that the ruling in that after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) is other than what was for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family). It may be a hearing he heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family), or the meaning we adduced for the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) particularity in that, as we described, apart from people, may have led him to it. Then we examined the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) manumission of Juwayriyah, whom he married upon it and made it her dower: how was it?"
"4026","4302 - And Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya - i.e. Ibn Abi Zaidah - narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took the captives of Banu al-Mustaliq, Juwayriyah bint al-Harith fell in the share of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas or a cousin of his, and she contracted kitabah upon herself. She said: She was a sweet woman, hardly seen by anyone but she took him; she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking help in her kitabah, and by Allah it was only that I saw her at the chamber door and disliked her, knowing he would see in her like what I saw. She said: O Messenger of Allah, I am Juwayriyah bint al-Harith ibn Abi Dirar, chief of his people, and there befell me of the affair what is not hidden; I fell in the share of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas or a cousin of his, and I contracted kitabah with him, so I came to the Messenger of Allah seeking help in my kitabah. He said: Shall you have better than that? She said: What is it, O Messenger of Allah? He said: I pay your kitabah for you and marry you. She said: Yes. He said: I have done so. The news went out to the people that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Juwayriyah bint al-Harith, and they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has become related by marriage, so they released what was in their hands. She said: By his marrying her he freed a hundred households of Banu al-Mustaliq; we know no woman greater in blessing upon her people than her. Aishah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with her, clarified the manumission Ibn Umar, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them both, mentioned - that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her upon it and made it her dower - how it was: it was only his paying on her behalf her kitabah to the one who contracted it, so she was freed by that payment. Then that manumission effected by the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) payment of the kitabah to her contractor was a dower for her from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as in Ibn Umar's hadith, may Allah be pleased with them both. None besides the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may pay on behalf of a mukatabah her kitabah to her master so she is freed by his payment, with that manumission being a dower for her from the payer, making her thereby his wife. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) could make this a dower as particular to him apart from his nation, he could also make the manumission he himself undertook a dower for whom he freed, as particular to him apart from his nation. This is the aspect of this chapter by reports. As for its aspect by reasoning: Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, said: The reasoning in my view is that manumission is a dower for the freed woman upon it, with nothing else for her - for we saw that when manumission occurs upon her marrying him, then she refuses marriage, she must work for her value. He said: What she must work for when refusing marriage becomes a dower for her when accepting marriage. He said: If he divorces her after that before consummation, she must work for half her value. This was also narrated from al-Hasan."
"4027","4303 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated to us, from Ashath, from al-Hasan, concerning a man who freed his slave woman making her freedom her dower, then divorced her before consummation, he said: She must work for half her value. Part of the proof in this against Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him: what he mentioned of the obligation of working upon her in her value when she refuses - he, Abu Hanifah, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them, have said it; so what bound them in her saying when she accepts marriage binds them both. As for Zufar, he used to say: No working upon them when she refuses, for though he stipulated marriage upon her in the basis of manumission, he only stipulated it upon her with a recompense he stipulated for her upon himself: the dower due her in his view when she accepts; so the manumission fell upon her without recompense, and the stipulated marriage upon her has a recompense besides manumission. That became like a man freeing his slave upon serving him a year for a thousand dirhams, and the slave accepted then refused to serve: nothing is upon him, for had he served he would deserve by his employing him a wage in exchange for service. Likewise, Zufar holding that the slave woman freed upon marriage, when she accepts marriage, deserves a dower in exchange for her private part, then when she refuses nothing is due upon her for her neck, for her neck was freed without recompense, and a marriage with recompense was stipulated upon her; the recompense for marriage is only established by the marriage's establishment, as the recompense for service only by service's establishment. Neither their nullity nor one's nullity entails anything in the manumission which fell upon nothing in exchange. This is the reasoning in this chapter as Zufar said, not as Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all, said. Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani used to hold in the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and upon his family) marrying Safiyyah upon her freedom as Abu Hanifah, Zufar, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all, held too."
"4028","4304 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan freed an umm walad of his making her freedom her dower. I mentioned that to Ayyub and he said: Had he made her freedom absolute? I said: Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not free Safiyyah making her freedom her dower? He said: Had a woman offered herself to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that would be his. I informed Hisham of that, so he made her freedom absolute, married her, and dowered her four hundred. If one says: I saw a man free his slave woman upon wealth, and she accepts from him, so she is free and that wealth is due him upon her - what do you deny that when he frees her upon her freedom being her dower, and she accepts from him, she is free with that wealth due him upon her? It is said to him: When he frees her upon wealth and she accepts from him, manumission is due her from him and wealth is due him upon her, so by that contract each owes the other something the contract conferred, which he did not own before. But when he frees her upon her freedom being her dower, he has conferred upon her her neck, upon her conferring upon him her private part; he conferred a neck he owned for her, which she did not own before, upon her conferring a private part he owned before that - so by that manumission she conferred upon him nothing he did not own before; she only conferred part of what he already had. Likewise nothing is due him upon her by that manumission, and that manumission was no dower for her. This is a proof against one who says she becomes his wife by the manumission which is her dower. As for one who says: She is not his wife except by a fresh marriage after manumission, with the dower due upon her by the manumission, marrying her upon it whenever he wills - the proof against him is to say to him: May her emancipator seize her for the debt of that dower established upon her by the manumission? If he says he may seize her by it, he leaves the saying of all the people of knowledge; if he says he may not seize her by it, it is said: What is the dower the manumission established upon her - wealth or not wealth? If wealth, he may seize her for his wealth upon her whenever he wills; if not wealth, he may not marry her upon non-wealth. Thus the corruption of this view too is established by what we mentioned, and Allah the Exalted knows best."
"4029","Chapter 6: Marriage of mutah. 4305 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn al-Qasim ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, he said: We would go out on campaigns with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) having no women with us, and we said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we not castrate ourselves? He forbade us that, and permitted us to marry for a garment until a term, then recited this verse: Do not forbid the good things Allah has made lawful for you, and do not transgress; indeed Allah does not love transgressors."
"4030","4306 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Bishr informed us, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr preach while alluding to Ibn Abbas, faulting his view on mutah. Ibn Abbas said: Let him ask his mother if he is truthful; so he asked her, and she said: Ibn Abbas spoke truly, that had occurred. Ibn Abbas, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them both, said: Had I wished I could name men of Quraysh born from it."
"4031","4307 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Amr ibn Dinar, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah and Salamah ibn al-Akwa: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to them and permitted them mutah. Abu Jafar said: A group went with these reports and said: There is no harm in a man enjoying a woman for known days for a known thing, and when those days pass she becomes forbidden to him, not by divorce but by expiry of the term they contracted mutah upon, with no mutual inheritance by that in their view. Others differed and said: This marriage is impermissible. They argued that the reports the first view cited against them had existed, then were abrogated after that, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mutah. They cited what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his forbidding it, in which abrogation is not mentioned, what follows:"
"4032","4308 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, he said: Juwayriyah narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri: Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib and al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali informed him that their father informed them he heard Ali ibn Abi Talib say to Ibn Abbas: You are a confused man; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mutah of women."
"4033","4309 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus, Usamah, and Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he did not say: You are a confused man."
"4034","4310 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd Allah and al-Hasan, sons of Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, from their father: Ali passed by Ibn Abbas while he was giving fatwa for mutah - mutah of women - that there is no harm in it. Ali said to him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it, and the flesh of domestic donkeys, on the day of Khaybar."
"4035","4311 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Umar ibn Muhammad al-Umari informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim ibn Abd Allah informed me that a man asked Abd Allah ibn Umar about mutah, and he said: Forbidden. He said: But so-and-so says concerning it, he said: By Allah he knew that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it on the day of Khaybar, and we were not fornicators. These reports contain the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding mutah. So what we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of permission therein may have been before the prohibition, then he forbade it, that prohibition abrogating the earlier permission. We examined that."
"4036","4312 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad al-Laythi narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-Rabi ibn Sabrah al-Juhani, from his father, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Makkah in the Farewell Hajj, and he permitted us mutah. I set out with a companion of mine to a woman of Banu Amir, as if a tall-necked young camel, and we offered ourselves to her. She said: What will you give me? I said: My cloak; my companion said: Two cloaks - his two cloaks being better than mine, while I was younger than him; when she looked at my companion's cloaks they pleased her, and when she looked at me I pleased her, so she said: You and your cloak suffice me, and I stayed with her three days. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has with him any of these women enjoyed in mutah, let him release her."
"4037","4313 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from al-Rabi ibn Sabrah al-Juhani, from his father, the like of it."
"4038","4314 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from al-Zuhri: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mutah of women on the day of the Conquest. I said: From whom did you hear it? He said: A man narrated to me, from his father, in Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz's presence. Mamar claimed it is al-Rabi ibn Sabrah."
"4039","4315 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abd Rabbihi ibn Said, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from al-Rabi ibn Sabrah, from his father: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted mutah, then a man married a woman, and after that he forbids it with severest forbidding, saying about it the severest words."
"4040","4316 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad informed us, he said: Abu Umays narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted mutah of women, then forbade it."
"4041","4317 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Tabuk campaign, and he halted at Thaniyyat al-Wada and saw lamps and weeping women, saying: What is this? It was said: Women whose husbands enjoyed them in mutah then parted from them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has forbidden - or: voided - mutah by divorce, marriage, waiting period, and inheritance. These reports contain the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding mutah after his permitting and allowing it. Thus what we mentioned establishes the abrogation of the first reports we cited at the start of this chapter. It was also narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with them, forbidding it too."
"4042","4318 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Mutah was only a mercy by which Allah showed mercy to this nation; and were it not for Umar ibn al-Khattab's forbidding it, none would fornicate but a wretch. Ata said: As if I hear it from Ibn Abbas: except a wretch."
"4043","4319 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from Khaythamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Mutah of women was only for us particularly."
"4044","4320 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik informed us, from Ata, from Jabir: They used to enjoy women in mutah until Umar forbade them."
"4045","4321 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about mutah of women, and a freedman of his said: That was only on campaign when women were few, and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: You spoke truly. Abu Jafar said: This Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade mutah of women in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and none of them objected to him - proof of their following him in what he forbade, and their consensus on forbidding it is proof of its abrogation. Then this Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, says: It was only permitted when women were few - i.e. when they became many the meaning for which it was permitted lapsed. Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: It was only for us particularly - possibly for the meaning Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, mentioned it was permitted for. As for Jabir's, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: We used to enjoy in mutah until Umar forbade us - he may not have known of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding it until he learned it from Umar's, may Allah be pleased with him, saying. His abandoning what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had permitted them proves the proof of its abrogation and forbidding had reached him. Thus what we mentioned necessitates the abrogation of the permission of women's mutah we narrated at this chapter's start. Some people of knowledge said: If marriage is contracted upon mutah of days, it is valid permanently and the condition is void. Part of the proof against this view: when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them mutah, he said to them: Whoever has with him any of these women enjoyed in mutah, let him part from them. That proves the prior contract does not necessitate permanence of contract forever; had it necessitated permanence, the condition they contracted would be annulled, not the marriage, once established sound and permissible before forbidding. His commanding them to part proves such a contract does not confer ownership of the private part, and this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"4046","Chapter 7: How long a man stays with a previously-married or virgin woman when he marries her. 4322 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, he said: For the virgin seven, and for the previously-married three."
"4047","4323 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid informed us, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, he said: When he marries a virgin while having a previously-married wife, he stays with her seven, then divides; and when he marries a previously-married woman he stays with her three. Khalid said in his hadith: Had I said he raised the hadith I would have spoken truly, but he said: The Sunnah is likewise."
"4048","4324 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, he said: I heard Abu Qilabah narrate from Anas, he said: The Sunnah is: when he marries a virgin he stays with her seven, and when he marries a previously-married woman he stays with her three."
"4049","4325 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, the like of it."
"4050","4326 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Maslamah al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas, he said: For the virgin seven, and for the previously-married three."
"4051","4327 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik informed him, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4052","4328 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, he said: The sunnah of the virgin is seven, and the previously-married three."
"4053","4329 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, he said: When a man marries a virgin while having another, she has seven, then he divides; and when he marries a previously-married woman, three, then he divides."
"4054","4330 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Humayd informed us, he said: I heard Anas say the like of that, adding that he said: Had I said he raised the hadith I would have spoken truly, but he said: The Sunnah is likewise."
"4055","4331 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Humayd informed us, he said: Anas ibn Malik narrated to us: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took Safiyyah bint Huyayy and took her, he stayed with her three. Abu Jafar said: A group held that when a man marries a previously-married woman he has the choice: if he wishes he gives her seven and gives seven to his other wives, and if he wishes he stays with her three and rotates among his remaining wives day by day or night by night; they argued with this hadith and with Umm Salamah's hadith, may Allah be pleased with her."
"4056","4332 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) consummated with Umm Salamah, he said to her: You are not despised by your family; if you wish I give you seven, otherwise I give three, then I rotate."
"4057","4333 - Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us. (H). 4334 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik informed him, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman - i.e. Ibn al-Harith - : When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Umm Salamah and she awoke with him, he said: You are not despised by your family; if you wish I stay seven with you and seven with them, and if you wish I give three then rotate. She said: Give three."
"4058","4335 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Abd Allah ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham narrated to me, from his father, from Umm Salamah: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umm Salamah when he married her: You are not despised by your family; if you wish I give you seven, and if I give you seven I give my wives seven. They said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you wish I give you seven, otherwise I give three, then I rotate, that proves the three is her exclusive right apart from the other women. Others differed and said: If he gives her three he gives three to his other wives, and if he gives her seven he gives seven to his other wives; they argued with Umm Salamah's hadith, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: If I stay seven with you I stay seven with them."
"4059","4336 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us. (H). 4337 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah Musa ibn Ismail al-Munqari narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit. (H). 4338 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Umar ibn Abi Salamah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with them both: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her - when he consummated with her and she awoke with him - : If you wish I give you seven, and if I give you seven I give my wives seven."
"4060","4339 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Jurayj informed us, he said: Habib ibn Abi Thabit informed me that Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Amr and al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman both informed him that they heard Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith report from Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with her, that she informed him, mentioning from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. They said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: If I give you seven I give my wives seven - i.e. I am just between you and them, making seven for each of them as I stayed seven with you - likewise when he gives her three he gives each of them three too. The holders of the first view said: What then is the meaning of his saying: then I rotate? It was said to them: It may mean: then I rotate with the three among them all; for had the three been her exclusive right apart from the other women, then when he stayed seven with her, three of them would be uncounted against her, and the other women would deserve four each. Since what the women get when he stays seven with her is seven each, likewise when he stays three with her each of them gets three. This is the correct reasoning with sound interpretation of these reports upon it, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all."
"4061","4340 - Chapter 8: The Chapter of Coitus Interruptus ('Azl). Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus and Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they both said: 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, she said: Judamah narrated to me, she said: Coitus interruptus was mentioned in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: That is the hidden burial alive (al-wa'd al-khafi)."
"4062","4341 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad informed me, he said: 'Urwah narrated to us, from 'A'ishah, from Judamah bint Wahb al-Asadiyyah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4063","4342 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, he said: Haywah informed us, from Abu al-Aswad, that he heard 'Urwah narrating from 'A'ishah, from Judamah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a group disliked coitus interruptus because of this report narrated regarding its dislike. And others opposed them in that, and saw no harm in it when the free woman permits her husband in it, but if she refuses him that, it is not lawful for him to practice coitus interruptus with her. And another group opposed them in this, and said: He may practice coitus interruptus with her, whether she wishes or refuses. And the first view in this - in our view - is the sounder of the two views; and that is because we consider that the husband may compel the wife to have intercourse with her even if she dislikes that, and he may compel her to fully consummate with her and not practice coitus interruptus with her. So he may compel her to fully consummate with her in his intercourse with her, just as he compels her to intercourse. And the wife may compel her husband to have intercourse with her, so she may compel him to fully consummate with her, just as he may compel her to intercourse and to full consummation. And the right of each of them in that over the other is equal, and it is his right to fully consummate with her in intercourse with her if she loves it and if she dislikes it - that is, hates it. So by analogy - upon what we have mentioned - it is likewise her right upon him also, that he fully consummate with her in his intercourse with her whether he loves that or dislikes it. And this is the correct analogy in this, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And the master, in all their views, according to one who dislikes coitus interruptus as a principle, may have intercourse with his slave woman and practice coitus interruptus with her in his intercourse, and he need not seek her permission in that. And if a man has a wife who is a slave owned by another, and she wants him to practice coitus interruptus with her, then Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them, used to say in that - in what..."
"4064","4343 - Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas narrated to me, from 'Ali ibn Ma'bad, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on them: That permission in that belongs to the master of the slave woman. And a view contrary to this view has been narrated from Abu Yusuf."
"4065","4344 - Ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn Shuja' narrated to me, from al-Hasan ibn Ziyad, from Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on them, he said: Permission in that belongs to the slave woman, not to her master. Ibn Abi 'Imran said: This is the correct analogy upon the principles on which this chapter is built; because if she permitted her husband to abandon intercourse with her, he would have latitude in that, and her master would have no right to compel her husband to have intercourse with her. So since the obligatory intercourse upon her husband belongs to her, and her husband is compelled by her to it, not to her master, likewise the full consummation in that intercourse is claimed to her, not to her master, so this is the correct analogy in this. And all these who permitted coitus interruptus denied what is in the hadith of Judamah of what she narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying in it: That it is the hidden burial alive, and they narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the rejection of that statement against one who said it."
"4066","And they mentioned in that what: 4345 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi 'Abdullah narrated to us. (H). 4346 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Hisham, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Rifa'ah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have a slave girl, and I practice coitus interruptus with her, and I dislike that she become pregnant, and I desire what men desire, and the Jews say: It is the lesser buried-alive female infant. So the Messenger of Allah said to him: The Jews have lied; if Allah intends to create him, you cannot divert it."
"4067","4347 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Muti' ibn Rifa'ah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4068","4348 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: 'Ayyash ibn 'Uqbah al-Hadrami informed me, from Musa ibn Wardan, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: It reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the Jews say: Coitus interruptus is the lesser buried-alive female infant. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Jews have lied, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Even if you consummated fully, it would be only by decree."
"4069","4349 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ayyash al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, and Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: I stationed a slave girl of mine in the market of Banu Qaynuqa', and a Jew passed by me and said: What is this slave girl? I said: A slave girl of mine. He said: Did you have intercourse with her? I said: Yes. He said: Perhaps there is in her belly from you a young lamb? He said: I said: I used to practice coitus interruptus with her. He said: That is the lesser buried-alive female infant. So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and he said: The Jews have lied, the Jews have lied. So this Abu Sa'id, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, has reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the declaring false of one who claimed that coitus interruptus is a buried-alive infant. Then it has been narrated from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, the refutation of that, and the alert to its corruption, with a subtle fine meaning."
"4070","4350 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ma'mar ibn Abi Habibah narrated to me, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Adi ibn al-Khiyar, he said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) discussed coitus interruptus in the presence of 'Umar, and they differed in it. So 'Umar said: You have differed while you are the excellent people of Badr, so how will the people after you be? When two men whispered together, 'Umar said: What is this whispering? He said: The Jews claim that it is the lesser buried-alive female infant. So 'Ali said: It is not a buried-alive infant until it passes through the seven stages: And We created man from an extract of clay, to the end of the verse."
"4071","4351 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ma'mar ibn Abi Habibah, he said: I heard 'Ubayd ibn Rifa'ah al-Ansari, he said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) discussed coitus interruptus, then he mentioned the like of it, (so 'Umar was amazed at his statement, and said: May Allah reward you with good). So 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, informed that there is no buried-alive infant except what the soul has been breathed into before that, and as for what the soul has not been breathed into, it is only a lifeless thing, not a buried-alive infant. And there has been narrated from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, also the like of what we have mentioned, from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4072","4352 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Abu al-Waddak, that some people asked Ibn 'Abbas about coitus interruptus, and he mentioned the like of 'Ali's statement exactly. So this 'Ali and Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, have agreed in this upon what we have mentioned, and 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, followed 'Ali upon what he said of that, and those in their presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So in this is proof that coitus interruptus is not disliked from this angle. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding coitus interruptus also what:"
"4073","4353 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered Khaybar we took women, and we used to have intercourse with them and practice coitus interruptus with them. So some of us said to others: Do you do this while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is beside you, and you do not ask him? He said: So they asked him about that, and he said: Not from all the fluid is the child; when Allah intends to create a thing, nothing prevents Him, so it is no harm upon you not to practice coitus interruptus."
"4074","4354 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from his father, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban narrated to me, that Ibn Muhayriz narrated to him, that Abu Sa'id narrated to him, that some people spoke to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about coitus interruptus, and that was regarding the campaign of Banu al-Mustaliq, when they took captives, and they disliked that they bear children from them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is no harm upon you not to practice coitus interruptus, for Allah has decreed what He will create until the Day of Resurrection."
"4075","4355 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to me, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, he said: My father narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, that Ibn Muhayriz narrated to him, that Abu Sa'id informed them, then he mentioned the like of it."
"4076","4356 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"4077","4357 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Ibn al-Muhayriz, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that they took captives on the day of Awtas, and they wanted to enjoy them without them becoming pregnant. So they asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he said: It is no harm upon you not to do so; for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has written whoever He will create until the Day of Resurrection."
"4078","4358 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhayriz al-Jumahi informed me, that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri informed him, that while he was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man from the Ansar came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, we take captives, and we love the prices, so what do you think about coitus interruptus? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you really do that? It is no harm upon you not to do that; for there is no soul that Allah has decreed to emerge except it will emerge."
"4079","4359 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Anas ibn Sirin, he said: I heard Ma'bad ibn Sirin narrating from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about coitus interruptus, and he said: It is no harm upon you not to do it, for it is only the decree."
"4080","4360 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abu Ishaq al-Sabi'i, he said: I heard Abu al-Waddak narrating from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When we took the captives of Khaybar, we asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about coitus interruptus, and he said: Not from all the fluid is the child; when Allah intends to create a thing, nothing prevents Him."
"4081","4361 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Sa'id, he said: We took captives on the day of Khaybar, and we used to practice coitus interruptus with them, seeking ransom, so we said: If we asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"4082","4362 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Zufar narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu al-'Aliyah, from Abu Sa'id, he said: We discussed coitus interruptus. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and said: It is no harm upon you not to do it, for it is only the decree."
"4083","4363 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they both said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu al-Fayd, he said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn Murrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Zuraqi, that a man from Ashja' asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about coitus interruptus, and he said: Whatever Allah decrees in the womb will be."
"4084","4364 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Abi al-Mughirah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, from Jarir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have only reached you from the polytheists with a singing slave girl of mine - or a slave girl - I practice coitus interruptus with her, intending the market with her, so he said: There has come to her what was decreed. Abu Ja'far said: So in these reports also is what indicates that coitus interruptus is not disliked; because when they informed him that they do it, he did not denounce that upon them, nor forbid them from it, and he said: It is no harm upon you not to do it, for it is only the decree. That is: If Allah has decreed that it will be, that child will be, and neither coitus interruptus nor other than it prevents it; because there may be with coitus interruptus a full consummation with a little fluid which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has decreed a child from, so a child is from it, and the rest of the fluid which they refrain from consummating with by coitus interruptus is surplus. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, may have decreed that there be no child from fluid, so consummating with that fluid and coitus interruptus are equal in that there is no child from it. So consummating with fluid produces no child from it except by its being in Allah's decree that there be no child from that fluid, so it is as decreed. And coitus interruptus, when it has preceded in Allah's decree that there be from that fluid which is interrupted a child, Allah conveys to the womb from it something, even if little, so the child is from it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed them that consummation produces no child by it except if that has preceded in Allah's decree, and that coitus interruptus does not prevent a child when it has preceded in Allah's knowledge that it will be, and he did not forbid them coitus interruptus in all of that."
"4085","Then it has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding its permission also what: 4365 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Jabir, he said: A man from the Ansar came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have a slave girl who walks drawing water upon my camel, and I have intercourse with her, shall I practice coitus interruptus? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, so practice coitus interruptus. The man did not remain but that he came and said: O Messenger of Allah, I practiced coitus interruptus with her and she became pregnant. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has decreed for a soul to create, it will surely be."
"4086","4366 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So this Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, has reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of what Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from him, and those we mentioned with him in the section before this, that he permitted coitus interruptus along with that."
"4087","Then it has been narrated from Jabir, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, regarding the permission of coitus interruptus also what: 4367 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ruwasi narrated to us, from his father, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted coitus interruptus."
"4088","4368 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from 'Ata, from Jabir, he said: We used to practice coitus interruptus in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while the Qur'an was being revealed."
"4089","4369 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, he said: We used to practice coitus interruptus while the Qur'an was being revealed. Shu'bah said: So I said to 'Amr: Did you hear this from Jabir? So he said: No."
"4090","4370 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they both said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: We used to practice coitus interruptus in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he did not forbid us from that. So when the meaning by which coitus interruptus was disliked was negated, and what those who mentioned that it is from the buried-alive infant mentioned, and there was established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we have mentioned from him of its permission, it is established that there is no harm in coitus interruptus for whoever wants it, upon the conditions we mentioned and detailed at the beginning of this chapter. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all."
"4091","4371 - Chapter 9: The Menstruating Woman, What Is Lawful for Her Husband of Her. Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud informed us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command one of us to wrap herself in a waist-wrapper while menstruating, then he would lie with her. Shu'bah said: And he said once: He would touch her."
"4092","4372 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Hurayth ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, from 'A'ishah, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sometimes touch me while I was menstruating, above the waist-wrapper."
"4093","4373 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad informed us, he said: Asbat narrated to us. (H). 4374 - And Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from 'Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from Maymunah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to touch his wives above the waist-wrapper, while they were menstruating."
"4094","4375 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus and al-Layth informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Habib, freedman of 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Nadabah - Ibn Wahb said: al-Layth says: Budayyah, freedwoman of Maymunah - from Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to touch a woman of his wives while she was menstruating, when she had a waist-wrapper reaching to the middle of the thighs or the knees, and in al-Layth's hadith: (wrapped with it)."
"4095","4376 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of what Ibn Wahb mentioned from al-Layth exactly. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that the menstruating woman's husband should not be intimate with her except likewise, nor look at her private parts. And they argued in that with the action of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we mentioned, and among those who held it is Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him."
"4096","4377 - And they argued in that also with what is narrated of the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn 'Amr al-Shami, from one of the group who came to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and they were three, and they asked him: What belongs to a man of his wife when she menstruates? - meaning menstruation. So he said: You asked me about a thing none has asked me about since I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it, and he said: For him of her is what is above the waist-wrapper, of kissing and embracing, and he does not look at what is beneath it."
"4097","4378 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn 'Amr al-Bajali, that some people came to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and asked him, then he mentioned the like of it."
"4098","4379 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, he said: 'Asim ibn 'Amr al-Bajali narrated to us, that some people came to 'Umar, then he mentioned the like of it."
"4099","4380 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad informed us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn 'Amr, from 'Umayr, freedman of 'Umar, from 'Umar, the like of it. And others opposed them in that, and said: There is no harm in what is above the waist-wrapper of her, and what is beneath the waist-wrapper when he avoids the places of blood. And they said: As for what you mentioned of the action of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it is no proof against us in that; because we do not deny that the menstruating woman's husband has of her what is above the waist-wrapper, so this hadith would be proof against us. Rather we say: For him of her is what is above the waist-wrapper and what is beneath it, when he avoids the places of blood, just as he may do that before menstruation occurs. And that hadith is only proof against one who denied that the menstruating woman's husband has of her what is above the waist-wrapper. As for one who permitted that to him, this hadith is not proof against him, and upon you is further proof for your statement: That he has of her only that. For it has been narrated from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, in this, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what agrees with what we went to, and opposes what you went to, and she is one of those you narrated from, of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do with his wives when they menstruated, what you mentioned of that."
"4100","4381 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu Maysarah, from 'A'ishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to touch me while I was in one garment, while I was menstruating, but he was the most controlling of you of his desire, or most controlling of his desire. So this is upon that he used to touch her in one waist-wrapper, so in that is permission of what is beneath the waist-wrapper. So when this came from her, and there had come from her that he used to command her to wrap herself then touch her, this was - in our view - upon that he used to do thus once, and thus once, and in that is permission of both meanings together. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from another route, what agrees with this view we authenticated upon it the two hadiths of 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, for those we mentioned."
"4101","4382 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, that the Jews would not eat, nor drink, nor sit with menstruating women in a house. So that was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: And they ask you about menstruation, say: It is harm, so keep away from women in menstruation. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do everything, except intercourse. So in this hadith, that they had been permitted of the menstruating woman everything of her, except her intercourse specifically, and that is upon intercourse in the private part, not what is besides it. And this statement itself has been narrated from 'A'ishah, may Allah Almighty be pleased with her."
"4102","4383 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, that a man asked 'A'ishah: (What is lawful for a man of his wife when she is menstruating?) So she said: (Everything except her private part)."
"4103","4384 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Khalid informed us, he said: 'Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, from Masruq, from 'A'ishah, the like of that."
"4104","4385 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Bukayr, from Abu Murrah, freedman of 'Aqil, from Hakim ibn 'Iqal, he said: I asked 'A'ishah: (What is forbidden to me of my wife when she menstruates?) She said: Her private part. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of authenticating the meanings of the reports. As for its aspect by way of analogy, we consider the woman before menstruation, her husband may have intercourse with her in her private part, and he has of her what is above the waist-wrapper, and what is beneath the waist-wrapper also. Then when she menstruates, intercourse in her private part becomes forbidden to him, and what is above the waist-wrapper becomes lawful to him by their agreement. And they differed regarding what is beneath the waist-wrapper upon what we mentioned, so some permitted it, making its ruling the ruling of what is above the waist-wrapper, and some prohibited it, making its ruling the ruling of intercourse in the private part. So when they differed in that, analogy was necessary, to know which of the two aspects it most resembles, so it be judged with its ruling. So we consider intercourse in the private part obligates the hadd, the dower, and ghusl, and we consider intercourse in other than the private part obligates none of that, and the ruling of what is above the waist-wrapper and what is beneath it is equal in that. So it is established by what we mentioned that the ruling of what is beneath the waist-wrapper more resembles what is above the waist-wrapper than intercourse in the private part. So by analogy upon that it is likewise in the ruling of the menstruating woman, its ruling being the ruling of intercourse above the waist-wrapper, not the ruling of intercourse in the private part. And this is the view of Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on him, and we take it. Abu Ja'far, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Then I looked after that in this chapter, and in authenticating the reports in it, and they indicate what Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, went to, not what Muhammad went to. And that is that we found them of three types: a type of them what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to touch his wives while menstruating above the waist-wrapper, so there was in that no proof of prohibiting the menstruating woman from touching beneath the waist-wrapper, for what we mentioned in its place of this chapter. And another type of them, and it is what 'Umayr, freedman of 'Umar, narrated from 'Umar, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), upon what we mentioned in its place. So in that was proof of prohibiting intimacy with menstruating women beneath the waist-wrapper; because what is in it of the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and his mentioning what is above the waist-wrapper, is only an answer to 'Umar's question to him, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him: What belongs to a man of his wife when menstruating? So he said: For him is what is above the waist-wrapper, so that was an answer to his question, with no deficiency nor shortcoming in it. And another type which is what is narrated from Anas, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, upon what we mentioned from him, so that permits intimacy with menstruating women besides the private part, even if beneath the waist-wrapper. So we wanted to see which of these two types is later than the other, to make it abrogating it. So we looked in that, and the hadith of Anas in it is information of what the Jews were upon, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to love agreeing with the People of the Book in what he had not been commanded to oppose them in - we have narrated that from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, in the Book of Funerals - and likewise Allah Almighty commanded him in His saying: Those are they whom Allah guided, so follow their guidance. So upon him was following those before him of the prophets until a law comes to him abrogating their law. So what abrogated what the Jews were upon of avoiding the speech of the menstruating woman and eating with her and gathering with her in a house, is what is in the hadith of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, with no intermediary between them. So in this hadith of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, is permission of intimacy with her in other than the private part. And what is in the hadith of 'Umar is permission for what is above the waist-wrapper, and prohibition of what is beneath the waist-wrapper. So it is impossible that it be prior to the hadith of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, when the hadith of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, is the abrogator of avoiding gathering with the menstruating woman, and eating and drinking with her. So it is established: That it is later than it, and abrogating part of what was permitted in it. So by that is established what Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, went to of this by authenticating the reports, and what Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on him, went to is negated."
"4105","4386 - Chapter 10: The Chapter of Intercourse with Women in Their Behinds. Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd informed us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sa'd, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id, that a man had intercourse with his wife in her behind, so the people denounced that upon him, and said: Are you leaving her unused, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: Your wives are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that intercourse with the woman in her behind is permissible. And they argued in that with this hadith, and interpreted this verse upon permission of that. And others opposed them in that, and disliked intercourse with women in their behinds, and prohibited that, and interpreted this verse upon other than this interpretation."
"4106","4387 - So Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, that the Jews said: Whoever comes to his wife in her private part from her behind, his child comes out squint-eyed, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: Your wives are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish."
"4107","4388 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, that Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to him, from Jabir, the like of it."
"4108","4389 - Muhammad ibn Zakariyya Abu Shurayh narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"4109","4390 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, he said: The Jews said: When a man comes to his wife kneeling, the child comes squint-eyed, so that was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. They said: So what was from the Jews' statement is what we mentioned, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed that in refutation of their statement, and permission of intercourse in the private part from the behind and from the front together. And others have narrated this hadith from Ibn al-Munkadir, upon what we mentioned, and added in it: When that is in the private part."
"4110","4391 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Ubayy narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Rashid narrating from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, that a Jew said: (When a man marries a woman bending forward, her child comes out squint-eyed), so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: Your wives are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish, if you wish bending forward, and if you wish not bending forward, when that is in one opening."
"4111","4392 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, that Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to him, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, that the Jews said to the Muslims: Whoever comes to his wife while she is turned away, her child comes squint-eyed, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: Your wives are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Facing and turned away, as long as it is in the private part. So in the Prophet's directing them in that to the private part, is information from him to them that the behind is contrary to that. And it has been said in the interpretation of this verse also other than this interpretation."
"4112","4393 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Za'idah, he said: I asked Ibn 'Abbas about coitus interruptus and he said: Your wives are a tilth for you, if you wish then practice coitus interruptus, and if you wish then do not practice it. And among the proof of the people of the first view also for their statement in that, is what has been narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, may Allah Almighty be pleased with them both, of permission of that."
"4113","4394 - As Abu Qurrah Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj and Abu Zayd 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Ghamr narrated to us, they both said: Ibn al-Qasim said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to me, he said: Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Abu al-Hubab Sa'id ibn Yasar, that he asked Ibn 'Umar about it, meaning about intercourse with women in their behinds, and he said: There is no harm in it. Abu Ja'far said: This has been narrated from Ibn 'Umar, as you mentioned, and contrary to that has been narrated from him."
"4114","4395 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us. (H). 4396 - And Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, they both said: al-Layth narrated to us - Ibn Wahb said in his hadith from al-Harith ibn Ya'qub, and 'Abdullah ibn Salih said, he said: al-Harith ibn Ya'qub narrated to me - from Sa'id ibn Yasar Abu al-Hubab, he said: I said to Ibn 'Umar, what do you say about slave girls, sour milk is prepared for them, he said: And what is souring? So I mentioned the behind. So he said: Does any of the Muslims do that? So this from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah Almighty be pleased with them both, has contradicted what the people of the first view narrated from him, of what we mentioned in that. And the proof of the soundness of this is Salim ibn 'Abdullah's denial that that was from his father."
"4115","4397 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: 'Attaf ibn Khalid informed us, from Musa ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn al-Hasan, that his father asked Salim ibn 'Abdullah to narrate to him the hadith of Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he saw no harm in coming to women in their behinds. So Salim said: The slave has lied or erred; he only said: (There is no harm in coming to them in their private parts, from their behinds). And Maymun ibn Mihran said: Nafi' only said that after he grew old and his mind went. 4398 - Fahd narrated that to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Maymun ibn Mihran. So what is more than this is weakened by less than the statement of Maymun. And Nafi' denied it initially, against one who narrated it from him also."
"4116","4399 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya, scribe of al-'Umari, narrated to us, he said: al-Mufaddal ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ayyash, from Ka'b ibn 'Alqamah, from Abu al-Nadr, that he informed him that he said to Nafi', freedman of 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar: Much has been said upon you, that you say from Ibn 'Umar, that he gave fatwa that women be come to in their behinds. Nafi' said: They have lied upon me, but I will inform you how the matter is: Ibn 'Umar عرض the mushaf one day while I was with him until he reached: Your wives are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish, and he said: O Nafi', do you know of the matter of this verse? I said: No. He said: We, the company of Quraysh, used to bend women forward, then when we entered Madinah and married the women of the Ansar, we wanted from them the like of what we wanted, and they disliked that and deemed it grave, and the women of the Ansar had taken the manner of the Jews, and they were only come to upon their sides, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: Your wives are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish. So in this hadith is Nafi''s denial of what has been narrated from him from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah Almighty be pleased with them both, of permission of intercourse with women in their behinds, and information from him from Ibn 'Umar, that the interpretation of His saying: Your wives are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish, is not upon what the people of the first view interpreted, but upon permission, and upon women by the parts of their private parts. And there has been narrated from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, also the like of that."
"4117","4400 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il, Abu Salamah al-Tabudhaki, narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit, he said: I came to Hafsah bint 'Abd al-Rahman, and I said to her: I want to ask you about a thing and I am shy of you, so she said: Ask, O son of my brother, about whatever occurs to you. I said: About coming to women in their behinds. She said: Umm Salamah narrated to me that the Ansar would not bend forward, and the Emigrants would bend forward, and the Jews used to say: Whoever bends forward, his child comes out squint-eyed. Then when the Emigrants came to Madinah, they married the women of the Ansar, so a man of the Emigrants married a woman of the Ansar and bent her forward, and she refused, and she came to Umm Salamah and mentioned that to her. Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, Umm Salamah mentioned that to him, and the Ansari woman was shy and went out. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Call her, so she called her, and he said: Your wives are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish, in one opening. So Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, has informed of the interpretation of this verse also, and of the Prophet's directing it with his saying: In one opening. So that is proof that the ruling of the opposite of that opening is contrary to the ruling of that opening, and were it not that, his saying: In one opening would have no meaning. And there has been narrated from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, in the interpretation of this verse what returns in meaning to this meaning also."
"4118","4401 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that 'Amir ibn Yahya al-Ma'afiri narrated to him that Hanash ibn 'Abdullah al-Saba'i narrated to him that he heard Ibn 'Abbas, that some people of Himyar came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking him about women, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: Your wives are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Come to her facing and turned away, when that is in the private part. Then the reports came successively with prohibition of coming to women in their behinds. Of that is what:"
"4119","4402 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Ibn al-Had, from 'Umarah ibn Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is not shy of the truth; do not come to women in their behinds."
"4120","4403 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: 'Umar, freedman of Ghufrah bint Rabah, sister of Bilal, mu'adhdhin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to me, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ali ibn al-Sa'ib, from 'Ubaydullah ibn al-Husayn, from 'Abdullah ibn Harami al-Khatmi, from Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned the like of it."
"4121","4404 - Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Ali narrated to me, he said: I was with Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi, and a man asked him and said: O Abu Hamzah, what do you think about coming to women in their behinds? So he turned away or was silent. So he said: This is the shaykh of Quraysh, so ask him - meaning 'Abdullah ibn 'Ali ibn al-Sa'ib - so 'Abdullah said: (O Allah, filthy, even if it were lawful). My grandfather said: And he had not heard anything in that. He said: Then 'Abdullah ibn 'Ali informed me, that he met 'Amr ibn Abi Uhayhah ibn al-Julah, and asked him about that, and he said: I bear witness that I heard Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, whose testimony the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the testimony of two men, saying: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I come to my wife from her behind? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, he said it twice or thrice. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) discerned, and said: In which of the two holes or in which of the two openings? As for from her behind in her front, then yes, and as for in her behind, Allah Almighty has forbidden you to come to women in their behinds."
"4122","4405 - 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Ufayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Husayn al-Ansari then al-Wa'ili narrated to me, from Harami ibn 'Abdullah al-Wa'ili, from Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Do not come to women in their behinds."
"4123","4406 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Haywah and Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, they both said: Hassan, freedman of Muhammad ibn Sahl, informed us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ali, from Harami ibn 'Amr al-Khatmi, from Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4124","4407 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"4125","4408 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, he said: Haywah informed us, he said: Hassan informed us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"4126","4409 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Hassan, freedman of Sahl ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, from Sa'id, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"4127","4410 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: It is the lesser sodomy, meaning intercourse with women in their behinds."
"4128","4411 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from al-Harith ibn Mukhallad, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not come to women in their behinds."
"4129","4412 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from al-Harith ibn Mukhallad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, does not look at a man who had intercourse with a woman in her behind."
"4130","4413 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'ah informed us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh informed us, he said: Yazid ibn al-Had informed me, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Except that he said: His wife."
"4131","4414 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn al-Had, from Suhayl, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"4132","4415 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash informed us, from Suhayl, from al-Harith ibn Mukhallad, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever comes to a menstruating woman or a woman in her behind, or a soothsayer, has disbelieved in what Allah sent down upon Muhammad."
"4133","4416 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hakim al-Athram, from Abu Tamimah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever comes to a menstruating woman or a woman in her behind, or a soothsayer, has disbelieved in what was sent down upon Muhammad."
"4134","4417 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is not shy of the truth; do not come to women in their private rear openings."
"4135","4418 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, and 'Umar, freedman of 'Ufrah, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is not shy of the truth; coming to women in their rear passages is not lawful, that is: their behinds."
"4136","4419 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah informed us, from 'Asim al-Ahwal, from 'Isa ibn Hittan, from Muslim ibn Sallam, from 'Ali ibn Talq, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is not shy of the truth; do not come to women in their buttocks."
"4137","4420 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from 'Asim al-Ahwal. (H). 4421 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from 'Asim al-Ahwal, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"4138","And the people of the first view also argued for their statement with what: 4422 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn al-Muhajir, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi, that he saw no harm in coming to women in their behinds, and he argued in that with His saying, Mighty and Majestic: Do you come to males among the worlds, and leave what your Lord created for you of your wives; rather you are a transgressing people, that is: of your wives the like of that, if you desire. It was said to them: And who agrees with Muhammad ibn Ka'b upon this interpretation? His opponents have said: And leave what your Lord created for you of your wives, of what He has made lawful for you of intercourse with them in their private parts. And this interpretation - in our view - is worthier than the first interpretation, for its agreement with what came from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what we mentioned. And if we must follow Muhammad ibn Ka'b in this statement, then following Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib is worthier."
"4139","4423 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, or Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman - and most of my conjecture is that it is Abu Bakr - used to forbid coming to the woman in her behind with the strongest prohibition, and how, when one greater than them both has said that?"
"4140","4424 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Abu al-Qa'qa' al-Jarmi, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: The rear passages of women are unlawful."
"4141","4425 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to me, from Qatadah, from Abu Ayyub, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, he said regarding one who comes to his wife in her behind, he said: (The lesser sodomy). And what is in this chapter from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and may He be pleased with them and their followers in agreement with this meaning up to here, is more than can be fully covered, but we have omitted that from our book for its abundance and length. So when these reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) successively established prohibition of intercourse with the woman in her behind, then came from his Companions, and from their followers what agrees with that, the statement with it became obligatory, and abandoning what opposes it. And this also is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all, and Allah knows best the correct."
"4142","4426 - Chapter 11: The Chapter of Intercourse with Pregnant Women. Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Ghaniyyah, 'Abd al-Malik ibn Humayd, narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Muhajir al-Ansari, from his father, from Asma bint Yazid al-Ansariyyah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Do not kill your children secretly, for killing by ghayl (intercourse while nursing/pregnant) overtakes the brave horseman - that is: the courageous - and throws him off from the back of his horse."
"4143","4427 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Muhajir, from his father, from Asma bint Yazid ibn al-Sakan al-Ansariyyah, she said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Do not kill your children secretly, for killing by ghayl overtakes the horseman upon the back of his horse, and throws him off. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to this, and disliked a man's intercourse with his wife or slave girl when pregnant, and argued in that with this hadith. And others opposed them in that, and said: There is no harm in that. And they argued in that with what."
"4144","4428 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr informed me, from 'Amir ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, that Usamah ibn Zayd informed his father Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I practice coitus interruptus with my wife. He said: Why? He said: Out of compassion for the child. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If it were that, then no; it would not harm Persia and Rome. So in this hadith is permission of intercourse with pregnant women, and information from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that that, when it does not harm Persia and Rome, does not harm others. So this hadith opposed the hadith of Asma, so we wanted to see which of them is the abrogator of the other, so we looked in that."
"4145","So we found Yunus had: - narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik informed him. 4430 - And we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had: - narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us. (H) 4431 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, from Judamah bint Wahb, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I had intended to forbid ghaylah until I remembered that Persia and Rome do that, and it does not harm their children."
"4146","4432 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, he said: Abu al-Aswad Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal narrated to me, he said: 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, from 'A'ishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from Judamah bint Wahb al-Asadiyyah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he intended to forbid ghayl, he said: Then I looked and Persia and Rome practice ghayl, and that does not harm their children."
"4147","4433 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus and Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they both said: al-Muqri narrated to us - meaning: Abu 'Abd al-Rahman - he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, that she said: Judamah narrated to me, and he mentioned the like of it."
"4148","4434 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, he said: Haywah informed us, from Abu al-Aswad, that he heard 'Urwah narrating from 'A'ishah, may Allah Almighty be pleased with her, from Judamah, may Allah Almighty be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So in this hadith that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended prohibition of that, until it reached him, or until he remembered that Persia and Rome do it, and it does not harm their children. So in that is permission of what the first hadith prohibited. And it is possible that one of the two matters is abrogating of the other."
"4149","4435 - So we looked in that, and Rawh ibn al-Faraj had: - narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from 'Ata, from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid ghayl, then he said: If it harmed anyone, it would harm Persia and Rome. So by this hadith permission after prohibition is established, so this is worthier than other than it, and the Prophet's prohibition of that came from his fear of harm because of it, then he permitted it when it was verified with him that it does not harm. And that indicated that what he prohibited was not by way of revelation, nor by way of what is lawful and unlawful, but upon the way of what occurred in his heart, peace be upon him, of something, so he commanded by it out of compassion from him upon his nation, nothing other than that, as he had commanded regarding abandoning pollination of date palms. For he had:"
"4150","4436 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, he said: Simak narrated to us, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, that he said: I passed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the date palms of Madinah, and people were at the tops of the palms pollinating the palms. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What are these doing? So it was said: They take from the male and place it in the female, so he said: I do not think that benefits anything, so it reached them, and they abandoned it and came down from them. So they did not bear that year. So that reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: It was only a conjecture I conjectured; if it benefits anything then let them do it, for I am only a human like you, and it was only a conjecture I conjectured, and conjecture errs and is correct, but what I say to you: Allah said, I will never lie upon Allah."
"4151","4437 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn 'Abdah narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Jumay' informed us, he said: Simak narrated to us, that he heard Musa ibn Talhah narrating from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4152","4438 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid and Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he narrated the like of it."
"4153","4439 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Simak, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in this hadith that what he said by way of conjecture, he is in it like the rest of people in their conjectures, and that what he says of what is not contrary to what he says is what he says from Allah, Mighty and Majestic. So when his prohibition of ghaylah was for what he feared of harm to the children of pregnant women, then he permitted it when he knew it does not harm them, that indicated that what he prohibited was not from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and that had it been from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, he would have stood him upon its reality. But it was from his conjecture which he stood after upon that what is in reality of what he prohibited what he prohibited of that because of, is contrary to what occurred in his heart of that. So it is established by what we mentioned that a man's intercourse with his wife and slave girl while pregnant is lawful, never forbidden to him. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"4154","4440 - Chapter 12: The Chapter of Seizing What Is Scattered upon the People of What People Do in Marriage. Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from al-Sunabihi, from 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pledged us upon that there be no seizing by force."
"4155","4441 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from 'Imran ibn Husayn, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever seizes by force is not of us."
"4156","4442 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far al-Razi narrated to us, from al-Rabi' ibn Anas, and Humayd from Anas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade plundering, and said: Whoever plunders is not of us."
"4157","4443 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from a freedman of Juhaynah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade snatching and plundering."
"4158","4444 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Simak ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Tha'labah ibn al-Hakam, brother of Banu Layth, informed me, that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pass by pots in which was sheep meat they had plundered, so he commanded with them and they were overturned, and he said: Plundering is not lawful."
"4159","4445 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Simak, from Tha'labah ibn al-Hakam, he said: The people took sheep in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and plundered them, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Plundering is not right, then he commanded with the pots and they were overturned."
"4160","4446 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, he said: Simak narrated to us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"4161","4447 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, he said: My father and others narrated to us, from Simak, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that when a man scatters something upon people, and permits them taking it: Its taking is disliked for them, and unlawful upon them, and they went in that to that it is of the plundering which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade in these reports. And others opposed them in that, and said: The plundering which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade in these reports is plundering of what taking it was not permitted in. As for what a man scattered upon people and permitted them its plundering and taking, it is not likewise; because it is permitted in, and the former is prohibited from. And we have found the like of that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted."
"4162","4448 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they both said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, he said: Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Rashid ibn Sa'd, from 'Abdullah ibn Luhayy, from 'Abdullah ibn Qurt, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most beloved days to Allah are the day of sacrifice, then the day of 'Arafah. So I brought near to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) five or six camels, and they began crowding to him, with which of them he begins, so when they fell, that is: their sides fell, he said a light word I did not understand. So I said to one beside me: What did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say? So he said: He said: Whoever wishes, let him cut off. So when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in this hadith: Whoever wishes, let him cut off, and permitted that, this indicated that what its owner permitted to people of food or other than it, they may take of that, and this is contrary to the plundering he forbade in the first reports. So it is established by what we mentioned that the plundering in the first reports is plundering of what was not permitted in, and that what was permitted of that and allowed in is upon what is in this second report. And there has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a disconnected hadith interpreting the ruling of the forbidden plundering and the permitted plundering, and we only intended by mentioning it here its interpretation of the meaning of this connected one."
"4163","4449 - 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Mu'awiyah al-'Attabi narrated to us, he said: 'Awn ibn 'Umarah narrated to us, he said: Limazah narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) attended the wedding of a young man of the Ansar, and when they married him he said: Upon affection and auspicious fortune and abundance in provision, may Allah bless you, scatter upon your companion's head, and it was not long before slave girls came with dishes upon them almonds and sugar, and the people withheld their hands. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Will you not seize? So they said: O Messenger of Allah, you had forbidden plundering, he said: That is the plundering of armies, as for weddings then no. He said: So I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pulling with them and they pulling with him. And there has been narrated from a group of the predecessors in that disagreement also."
"4164","4450 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Isra'il informed us, from Abu Husayn, from 'Abdullah ibn Sinan, that Abu Mas'ud had young boys in the school, and he wanted them to seize upon them, so he bought for them walnuts for two dirhams, and disliked that they seize with the boys. So it is possible that that is upon his fear for them of plundering, not for other than that."
"4165","4451 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, that he used to like sugar to be placed at the wedding, and dislike that it be scattered."
"4166","4452 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from Husayn, from 'Ikrimah that he disliked it."
"4167","4453 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, he said: I was walking between Ibrahim and al-Sha'bi, and they discussed scattering at the wedding, so Ibrahim disliked it, and al-Sha'bi did not dislike it. So it is also possible that Ibrahim disliked that because of what we mentioned of fear of harm upon those seizing."
"4168","4454 - So we looked in that, and Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman had: narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, regarding seizing at the wedding, he said: (They used to take it for the boys). So what is narrated from Ibrahim in this indicated, with his mentioning from those before him of those taken as example, that they used to take it for the boys in this hadith, that his dislike of it in the first section is not from the angle of its prohibition, but from the angle of what we mentioned."
"4169","4455 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, that he saw no harm in that."
"4170","4456 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, from Ash'ath, from al-Hasan, he said: There is no harm in seizing walnuts, and Muhammad ibn Sirin said: They place in their hands. And what in it is permission of these reports - in our view - is worthier in analogy than what in it is dislike of them, and this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them."
"4171","8 - Book of Divorce. 1 - Chapter of a Man Divorcing His Wife While Menstruating Then Wanting to Divorce Her According to the Sunnah, When Is That for Him? 4457 - Abu Bakrah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they both said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, he said: I heard 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ayman asking 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar about a man divorcing his wife while menstruating, he said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar did that. So 'Umar asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he said: Command him to take her back until she is pure, then divorce her, he said: Then he recited: When you divorce women, divorce them for their waiting period, that is: In the beginning of their waiting period."
"4172","4458 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, he said: Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, freedman of the family of Talhah, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar, that he divorced his wife while menstruating, so 'Umar asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Command him to take her back, then divorce her while she is pure, or pregnant."
"4173","4459 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Bishr narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: I divorced my wife while menstruating, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned her to me until I divorced her while she was pure."
"4174","4460 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"4175","4461 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Yunus ibn Jubayr, he said: I asked Ibn 'Umar about a man divorcing his wife while menstruating, and he said: Do you know 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar? I said: Yes. He said: He divorced his wife while menstruating, so 'Umar came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and he said: Command him to take her back, and when she is pure, divorce her. I said: And is that divorce counted? He said: What then? Have you seen if he was incapable and foolish? And Abu Bakrah did not mention in this hadith of his other than what we mentioned in it."
"4176","4462 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, he said: Anas ibn Sirin informed me, he said: I heard Ibn 'Umar saying: Ibn 'Umar divorced his wife while menstruating, so 'Umar mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Command him to take her back, and when she is pure then divorce her, so it was said: Is it counted by it? He said: What then?"
"4177","4463 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Nufayli narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us, from Anas ibn Sirin, he said: I asked Ibn 'Umar: How did you do with your wife whom you divorced? He said: I divorced her while menstruating, so that was mentioned to 'Umar, so 'Umar came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him, and he said: Command him to take her back, then divorce her at purity. He said: So I said: May I be made your ransom, is the first divorce counted? He said: And what prevents me if I did wrong and was foolish."
"4178","4464 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: Yunus - he is Ibn Jubayr - narrated to me, he said: I asked 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, I said: A man divorcing his wife while menstruating? He said: Do you know 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar? So I said: Yes, he said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar divorced his wife while menstruating, so 'Umar came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to take her back, then divorce her at the beginning of her waiting period. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to these reports, and said: Whoever divorces his wife while menstruating has sinned, and he should take her back, for his divorce is an erroneous divorce, and if he leaves her to proceed in the waiting period, she is separated from him by an erroneous divorce, but he is commanded to take her back, to remove her by that from the causes of erroneous divorce, then leave her until she is pure from this menstruation, then divorce her a correct divorce, so she proceeds in a waiting period from a correct divorce, and if he wishes he takes her back, and she is his wife and the waiting period is void, and if he wishes he leaves her until she is separated from him by a correct divorce. So this is the view of Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him. And others opposed them in that, among them Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, and claimed that when he divorces her menstruating, he has no right after that to divorce her until she is pure from this menstruation, then menstruates another menstruation, then is pure from it. And they opposed the reports we narrated in agreement with the first view."
"4179","With what: 4465 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they both said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: 'Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim ibn 'Abdullah informed me, that 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar informed him that he divorced a wife of his while menstruating, so 'Umar mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was angered at him, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let him take her back, then keep her until she is pure, then menstruates and is pure, and if it appears to him to divorce her, let him divorce her pure before touching her, so that is the waiting period as Allah commanded."
"4180","4466 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"4181","4467 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he divorced his wife while menstruating, in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so 'Umar asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he said: Command him to take her back, then keep her until she is pure, then menstruates, then is pure, so that is the waiting period which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded women be divorced for."
"4182","4468 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Except that he said: Then leave her until she is pure, then menstruates, then is pure, then if he wishes he divorces."
"4183","4469 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub and 'Ubaydullah. (H). 4470 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, and 'Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4184","4471 - Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, from Zuhayr ibn Muhammad, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id, and Musa ibn 'Uqbah, and 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar informed me, from Nafi', that 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, then he mentioned the like of it and added: Before consummating with her."
"4185","4472 - Fahd and Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, they both said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Nafi' narrated to me that 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, then he mentioned the like of it. So Salim and Nafi' have informed, from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, in these reports, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to keep her until she is pure, then menstruates, then is pure. So that added upon what is in the first reports, so it is worthier than them. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for its aspect by way of analogy, we found the principle in that that a man is forbidden to divorce his wife menstruating, and forbidden to divorce her in a purity in which he has divorced her, and he has been forbidden from divorce in the purity in which he divorced her, as he has been forbidden from divorce in menstruation. Then we saw them not differing, regarding a man who had intercourse with his wife menstruating, then wanted to divorce her according to the Sunnah, that he is prohibited from that until she is pure from this menstruation in which intercourse was, and from another menstruation after it, and his intercourse with her in menstruation was made like his intercourse with her in the purity following that menstruation. So when the ruling of the purity after every menstruation is like the ruling of the menstruation itself in divorce occurring in intercourse in that, and one who had intercourse with his wife while menstruating has no right to divorce her after that, until there be between that intercourse and the divorce he effects a complete future menstruation. Likewise it is in analogy that when he divorces his wife while menstruating, then wants after that to divorce her, he has no right to that until there be between the first divorce with which he divorced her, and his second divorce of her, a future menstruation. So this is the aspect of analogy - in our view - in this chapter with agreement of the reports, and it is the view of Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him. And in the Prophet's prohibiting Ibn 'Umar from divorcing his wife after the first divorce, until there be after that future menstruations, so there be between the two divorces a future menstruation, is proof that the ruling of Sunnah divorce is that two divorces not be combined in one purity. So understand that; for it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all."
"4186","4473 - Chapter 2: The Chapter of a Man Divorcing His Wife Thrice Together. Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, that Abu al-Sahba said to Ibn 'Abbas: Do you know that the three used to be made one in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr, and three from the rule of 'Umar? Ibn 'Abbas said: Yes. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that when a man divorces his wife thrice together, one has occurred upon her if she is in a time of Sunnah, and that is that she be pure without intercourse. And they argued in that with this hadith, and said: Since Allah, Mighty and Majestic, only commanded His servants to divorce for a time upon a description, and they divorced upon other than what He commanded them, their divorce does not occur. And they said: Do you not see that if a man commanded a man to divorce his wife at a time upon a description, and he divorced her in other than it, or commanded him to divorce her upon a condition, and he divorced her upon other than that condition, that that does not occur; since he opposed what was commanded. They said: So likewise divorce which the servants were commanded with, so when they effect it as commanded it occurs, and when they effect it contrary to that it does not occur. And most of the people of knowledge opposed them in that, and said: What the servants were commanded with of effecting divorce, it is as you mentioned, when the woman is pure without intercourse, or pregnant, and they were commanded to separate the three when they want to effect them, and not effect them together. So when they oppose that, and divorce in the time they should not divorce in, and effect of divorce more than commanded to effect, what they effected of that binds them, and they are sinful in transgressing what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded them. And that is not like agencies; because agents only do that for those appointing them, so they stand in those actions of theirs in their place, so if they do that as commanded it binds, and if they do that upon other than commanded it does not bind. And the servants in their divorce only do it for themselves, not for other than them, not for their Lord, Mighty and Majestic, nor do they stand in their doing that in the place of other than them, so there be sought from them in that hitting what those they stand in their doing that in place of commanded them. So when that was likewise, what they did bound them, even if that was of what they were forbidden from; because we have seen things of what Allah Almighty forbade servants from doing, He obligated upon them when they did them rulings. Of that that He forbade them from zihar, and described it as a denied statement and falsehood, and what was likewise did not prevent that the woman be forbidden by it upon her husband, until he does what Allah Almighty commanded him of expiation. So when we saw zihar a denied and false statement, and a prohibition bound by it, likewise the forbidden divorce is a denied statement and falsehood, and prohibition by it is obligatory. And we have seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, asked him about 'Abdullah's divorce of his wife while menstruating, commanded him to take her back, and the reports successively established from him with that, and I have mentioned them in the first chapter, and it is not permissible to command taking back one whose divorce did not occur. So when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bound him with divorce in menstruation, and it is a time divorce effecting in it is not lawful, likewise whoever divorces his wife thrice, and effects each in the time of divorce, binds him of that what he bound himself, even if he did it contrary to commanded. So this is the analogy in this chapter. And in the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, is what if we sufficed with it would be decisive proof, and that is that he said: (When it was the time of 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: O people, you had in divorce deliberation, and whoever hastens Allah's deliberation in divorce, we bind him with it)."
"4187","4474 - Ibn Abi 'Imran narrated that to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us. (H). 4475 - And 'Abd al-Hamid ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, the like of the hadith we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter, except that they did not mention Abu al-Sahba nor his asking Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah Almighty be pleased with them both, but mentioned the like of Ibn 'Abbas's answer, may Allah be pleased with them both, in that hadith, and mentioned after that of 'Umar's speech, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, what we mentioned before this hadith. So 'Umar, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, addressed thereby all people, and among them the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and may He be pleased with them, who had known what preceded of that, in that, in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and no denier of them denied it upon him, nor repelled it a repeller, so that was the greatest proof in abrogating what preceded of that. Because when the action of all the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is an action by which proof is obligatory, likewise also their consensus upon a statement is a consensus by which proof is obligatory. And as their consensus upon transmission is free of illusion and error, likewise their consensus upon opinion is free of illusion and error. And we have seen things that were in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon meanings, then his Companions, may Allah Almighty be pleased with them, after him made them upon contrary to those meanings, when they saw in it of what was hidden upon those after them, so that was abrogating proof of what preceded it. Of that, compiling the registers and prohibiting sale of mothers of children, and they were sold before that. And timing in the hadd of wine, and there was no timing in it before that. So when what they acted with of that, and we stood upon it, it is not permissible for us to oppose it to what we saw, of what preceded their doing it, likewise what they informed us of of the thrice divorce effected together, that it binds, it is not permissible for us to oppose it to other than it, of what was narrated that it was before it upon contrary to that. Then this Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah Almighty be pleased with them both, after that used to give fatwa to one who divorced his wife thrice together, that his divorce bound him, and forbade her to him."
"4188","4476 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Malik ibn al-Harith, he said: A man came to Ibn 'Abbas and said: My uncle divorced his wife thrice, so he said: Your uncle disobeyed Allah, so Allah completed it, and obeyed Satan, so He made no way out for him. So I said: What do you think of a man making her lawful to him? So he said: (Whoever deceives Allah, He deceives him)."
"4189","4477 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Muhammad ibn Iyas ibn al-Bukayr, he said: A man divorced his wife thrice before consummating with her, then it appeared to him to marry her, so he came seeking fatwa, so I went with him asking for him Abu Hurayrah and 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas about that. So they both said: We do not consider that you marry her, until she marries a husband other than you. So he said: My divorcing her was only one. So Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: You sent from your hand what bounty you had."
"4190","4478 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, that Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj informed him from Mu'awiyah ibn Abi 'Ayyash al-Ansari, that he was sitting with 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr and 'Asim ibn 'Umar, and Muhammad ibn Iyas ibn al-Bukayr came to them and said: A man of the desert people divorced his wife thrice before consummating with her, so what do you two think? So Ibn al-Zubayr said: This matter we have no statement in, so go to 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them, and ask them, then come to us and inform us. So he went and asked them, so Ibn 'Abbas said to Abu Hurayrah: Give him fatwa, O Abu Hurayrah, for a difficult matter has come to you, (that is: a hard problematic question). So Abu Hurayrah said: The one separates her, and the three forbid her, until she marries a husband other than him."
"4191","4479 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Muhammad ibn Iyas ibn al-Bukayr, that a man asked Ibn 'Abbas, Abu Hurayrah, and Ibn 'Amr about divorcing the virgin thrice while with him, and all of them said: She is forbidden to you."
"4192","4480 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn 'Abbas, that they both said regarding a man divorcing the virgin thrice: She is not lawful to him until she marries a husband other than him."
"4193","4481 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that a man asked Ibn 'Abbas, that a man divorced his wife a hundred. So he said: Three forbid her to him, and ninety-seven on his neck; he took Allah's verses in mockery."
"4194","4482 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from 'Abd al-A'la, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, the like of it."
"4195","4483 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, and Humayd al-A'raj, from Mujahid, that a man said to Ibn 'Abbas: A man divorcing his wife a hundred, so he said: You disobeyed your Lord and your wife is separated from you; you did not fear Allah so He make for you a way out; whoever fears Allah He makes for him a way out; Allah Almighty said: (O Prophet, when you divorce women, divorce them at the beginning of their waiting period). Then it has been narrated from other than him of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and may He be pleased with them what agrees with that also."
"4196","4484 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Sufyan and Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from 'Abdullah, that he said - regarding one who divorces his wife thrice before consummating with her - he said: She is not lawful to him until she marries a husband other than him."
"4197","4485 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, from 'Abdullah, that he was asked about a man divorcing his wife a hundred, he said: (Three separate her from you, and the rest is transgression)."
"4198","4486 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, from al-Nu'man ibn Abi 'Ayyash al-Ansari, from 'Ata ibn Yasar, that he said: A man came to 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr and asked him about a man divorcing his wife thrice before touching her. 'Ata said: So I said to him: The virgin's divorce is one? So 'Abdullah said: (You are only a storyteller; the one separates her, and the three forbid her until she marries a husband other than him)."
"4199","4487 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah and Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, they both said: Ibn al-Had narrated to us, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Ata ibn Yasar, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, he said: (The one separates her, and the three forbid her)."
"4200","4488 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id, he is Ibn Mansur, narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us: from Shaqiq, from Anas, he said: (She is not lawful to him until she marries a husband other than him). He said: And 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, when brought a man divorcing his wife thrice, would pain his back."
